WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.460
Andrew Huberman: [MUSIC PLAYING] Welcome to the Huberman Lab

00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:05.560
podcast, where we discuss science and
science-based tools for everyday life.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:13.129
I'm Andrew Huberman, and I'm a professor
of neurobiology and ophthalmology

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:14.750
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:17.229
Today, my guest is Marc Andreessen.

00:00:17.739 --> 00:00:21.970
Marc Andreessen is a software engineer
and an investor in technology companies.

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:25.210
He co-founded and developed
Mosaic, which was one of the

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:26.979
first widely used web browsers.

00:00:27.309 --> 00:00:30.820
He also co-founded and developed
Netscape, which was one of the

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.110
earliest widespread used web browsers.

00:00:33.349 --> 00:00:36.900
And he co-founded and is a general
partner at Andreessen Horowitz,

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:40.560
one of the most successful Silicon
Valley venture capital firms.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:44.629
All of that is to say that Mark
Andreessen is one of the most successful

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.830
innovators and investors ever.

00:00:47.429 --> 00:00:50.630
I was extremely excited to record this
episode with Marc for several reasons.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:53.149
First of all, he himself
is an incredible innovator.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:58.410
Second of all, he has an uncanny ability
to spot the innovators of the future.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:02.400
And third, Marc has shown over and
over again the ability to understand

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.919
how technologies not yet even
developed are going to impact the

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.820
way that humans interact at large.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:12.900
Our conversation starts off by discussing
what makes for an exceptional innovator,

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.350
as well as what sorts of environmental
conditions make for exceptional

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.580
innovation and creativity more generally.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.840
In that context, we talk about risk
taking, not just in terms of risk taking

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:27.260
in one's profession, but about how some
people, not all, but how some people who

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.550
are risk takers and innovators in the
context of their work also seem to take

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:35.940
a lot of risks in their personal life and
some of the consequences that can bring.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.289
Then we discuss some of the most
transformative technologies that are

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:44.050
now emerging, such as novel approaches
to developing clean energy, as well

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.360
as AI or artificial intelligence.

00:01:46.619 --> 00:01:51.060
With respect to AI, Marc shares
his views as to why AI is likely to

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.830
greatly improve human experience,
and we discuss the multiple roles

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:58.529
that AI is very likely to have in
all of our lives in the near future.

00:01:59.059 --> 00:02:03.339
Marc explains how not too long from now,
all of us are very likely to have AI

00:02:03.339 --> 00:02:07.990
assistants, for instance, assistants that
give us highly informed health advice,

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:10.030
highly informed psychological advice.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:14.590
Indeed, it is very likely that all of us
will soon have AI assistants that govern

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:16.980
most, if not all, of our daily decisions.

00:02:17.389 --> 00:02:21.440
And Marc explains how, if done
correctly, this can be a tremendously

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:23.209
positive addition to our life.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.570
In doing so, Marc provides a stark
argument for those that argue that AI

00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:30.710
is going to diminish human experience.

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:34.430
So if you're hearing about and or
concerned about the ways that AI is

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:38.250
likely to destroy us today, you are
going to hear about the many different

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:42.309
ways that AI technologies now in
development are likely to enhance

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:44.300
our human experience at every level.

00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:47.560
What you'll soon find is that while
today's discussion does center around

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:51.500
technology and technology development,
it is really a discussion about

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:53.410
human beings and human psychology.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:56.959
So whether you have an interest in
technology development and or AI,

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:01.020
I'm certain that you'll find today's
discussion to be an important and

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:04.839
highly lucid view into what will soon
be the future that we all live in.

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.609
Before we begin, I'd like to emphasize
that this podcast is separate from my

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:10.239
teaching and research roles at Stanford.

00:03:10.469 --> 00:03:13.390
It is, however, part of my desire
a nd effort to bring zero cost

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:15.699
to consumer information about
science and science-related

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.229
tools to the general public.

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:20.980
In keeping with that theme, I'd like to
thank the sponsors of today's podcast.

