00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.980
And now for my discussion
with Marc Andreessen.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:06.250
Marc, welcome.

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:07.140
Marc Andreessen: Hey, thank you.

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:09.620
Andrew Huberman: Delighted to
have you here and have so many

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:14.229
questions for you about innovation
AI, your view of the landscape

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:17.179
of tech, and humanity in general.

00:06:18.199 --> 00:06:21.450
I want to start off by talking
about innovation from three

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:22.340
different perspectives.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:27.520
There's the inner game, so to speak,
or the psychology of the innovator,

00:06:27.849 --> 00:06:32.729
or innovators, things like their
propensity for engaging in conflict

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:37.930
or not, their propensity for having a
dream or a vision, and in particular,

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:44.659
their innovation as it relates to some
psychological trait or expression.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:46.290
So we'll get to that in a moment.

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:50.420
The second component that I'm
curious about is the outer landscape

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:54.309
around innovators, who they place
themselves with, the sorts of

00:06:54.310 --> 00:06:58.050
choices that they make and also
the sorts of personal relationships

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.410
that they might have or not have.

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:04.179
And then the last component is this
notion of the larger landscape that

00:07:04.179 --> 00:07:05.390
they happen to find themselves in.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:06.480
What time in history?

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:08.960
What's the geography?

00:07:09.049 --> 00:07:11.800
Bay Area, New York, Dubai, etc.

00:07:12.449 --> 00:07:19.090
So to start off, is there a common
trait of innovators that you think

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:25.420
is absolutely essential as a seed to
creating things that are really impactful?

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:26.599
Marc Andreessen: Yeah.

00:07:26.599 --> 00:07:29.080
So I'm not a psychologist,
but I've picked up some of the

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:31.140
concepts and some of the terms.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:34.650
And so it was a great moment of delight
in my life when I learned about the Big

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:37.909
Five personality traits, because I was
like, aha, there's a way to actually

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:41.190
describe the answer to this question in
at least reasonably scientific terms.

00:07:42.099 --> 00:07:44.770
And so I think what you're looking
for, when you're talking about real

00:07:44.799 --> 00:07:47.440
innovators, like people who actually do
really creative breakthrough work, I think

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:48.282
you're talking about a couple of things.

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:52.570
So one is very high in what's called
trait openness, which is one of the

00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:55.990
Big Five, which is basically just
like, flat out open to new ideas.

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:00.409
And of course, the nature of trait
openness is trait openness means

00:08:00.409 --> 00:08:02.479
you're not just open to new ideas
in one category, you're open to

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:03.780
many different kinds of new ideas.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:06.760
And so we might talk about the
fact that a lot of innovators also

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:10.360
are very creative people in other
aspects of their lives, even outside

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:12.380
of their specific creative domain.

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:13.700
So that's important.

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:16.159
But of course, just being open is not
sufficient, because if you're just

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:18.799
open, you could just be curious and
explore and spend your entire life

00:08:18.799 --> 00:08:21.740
reading and doing, talking to people
and never actually create something.

00:08:21.759 --> 00:08:23.780
So you also need a couple of other things.

00:08:23.780 --> 00:08:25.830
You need a high level of
conscientiousness, which is

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:26.800
another one of the Big Five.

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:30.270
You need somebody who's really willing
to apply themselves, and in our world,

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:33.970
typically over a period of many years to
be able to accomplish something great.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:35.700
They typically work very hard.

00:08:36.549 --> 00:08:39.359
That often gets obscured because
the stories that end up getting told

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.300
about these people are, it's just like
this kid, and he just had this idea,

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:43.350
and it was like a stroke of genius.

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:46.200
And it was like a moment in time and
was just like, oh, he was so lucky.

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:48.980
And it's like, no, for most of
these people, it's years and

00:08:48.980 --> 00:08:50.830
years and years of applied effort.

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:54.689
And so you need somebody with an
extreme, basically, willingness to defer

00:08:54.690 --> 00:08:58.839
gratification and really apply themselves
to a specific thing for a long time.

00:08:58.839 --> 00:09:02.120
And of course, this is why there aren't
very many of these people, there aren't

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:05.735
many people who are high in openness and
high in conscientiousness because to a

00:09:05.735 --> 00:09:07.780
certain extent, they're opposed traits.

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:09.260
And so you need somebody
who has both of those.

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:13.109
Third is you need somebody
high in disagreeableness, which

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:14.579
is the third of the Big Five.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:19.690
So you need somebody who's just basically
ornery, because if they're not ornery,

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:22.340
then they'll be talked out of their
ideas by people who will be like, oh,

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:25.240
well, because the reaction, most people
have new ideas is, oh, that's dumb.

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:29.250
And so somebody who's too agreeable
will be easily dissuaded to not

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:31.560
pursue, not pulling the thread anymore.

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:33.120
So you need somebody highly disagreeable.

00:09:33.139 --> 00:09:35.560
Again, the nature of
disagreeableness is they tend to

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:37.300
be disagreeable about everything.

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:41.710
So they tend to be these very sort of
iconoclastic kind of renegade characters.

00:09:42.590 --> 00:09:44.790
And then there's just a table
stakes component, which is they

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:45.979
just also need to be high IQ.

00:09:46.379 --> 00:09:49.590
They just need to be really smart
because it's hard to innovate in

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:52.730
any category if you can't synthesize
large amounts of information quickly.

00:09:53.559 --> 00:09:58.660
And so those are four basically
high spikes, very rare traits that

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:00.750
basically have to come together.

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:04.920
You could probably also say they probably
at some point need to be relatively low

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:07.990
on neuroticism, which is another of the
Big Five, because if they're too neurotic,

00:10:08.139 --> 00:10:09.400
they probably can't handle the stress.

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:11.679
Right.
So it's kind of this dial in there.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:15.920
And then, of course, if you're into the
sort of science of the Big Five, basically

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:20.290
these are all people who are on the far
outlying kind of point on the normal

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:21.790
distribution across all these traits.

00:10:21.809 --> 00:10:25.340
And then that just gets you to, I
think, the sort of hardest topic of

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:28.579
all around this whole concept, which
there are very few of these people.

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:31.540
Andrew Huberman: Do you think
they're born with these traits?

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:34.020
Marc Andreessen: Yeah,
they're born with the traits.

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:36.780
And then, of course, the traits are
not genetics, are not destiny, and so

00:10:37.140 --> 00:10:40.360
the traits are not deterministic in
the sense of that just because they

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:43.410
have those personality traits doesn't
mean they're going to deliver great

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:47.300
creativity, but they need to have those
properties because otherwise they're just

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:49.540
not either going to be able to do the
work or they're not going to enjoy it.

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:50.330
Right.

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:53.550
I mean, look, a lot of these people
are highly capable, competent people.

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:57.580
It's very easy for them to get,
like, high paying jobs in traditional

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:01.870
institutions and get lots of traditional
awards and end up with big paychecks.

00:11:01.870 --> 00:11:06.319
And there's a lot of people at big
institutions that you and I know well,

00:11:06.319 --> 00:11:08.945
and I deal with many of these where
people get paid a lot of money and

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:11.630
they get a lot of respect and they go
for 20 years and it's great and they

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:13.690
never create anything new, right?

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:18.110
There's a lot of administrators, a
lot of them end up in administrative

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:20.319
jobs, and that's fine, that's good.

00:11:20.320 --> 00:11:22.400
The world needs that also, right?

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:25.440
The innovators can't run
everything because the rate

00:11:25.449 --> 00:11:26.310
of change would be too high.

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:27.920
Society, I think, probably
wouldn't be able to handle it.

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:30.750
So you need some people who are on the
other side who are going to kind of keep

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:32.170
the lights on and keep things running.

00:11:32.179 --> 00:11:35.970
But there is this decision that people
have to make, which is okay if I

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:39.340
have the sort of latent capability
to do this, is this actually what

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:40.760
I want to spend my life doing?

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:43.859
And do I want to go through the
stress and the pain and the trauma

00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:46.780
and anxiety and the risk of failure?

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:48.500
And so, do I really want to?

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:50.469
Once in a while you run into
somebody who's just like,

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:51.700
can't do it any other way.

00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:53.320
They just have to.

00:11:53.440 --> 00:11:54.253
Andrew Huberman: Who's an example of that?

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:57.620
Marc Andreessen: I mean, Elon's the
paramount example of our time, and

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:00.769
I bring him up in part because he's
such an obvious example, but in part

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:04.640
because he's talked about this in
interviews where he basically says,

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:06.060
he's like, I can't turn it off.

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:10.130
The ideas come, I have
to pursue them, right?

00:12:10.130 --> 00:12:12.030
It's why he's like running
five companies at the same time

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:14.320
and, like working on a sixth.

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:16.270
It's just like he can't turn it off.

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:20.160
Look, there's a lot of other people who
probably had the capability to do it,

00:12:20.160 --> 00:12:24.260
who ended up talking themselves into or
whatever events conspired to put them in

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:25.700
a position where they did something else.

00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:28.300
Obviously, there are people
who try to be creative, who

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:29.960
just don't have the capability.

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:33.579
And so, there's some venn diagram
there of determinism through traits,

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:37.710
but also choices in life, and then
also, of course, the situation in

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:41.810
which they're born, the context within
which they grow up, culture, what their

00:12:41.810 --> 00:12:43.770
parents expect of them, and so forth.

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:47.469
And so to kind of get all the way
through this, you have to thread all

00:12:47.469 --> 00:12:48.689
these needles kind of at the same time.

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:52.779
Andrew Huberman: Do you think there are
folks out there that meet these criteria

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:58.080
who are disagreeable, but that can feign
agreeableness, you know that can...?

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:01.660
[BOTH LAUGH] For those just listening,
Marc just raised his right hand.

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:07.520
In other words, they can sort of,
phrase that comes to mind maybe because

00:13:07.520 --> 00:13:11.689
I can relate to it a little bit, they
sneak up through the system, meaning

00:13:11.719 --> 00:13:14.780
they behave ethically as it relates
to the requirements of the system.

00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:17.680
They're not breaking laws or breaking
rules, in fact, quite the opposite,

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:20.450
they're paying attention to the
rules and following the rules until

00:13:20.450 --> 00:13:25.579
they get to a place where being
disagreeable feels less threatening

00:13:26.049 --> 00:13:27.609
to their overall sense of security.

00:13:28.959 --> 00:13:30.600
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, I mean, look,
the really highly competent people

00:13:30.609 --> 00:13:31.770
don't have to break laws, right?

00:13:33.260 --> 00:13:34.581
There was this myth that happened
around the movie The Godfather , and

00:13:34.581 --> 00:13:40.689
then there was this character, Meyer
Lansky, who's like, ran basically

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:42.510
the Mafia 50, 60, 70 years ago.

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:45.470
And there was this great line of like,
well, if Meyer Lansky had only applied

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:47.909
himself to running General Motors, he
would have been the best CEO of all time.

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:49.660
It's like, no, not really, right?

00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:52.930
The people who are great at
running the big companies, they

00:13:52.930 --> 00:13:54.020
don't have to be mob bosses.

00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:55.040
They don't have to break laws.

00:13:57.300 --> 00:14:00.160
They're smart and sophisticated enough
to be able to work inside the system.

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:01.980
They don't need to take the easy out.

00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:05.589
So, I don't think there's any
implication that they have to break laws.

00:14:05.590 --> 00:14:07.470
That said, they have
to break norms, right?

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:11.229
And specifically, this is probably
the thing that gets missed the most,

00:14:11.230 --> 00:14:15.060
because the process of innovating,
the process of creating something

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:20.559
new, once it works, the stories get
retconned, as they say in comic books.

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:23.519
So the stories get adapted to where
it's like it was inevitable all along.

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:25.620
Everybody always knew
that this was a good idea.

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:28.620
The person has won all these
awards, society embraced them.

00:14:29.030 --> 00:14:33.439
And invariably, if you were with them when
they were actually doing the work, or if

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:35.970
you actually get a couple of drinks into
them and talk about it, it'd be like,

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:37.240
no, that's not how it happened at all.

00:14:37.590 --> 00:14:41.069
They faced a wall of skepticism,
just like a wall of basically

00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:42.739
social, essentially denial.

00:14:42.750 --> 00:14:43.910
No, this is not going to work.

00:14:43.950 --> 00:14:45.320
No, I'm not going to join your lab.

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:46.890
No, I'm not going to come
work for your company.

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:48.939
No, I'm not going to
buy your product, right?

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:50.010
No, I'm not going to meet with you.

00:14:50.420 --> 00:14:54.209
And so they get just like
tremendous social resistance.

00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:58.269
They're not getting positive feedback
from their social network the way that

00:14:58.279 --> 00:15:00.559
more agreeable people need to have, right?

00:15:00.559 --> 00:15:03.079
And this is why agreeableness
is a problem for innovation.

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:05.080
If you're agreeable, you're going
to listen to the people around you.

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:08.690
They're going to tell you that new
ideas are stupid, end of story.

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:09.869
You're not going to proceed.

00:15:10.429 --> 00:15:13.224
And so I would put it more on like,
they need to be able to deal with, they

00:15:13.224 --> 00:15:16.969
need to be able to deal with social
discomfort to the level of ostracism,

00:15:17.590 --> 00:15:20.050
or at some point they're going to get
shaken out and they're just going to quit.

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:22.470
Andrew Huberman: Do you think that
people that meet these criteria

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:26.330
do best by banding with others
that meet these criteria early?

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:32.149
Or is it important that they form this
deep sense of self, like the ability

00:15:32.150 --> 00:15:36.710
to cry oneself to sleep at night or
lie in the fetal position, worrying

00:15:36.719 --> 00:15:39.660
that things aren't going to work
out and then still get up the next

00:15:39.660 --> 00:15:41.219
morning and get right back out there.

00:15:41.470 --> 00:15:41.940
Marc Andreessen: Right.

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:45.040
So, Sean Parker has the best
line, by the way, on this.

00:15:45.059 --> 00:15:48.650
He says being an entrepreneur or being
a creator is like getting punched

00:15:48.650 --> 00:15:49.990
in the face over and over again.

00:15:50.009 --> 00:15:52.070
He said, eventually you start to
like the taste of your own blood.

00:15:53.170 --> 00:15:55.940
And I love that line because it makes
everybody massively uncomfortable,

00:15:55.950 --> 00:15:59.689
but it gives you a sense of how
basically painful the process is.

00:15:59.689 --> 00:16:02.800
If you talk to any entrepreneur who's
been through it about that, they're like,

00:16:03.010 --> 00:16:04.619
oh, yeah, that's exactly what it's like.

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:07.950
So, there is a big
individual component to it.

00:16:08.299 --> 00:16:12.860
But look, it can be very lonely, and
especially very hard, I think, to do

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:15.860
this if nobody around you is trying
to do anything even remotely similar.

00:16:16.040 --> 00:16:18.219
And if you're getting just
universally negative responses,

00:16:18.230 --> 00:16:21.489
like very few people, I think very
few people have the ego strength to

00:16:21.490 --> 00:16:22.730
be able to survive that for years.

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:26.809
So I do think there's a huge advantage,
and this is why you do see clusters.

00:16:26.809 --> 00:16:28.130
There's a huge advantage to clustering.

00:16:30.109 --> 00:16:31.900
Throughout history, you've
had this clustering effect.

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:36.270
You had clustering of the great
artists and sculptors in, you had the

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:37.800
clustering of the philosophers of Greece.

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:39.970
You had the clustering of
tech people in Silicon Valley.

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:42.739
You have the clustering of
know, arts, movie, TV people

00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:44.679
in Los Angeles, and so forth.

00:16:44.679 --> 00:16:47.479
And so, know, there's
always a scene, right?

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:50.420
There's always, like a nexus
and a place where people come

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:52.630
together for these kinds of things.

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:56.110
So, generally speaking, if somebody
wants to work in tech, innovate in tech,

00:16:56.110 --> 00:16:59.010
they're going to be much better off being
around a lot of people who are trying to

00:16:59.020 --> 00:17:02.280
do that kind of thing than they are in
a place where nobody else is doing it.

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:06.469
Having said that, the clustering can
have downsides, it can have side effects.

00:17:06.469 --> 00:17:09.290
And you put any group of people
together, and you do start to get

00:17:09.290 --> 00:17:12.200
groupthink, even among people who
are individually very disagreeable.

00:17:12.980 --> 00:17:16.319
And so these same clusters where
you get these very idiosyncratic

00:17:16.339 --> 00:17:19.720
people, they do have fads and
trends just like every place else.

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:23.649
And so they get wrapped up
in their own social dynamics.

00:17:24.279 --> 00:17:27.099
The good news is the social dynamic in
those places is usually very forward

00:17:27.099 --> 00:17:31.850
looking, and so it's usually like, I don't
know, it's like a herd of iconoclasts

00:17:31.850 --> 00:17:32.669
looking for the next big thing.

00:17:33.849 --> 00:17:35.329
So iconoclasts, looking
for the next big thing.

00:17:35.330 --> 00:17:35.719
That's good.

00:17:35.719 --> 00:17:36.340
The herd part.

00:17:36.790 --> 00:17:38.589
That's what you've got to be careful of.

00:17:38.589 --> 00:17:40.429
So even when you're in one of
these environments, you have to

00:17:40.440 --> 00:17:42.560
be careful that you're not getting
sucked into the groupthink too much.

00:17:43.010 --> 00:17:45.230
Andrew Huberman: When you say groupthink,
do you mean excessive friction?

00:17:45.390 --> 00:17:47.950
Do you do pressure testing each
other's ideas to the point where

00:17:48.110 --> 00:17:49.230
things just don't move forward?

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:52.820
Or are you talking about groupthink,
where people start to form a consensus?

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:57.070
Or the self belief that, gosh, we are
so strong because we are so different?

00:17:59.609 --> 00:18:00.330
Can we better define groupthink?

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:03.850
Marc Andreessen: It's actually less
either one of those things both happen.

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:04.490
Those are good.

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:05.720
Those are good.

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:10.189
The part of groupthink I'm talking about
is just like, we all basically zero in, we

00:18:10.190 --> 00:18:11.720
just end up zeroing in on the same ideas.

00:18:11.889 --> 00:18:12.389
Right.

00:18:12.830 --> 00:18:14.070
In Hollywood, there's this classic thing.

00:18:15.219 --> 00:18:17.500
There are years where all of a sudden
there's, like, a lot of volcano movies.

00:18:18.559 --> 00:18:20.260
It's like, why are there
all these volcano movies?

00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:22.680
And it's just like, there was just
something in the gestalt, right?

00:18:22.680 --> 00:18:23.689
There was just something in the air.

00:18:24.260 --> 00:18:25.760
Look, Silicon Valley has this.

00:18:25.930 --> 00:18:27.820
There are moments in time
where you'll have these.

00:18:27.820 --> 00:18:29.589
It's like the old thing.

00:18:29.719 --> 00:18:31.060
What's the difference
between a fad and a trend?

00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:33.179
Fad is the trend that doesn't last.

00:18:33.179 --> 00:18:33.679
Right.

00:18:33.799 --> 00:18:38.280
And so Silicon Valley is subject to both
fads and trends, just like any place.

00:18:39.220 --> 00:18:41.560
In other words, you take smart,
disagreeable people, you cluster them

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:43.429
together, they will act like a herd.

00:18:43.970 --> 00:18:46.970
They will end up thinking the same
things unless they try very hard not to.

00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:51.210
Andrew Huberman: You've talked about these
personality traits of great innovators

00:18:51.290 --> 00:18:54.429
before, and we're talking about them now.

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:59.090
You invest in innovators, you try
and identify them, and you are one.

00:18:59.090 --> 00:19:01.670
So you can recognize these traits here.

00:19:01.670 --> 00:19:03.149
I'm making the presumption
that you have these traits.

00:19:03.150 --> 00:19:03.930
Indeed you do.

00:19:04.780 --> 00:19:06.279
We'll just get that out of the way.

00:19:08.099 --> 00:19:13.500
Have you observed people trying to
feign these traits, and are there any

00:19:13.500 --> 00:19:20.730
specific questions or behaviors that are
a giveaway that they're pretending to

00:19:20.730 --> 00:19:25.620
be the young Steve Jobs or that they're
pretending to be the young Henry Ford?

00:19:26.719 --> 00:19:31.560
Pick your list of other names that qualify
as authentic, legitimate innovators.

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:34.760
We won't name names of people
who have tried to disguise

00:19:34.770 --> 00:19:36.030
themselves as true innovators.

00:19:36.030 --> 00:19:40.349
But what are some of the litmus tests?

00:19:40.349 --> 00:19:43.419
And I realize here that we don't
want you to give these away to the

00:19:43.420 --> 00:19:45.379
point where they lose their potency.

00:19:45.380 --> 00:19:46.840
But if you could share a few of those.

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:49.339
Marc Andreessen: Good, we're
actually a pretty open book on this.

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:53.896
First of all, yes, so there are people who
definitely try to come in and basically

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:56.790
present as being something that they're
not, and they've read all the books.

00:19:56.790 --> 00:19:57.879
They will have listened to this interview.

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:01.919
They study everything and they
construct a facade, and they come in

00:20:01.920 --> 00:20:02.979
and present as something they're not.

00:20:02.979 --> 00:20:06.660
I would say the amount of that varies
exactly, correlated to the NASDAQ.

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:13.320
And so when stock prices are super
low, you actually get the opposite.

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:15.460
When stock prices are super
low, people get too demoralized.

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:17.409
And people who should be doing
it basically give up because they

00:20:17.409 --> 00:20:20.379
just think that the industry is
over, the trend is over, whatever.

00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:21.050
It's all hopeless.

00:20:21.370 --> 00:20:22.410
And so you get this flushing thing.

00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:25.509
So nobody ever shows up at a stock
market low and says, like, I'm the

00:20:26.310 --> 00:20:32.129
new next big thing and doesn't really
want to do it because there are higher

00:20:32.130 --> 00:20:35.230
status, the kinds of people who do the
thing that you're talking about, they're

00:20:35.230 --> 00:20:36.790
fundamentally oriented for social status.

00:20:36.790 --> 00:20:41.310
They're trying to get the social
status without actually the substance.

00:20:41.310 --> 00:20:43.620
And there are always other places
to go to get social status.

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:49.570
So after 2000, the joke was,
when I got to Silicon Valley in

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:51.559
'93, '94, the Valley was dead.

00:20:51.610 --> 00:20:52.609
We can talk about that.

00:20:52.630 --> 00:20:55.930
By '98, it was roaring, and you had
a lot of these people showing up, who

00:20:55.930 --> 00:20:59.280
were, you basically had a lot of people
showing up with these kind of stories.

00:20:59.830 --> 00:21:00.689
2000, the market crashed.

00:21:00.690 --> 00:21:06.110
By 2001, the joke was that there
were these terms, B to C and B to B.

00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:10.280
And in 1998, they meant B to C meant
business to consumer and B to B

00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:12.639
meant business to business, which
is two different kinds of business

00:21:12.639 --> 00:21:13.669
models for Internet companies.

00:21:13.670 --> 00:21:19.070
By 2001, B to B meant back to banking
and B to C meant back to consulting,

00:21:20.180 --> 00:21:23.620
which is the high status people who, the
people oriented to status, who showed up

00:21:23.620 --> 00:21:25.489
to be in tech were like, yeah, screw it.

00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:26.300
This is over.

00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:27.100
Stick a fork in it.

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:29.580
I'm going to go back to Goldman
Sachs or go back to McKinsey,

00:21:30.360 --> 00:21:31.959
where I can be high status.

00:21:31.959 --> 00:21:35.730
And so you get this flushing kind of
effect that happens in a downturn.

00:21:36.620 --> 00:21:42.710
That said, in a big upswing, yeah, you
get a lot of people showing up with a

00:21:42.710 --> 00:21:46.050
lot of kind of, let's say, public persona
without the substance to back it up.

00:21:46.950 --> 00:21:50.870
So the way we stress that you can actually
say exactly how we test for this, because

00:21:52.340 --> 00:21:55.220
the test exactly addresses the issue
in a way that is impossible to fake.

00:21:56.110 --> 00:21:59.530
And it's actually the same way homicide
detectives try to find out if you've

00:22:00.660 --> 00:22:02.310
actually, like, if you're innocent
or whether you've killed somebody.

00:22:02.310 --> 00:22:08.519
It's the same tactic, which is, you ask
increasingly detailed questions, right?

00:22:08.519 --> 00:22:12.220
And so the way the homicide cop does
this is, what were you doing last night?

00:22:12.520 --> 00:22:13.370
Oh, I was at a movie.

00:22:13.790 --> 00:22:14.520
Which movie?

00:22:16.150 --> 00:22:16.820
Which theater?

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:18.610
Okay, which seat did you sit in?

00:22:19.300 --> 00:22:20.930
Okay, what was the end of the movie?

00:22:22.010 --> 00:22:25.500
And you ask increasingly detailed
questions and people have trouble.

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:28.279
At some point, people have trouble
making up and things just fuzz

00:22:28.279 --> 00:22:29.580
into just kind of obvious bullshit.

00:22:29.600 --> 00:22:32.350
And basically fake founders
basically have the same problem.

00:22:33.340 --> 00:22:35.990
They're able to relay a conceptual
theory of what they're doing that they've

00:22:35.990 --> 00:22:39.649
kind of engineered, but as they get
into the details, it just fuzzes out.

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:44.169
Whereas the true people that you want
to back that can do it, basically what

00:22:44.170 --> 00:22:47.749
you find is they've spent five or ten
or 20 years obsessing on the details

00:22:47.789 --> 00:22:49.070
of whatever it is they're about to do.

00:22:49.070 --> 00:22:51.390
And they're so deep in the
details that they know so much

00:22:51.390 --> 00:22:52.500
more about it than you ever will.

00:22:53.030 --> 00:22:56.570
And in fact, the best possible
reaction is when they get mad, which

00:22:56.570 --> 00:22:57.809
is also what the homicide cops say.

00:22:58.529 --> 00:23:02.180
What you actually want is you want the
emotional response of like, I can't

00:23:02.180 --> 00:23:05.589
believe that you're asking me questions
this detailed and specific and picky

00:23:06.209 --> 00:23:08.930
and they kind of figure out what
you're doing and then they get upset.

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:11.590
That's good, that's perfect, right?

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:15.030
But then they have to have proven
themselves in the sense of,

00:23:15.160 --> 00:23:17.439
they have to be able to answer
the questions in great detail.

00:23:18.400 --> 00:23:20.570
Andrew Huberman: Do you think that people
that are able to answer those questions

00:23:20.570 --> 00:23:24.760
in great detail have actually taken the
time to systematically think through the

00:23:24.770 --> 00:23:29.616
if-ands of all the possible implications
of what they're going to do and they

00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:34.079
have a specific vision in mind of how
things need to turn out or will turn out?

00:23:34.760 --> 00:23:39.370
Or do you think that they have
a vision and it's a no matter

00:23:39.370 --> 00:23:42.830
what, it will work out because the
world will sort of bend around it?

00:23:42.830 --> 00:23:45.270
I mean, in other words, do you think
that they place their vision in

00:23:45.270 --> 00:23:49.379
context or they simply have a vision
and they have that tunnel vision of

00:23:49.379 --> 00:23:51.759
that thing and that's going to be it?

00:23:51.760 --> 00:23:54.310
Let's use you for an
example with Netscape.

00:23:55.290 --> 00:23:56.759
That's how I first came to know your name.

00:23:58.330 --> 00:24:02.750
When you were conceiving Netscape,
did you think, okay, there's this

00:24:03.730 --> 00:24:07.840
search engine and this browser and
it's going to be this thing that

00:24:07.840 --> 00:24:13.350
looks this way and works this way and
feels this way, did you think that?

00:24:13.580 --> 00:24:17.560
And also think about that there was
going to be a gallery of other search

00:24:17.560 --> 00:24:20.560
engines and it would fit into that
landscape of other search engines?

00:24:20.560 --> 00:24:23.460
Or were you just projecting your
vision of this thing as this

00:24:23.460 --> 00:24:27.340
unique and special brainchild?

00:24:27.410 --> 00:24:29.060
Marc Andreessen: Let me give the
general answer, and then we can

00:24:29.060 --> 00:24:30.069
talk about the specific example.

00:24:30.219 --> 00:24:31.180
So the general answer is what?

00:24:31.760 --> 00:24:34.840
Entrepreneurship, creativity,
innovation is what economists call

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:36.169
decision making under uncertainty.

00:24:37.439 --> 00:24:38.870
In both parts, those are
important decision making.

00:24:38.870 --> 00:24:40.279
Like, you're going to make a ton
of decisions because you have to

00:24:40.350 --> 00:24:41.600
decide what to do, what not to do.

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:44.980
And then uncertainty, which is like,
the world's a complicated place.

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:48.040
And in mathematical terms, the
world is a complex adaptive

00:24:48.040 --> 00:24:49.240
system with feedback loops.

00:24:49.270 --> 00:24:56.420
And Isaac Asimov wrote in his
novels, he wrote about this field

00:24:56.420 --> 00:24:58.810
called psychohistory, which is
the idea that there's like a

00:24:58.810 --> 00:25:01.300
supercomputer that can predict the
future of human affairs, right?

00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:02.610
And it's like, we don't have that.

00:25:03.770 --> 00:25:04.620
[LAUGHS] Not yet.

00:25:04.620 --> 00:25:05.110
Andrew Huberman: [LAUGHS] Not yet.

00:25:05.110 --> 00:25:06.350
We'll get to that later.

00:25:06.759 --> 00:25:07.789
Marc Andreessen: We certainly
don't have that yet.

00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:12.269
And so you're just dealing, you
know, military commanders call

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:13.430
this the fog of war, right?

00:25:13.770 --> 00:25:15.570
You're just dealing with a
situation where the number of

00:25:15.580 --> 00:25:17.100
variables are just off the charts.

00:25:17.460 --> 00:25:20.840
It's all these other people who are
inherently unpredictable, making all

00:25:20.840 --> 00:25:22.240
these decisions in different directions.

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:24.450
And then the whole system is
combinatorial, which is these

00:25:24.450 --> 00:25:26.430
people are colliding with each
other, influencing their decisions.

00:25:26.910 --> 00:25:30.240
And so, I mean, look, the most
straightforward kind of way to

00:25:30.240 --> 00:25:32.440
think about this is, it's amazing.

00:25:32.450 --> 00:25:34.370
Like, anybody who believes in
economic central planning, it always

00:25:34.370 --> 00:25:36.739
blows my mind because it's just
like, try opening a restaurant.

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:40.800
Try just opening a restaurant
on the corner down here.

00:25:40.849 --> 00:25:43.450
And like 50/50 odds, the
restaurant is going to work.

00:25:43.450 --> 00:25:46.409
And all you have to do to run a
restaurant is have a thing and serve food.

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:48.650
And it's like most
restaurants fail, right?

00:25:49.410 --> 00:25:50.700
People who run restaurants
are pretty smart.

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:53.560
They usually think about these things
very hard, and they all want to

00:25:53.560 --> 00:25:55.439
succeed, and it's hard to do that.

00:25:55.439 --> 00:25:59.599
And so to start a tech company or to
start an artistic movement or to fight

00:25:59.599 --> 00:26:04.070
a war, you're just going into this,
basically conceptual battleground or

00:26:04.070 --> 00:26:07.790
in military terms, real battleground,
where there's just like incredible levels

00:26:07.800 --> 00:26:12.560
of complexity, branching future paths,
and so there's nothing predictable.

00:26:12.560 --> 00:26:18.020
And so what we look for is basically
the really good innovators.

00:26:18.020 --> 00:26:21.019
They've got a drive to basically be able
to cope with that and deal with that.

00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:22.709
And they basically do that in two steps.

00:26:22.709 --> 00:26:26.199
So one is they try to pre-plan as
much as they possibly can and we

00:26:26.200 --> 00:26:28.850
call that the process of navigating
the, what we call the idea maze.

00:26:29.190 --> 00:26:32.340
And so the idea maze basically is, I've
got this general idea, and it might be

00:26:32.340 --> 00:26:35.380
the Internet is going to work or search
or whatever, and then it's like, okay, in

00:26:35.380 --> 00:26:38.860
their head, they have thought through of
like, okay, if I do it this way, that way,

00:26:38.860 --> 00:26:40.220
this third way, here's what will happen.

00:26:40.230 --> 00:26:42.310
Then I have to do that, then I
have to do this, then I have to

00:26:42.310 --> 00:26:43.320
bring in somebody to do that.

00:26:43.330 --> 00:26:44.770
Here's the technical
challenge I'm going to hit.

00:26:45.170 --> 00:26:49.020
And they got in their heads as
best anybody could, they've got as

00:26:49.020 --> 00:26:52.160
complete a sort of a map of possible
futures as they could possibly have.

00:26:52.160 --> 00:26:55.089
And this is where I say, when you ask them
increasingly detailed questions, that's

00:26:55.089 --> 00:26:58.919
what you're trying to kind of get them to
kind of chart out, is, okay, how far ahead

00:26:58.930 --> 00:27:02.170
have you thought, and how much are you
anticipating all of the different twists

00:27:02.170 --> 00:27:03.220
and turns that this is going to take?

00:27:03.910 --> 00:27:05.970
Okay, so then they start on day
one, and then, of course, what

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:09.350
happens is now they're in it, now
they're in the fog of war, right?

00:27:10.050 --> 00:27:10.349
They're in future uncertainty.

00:27:10.349 --> 00:27:13.630
And now that idea maze is maybe not
helpful practically, but now they're

00:27:13.630 --> 00:27:16.230
going to be basically constructing
it on the fly, day by day, as they

00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:19.030
learn and discover new things and
as the world changes around them.

00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:21.820
And of course, it's a feedback loop,
because if their thing starts to

00:27:21.820 --> 00:27:22.910
work, it's going to change the world.

00:27:22.910 --> 00:27:24.850
And then the fact the world
is changing is going to cause

00:27:25.060 --> 00:27:26.850
their plan to change as well.

00:27:27.880 --> 00:27:32.099
And so, yeah, the great ones,
basically, the great ones

00:27:32.099 --> 00:27:33.269
course correct every single day.

00:27:33.880 --> 00:27:35.169
They take stock of what they've learned.

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:37.409
They modify the plan.

00:27:38.130 --> 00:27:40.450
The great ones tend to think
in terms of hypotheses, right?

00:27:41.310 --> 00:27:43.290
Like a scientific sort of mentality,
which is they tend to think,

00:27:43.290 --> 00:27:44.590
okay, I'm going to try this.

00:27:45.679 --> 00:27:48.060
I'm going to go into the world, I'm going
to announce that I'm doing this for sure.

00:27:49.040 --> 00:27:50.369
I'm going to say, this is my plan.

00:27:50.369 --> 00:27:52.339
I'm going to tell all my employees
that, and I'm going to tell all my

00:27:52.350 --> 00:27:54.080
investors that, and I'm going to put
a stake in there, and it's my plan,

00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:57.310
and then I'm going to try it, and even
though I sound like I have complete

00:27:57.310 --> 00:28:00.140
certainty, I know that I need to test
to find out whether it's going to work.

00:28:00.410 --> 00:28:03.050
And if it's not, then I have to go
back to all those same people and

00:28:03.050 --> 00:28:05.290
have to say, well, actually, we're
not going left, we're going right.

00:28:05.780 --> 00:28:10.260
And they have to run that loop thousands
of times to get through the other side.

00:28:10.260 --> 00:28:14.100
And this led to the creation of this great
term pivot, which has been very helpful

00:28:14.100 --> 00:28:17.380
in our industry because the word, when
I was young, the word we used was fuck

00:28:17.380 --> 00:28:21.790
up, and pivot sounds like so much better,
sounds like so much more professional.

00:28:22.260 --> 00:28:23.689
But, yeah, you make mistakes.

00:28:23.700 --> 00:28:26.439
It's just too complicated to understand.

00:28:26.450 --> 00:28:28.720
You course correct,
you adjust, you evolve.

00:28:29.070 --> 00:28:32.330
Often these things, at least in business,
the businesses that end up working

00:28:32.330 --> 00:28:36.270
really well tend to be different than
the original plan, but that's part of

00:28:36.270 --> 00:28:39.649
the process of a really smart founder
basically working their way through

00:28:39.660 --> 00:28:42.310
reality as they're executing their plan.

00:28:42.849 --> 00:28:44.879
Andrew Huberman: The way you're
describing this has parallels to

00:28:44.880 --> 00:28:49.079
a lot of models in biology and the
practice of science, random walks,

00:28:49.110 --> 00:28:52.360
but that aren't truly random,
pseudo-random walks in biology, etc.

00:28:52.820 --> 00:28:56.350
But one thing that is becoming
clear from the way you're describing

00:28:56.350 --> 00:29:01.150
this is that I could imagine
a great risk to early success.

00:29:01.580 --> 00:29:04.590
So, for instance, somebody develops
a product, people are excited by it,

00:29:05.400 --> 00:29:08.820
they start to implement that product,
but then the landscape changes, and

00:29:08.880 --> 00:29:13.230
they don't learn how to pivot to
use the less profane version of it.

00:29:13.850 --> 00:29:15.090
They don't learn how to do that.

00:29:15.340 --> 00:29:19.480
In other words, and I think of everything
these days, or most everything, in

00:29:19.480 --> 00:29:23.320
terms of reward schedules and dopamine
reward schedules, because that is

00:29:23.320 --> 00:29:25.050
the universal currency of reward.

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:29.680
And so when you talk about the Sean
Parker quote of learning to enjoy

00:29:29.690 --> 00:29:33.520
the taste of one's own blood, that
is very different than learning to

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:35.609
enjoy the taste of success, right?

00:29:35.620 --> 00:29:39.780
It's about internalizing success
as a process of being self

00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:43.140
determined and less agreeable, etc.

00:29:43.150 --> 00:29:47.980
In other words, building up of those five
traits becomes the source of dopamine,

00:29:48.150 --> 00:29:50.220
perhaps in a way that's highly adaptive.

00:29:50.220 --> 00:29:53.410
So on the outside, we just see the
product, the end product, the iPhone,

00:29:53.420 --> 00:29:55.489
the MacBook, the Netscape, etc.

00:29:55.830 --> 00:30:00.100
But I have to presume, and I'm not
a psychologist, but I have done

00:30:00.250 --> 00:30:04.220
neurophysiology and I've studied the
dopamine system enough to know that

00:30:04.510 --> 00:30:08.459
what's being rewarded in the context
of what you're describing sounds

00:30:08.460 --> 00:30:12.629
to be a reinforcement of those five
traits, rather than, oh, it's going

00:30:12.630 --> 00:30:15.379
to be this particular product, or the
company is going to look this way, or

00:30:15.380 --> 00:30:16.790
the logo is going to be this or that.

00:30:16.969 --> 00:30:22.730
That all seems like the peripheral
to what's really going on, that great

00:30:22.730 --> 00:30:26.860
innovators are really in the process
of establishing neural circuitry

00:30:27.010 --> 00:30:31.800
that is all about reinforcing
the me and the process of being.

00:30:33.460 --> 00:30:36.719
Marc Andreessen: So this is like
extrinsic versus intrinsic motivation.

00:30:36.740 --> 00:30:39.140
So, the Steve Jobs kind of
Zen version of this, right?

00:30:39.140 --> 00:30:41.630
Or the sort of hippie version of
this was the journey is the reward.

00:30:43.500 --> 00:30:44.460
He always told his employees that.

00:30:44.460 --> 00:30:47.220
It's like, look, everybody thinks in
terms of these big public markers,

00:30:47.220 --> 00:30:49.719
like the stock price or the IPO
or the product launch or whatever.

00:30:49.719 --> 00:30:53.480
He's like, no, it's actually
the process itself is the point.

00:30:53.910 --> 00:30:57.660
Right to your point, if you have that
mentality, then that's an intrinsic

00:30:57.660 --> 00:30:59.650
motivation, not an extrinsic motivation.

00:30:59.650 --> 00:31:02.130
And so that's the kind of
intrinsic motivation that can

00:31:02.130 --> 00:31:03.240
keep you going for a long time.

00:31:03.620 --> 00:31:05.889
Another way to think about it is
competing against yourself, right?

00:31:05.889 --> 00:31:08.199
It's like, can I get better at doing this?

00:31:08.809 --> 00:31:10.350
And can I prove to myself
that I can get better?

00:31:11.350 --> 00:31:13.970
There's also a big social component
to this, and this is one of the

00:31:13.970 --> 00:31:17.140
reasons why Silicon Valley punches
so far above its weight as a place.

00:31:17.580 --> 00:31:20.230
There's a psychological component
which also goes to the comparison set.

00:31:21.139 --> 00:31:25.090
So a phenomenon that we've observed
over time is the leading tech company

00:31:25.090 --> 00:31:29.709
in any city will aspire to be as large
as the previous leading tech company in

00:31:29.709 --> 00:31:32.850
that city, but often not larger, right?

00:31:32.870 --> 00:31:35.100
Because they have a model of success.

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:37.780
And as long as they beat that level
of success, they've kind of checked

00:31:37.780 --> 00:31:40.689
the box like they've made it.

00:31:40.710 --> 00:31:43.060
But then, in contrast, you're in
Silicon Valley, and you look around

00:31:43.060 --> 00:31:47.000
and it's just like Facebook and Cisco
and Oracle and Hewlett Packard and--

00:31:47.020 --> 00:31:47.780
Andrew Huberman: --Gladiators--

00:31:47.980 --> 00:31:48.270
Marc Andreessen: --Yeah.

00:31:48.270 --> 00:31:51.340
And you're just, like,
looking at these giants.

00:31:52.090 --> 00:31:54.730
Many of them are still, Mark Zuckerberg,
still going to work every day.

00:31:56.610 --> 00:31:59.360
And so these people are, like,
the role models are, like, alive.

00:32:00.300 --> 00:32:04.109
They're, like, right there, and it's so
clear how much better they are and how

00:32:04.109 --> 00:32:05.420
much bigger their accomplishments are.

00:32:05.420 --> 00:32:08.770
And so what we find is young
founders in that environment

00:32:08.780 --> 00:32:10.540
have much greater aspirations.

00:32:11.190 --> 00:32:14.780
Because, again, at that point, maybe
it's the social status, maybe there's

00:32:15.030 --> 00:32:19.129
an extrinsic component to that, or
maybe it helps calibrate that internal

00:32:19.130 --> 00:32:22.620
system to basically say, actually, no,
the opportunity here is not to build

00:32:23.379 --> 00:32:26.360
what you may call a local maximum
form of success, but let's build to a

00:32:26.360 --> 00:32:29.560
global maximum form of success, which
is something as big as we possibly can.

00:32:30.470 --> 00:32:33.010
Ultimately, the great ones are
probably driven more internally than

00:32:33.010 --> 00:32:34.690
externally when it comes down to it.

00:32:34.690 --> 00:32:38.809
And that is where you get this phenomenon
where you get people who are extremely

00:32:38.809 --> 00:32:41.099
successful and extremely wealthy
who very easily could punch out and

00:32:41.109 --> 00:32:44.429
move to Fiji and just call it, and
they're still working 16 hour days.

00:32:45.969 --> 00:32:48.900
Obviously something explains that that
has nothing to do with external rewards,

00:32:49.050 --> 00:32:50.900
and I think it's an internal thing.

