00:34:21.090 --> 00:34:23.429
mix because it was written some time ago.

00:34:24.790 --> 00:34:29.060
In any event, I want to return to
that, but I want to focus on an aspect

00:34:29.109 --> 00:34:33.980
of this intrinsic versus extrinsic
motivators in terms of something

00:34:33.980 --> 00:34:38.159
that's a bit more cryptic, which
is one's personal relationships.

00:34:38.980 --> 00:34:43.710
If I think about the catalog of innovators
in Silicon Valley, some of them, like

00:34:43.710 --> 00:34:47.260
Steve Jobs, had complicated personal
lives, romantic personal lives early

00:34:47.260 --> 00:34:48.949
on, and it sounds like he worked it out.

00:34:48.949 --> 00:34:49.449
I don't know.

00:34:50.159 --> 00:34:51.830
I wasn't their couple's therapist.

00:34:51.830 --> 00:34:56.520
But when he died, he was in a
marriage that for all the world

00:34:56.520 --> 00:34:57.470
seemed like a happy marriage.

00:34:58.940 --> 00:35:03.089
You also have examples of innovators
who have had many partners, many

00:35:03.099 --> 00:35:04.209
children with other partners.

00:35:04.219 --> 00:35:05.279
Elon comes to mind.

00:35:05.570 --> 00:35:09.250
I don't think I'm disclosing
anything that isn't already obvious.

00:35:10.450 --> 00:35:13.060
Those could have been happy
relationships and just had many of them.

00:35:13.500 --> 00:35:17.249
But the reason I'm asking this is you
can imagine that for the innovator,

00:35:17.809 --> 00:35:22.909
the person with these traits, who's
trying to build up this thing, whatever

00:35:22.910 --> 00:35:30.670
it is, that having someone, or several
people in some cases, who just truly

00:35:30.670 --> 00:35:34.640
believe in you when the rest of the
world may not believe in you yet or

00:35:34.640 --> 00:35:36.790
at all, could be immensely powerful.

00:35:36.790 --> 00:35:41.240
And we have examples from
cults that embody this.

00:35:41.240 --> 00:35:42.560
We have examples from politics.

00:35:42.560 --> 00:35:45.700
We have examples from tech
innovation and science.

00:35:46.420 --> 00:35:49.379
And I've always been fascinated by
this because I feel like it's the more

00:35:49.380 --> 00:35:54.620
cryptic and yet very potent form of
allowing someone to build themselves up.

00:35:54.960 --> 00:35:58.919
It's a combination of inner
psychology and extrinsic motivation.

00:35:58.920 --> 00:36:02.289
Because obviously, if that person
were to die or leave them or cheat

00:36:02.410 --> 00:36:06.860
on them or pair up with some other
innovator, which we've seen several

00:36:06.860 --> 00:36:10.260
times recently and in the past, it
can be devastating to that person.

00:36:10.260 --> 00:36:14.179
But what are your thoughts on the
role of personal, and in particular,

00:36:14.179 --> 00:36:19.469
romantic relationship as it relates
to people having an idea and their

00:36:19.470 --> 00:36:22.690
feeling that they can really bring
that idea to fruition in the world?

00:36:22.980 --> 00:36:24.470
Marc Andreessen: So it's a real mixed bag.

00:36:24.510 --> 00:36:26.819
You have lots of examples
