00:36:42.150 --> 00:36:44.600
And maybe you just need to find the
right person who compliments that

00:36:44.600 --> 00:36:47.420
and is willing to, there's a lot of
relationships where it's always this

00:36:47.420 --> 00:36:48.450
question about relationships, right?

00:36:48.450 --> 00:36:51.319
Which is, do you want to have the
same personality growth profile, the

00:36:51.320 --> 00:36:53.409
same behavioral traits, basically,
as your partner, or do you actually

00:36:53.410 --> 00:36:56.150
want to have, is it an opposite thing?

00:36:57.230 --> 00:36:57.903
I'm sure you've seen this.

00:36:57.903 --> 00:37:00.120
There are relationships where you'll
have somebody who's highly disagreeable,

00:37:00.120 --> 00:37:02.430
who's paired with somebody who's highly
agreeable, and it actually works out great

00:37:03.150 --> 00:37:05.620
because one person just gets to be on
their soapbox all the time, and the other

00:37:05.620 --> 00:37:08.720
person is just like, okay, it's fine.

00:37:08.720 --> 00:37:09.150
Right?
It's fine.

00:37:09.150 --> 00:37:09.650
It's good.

00:37:10.570 --> 00:37:13.980
You put two disagreeable people
together, maybe sparks fly and they

00:37:13.980 --> 00:37:16.190
have great conversations all the time,
and maybe they come to hate each other.

00:37:18.050 --> 00:37:20.330
Anyway, so these people, if you're
going to be with one of these

00:37:20.330 --> 00:37:22.279
people, you're fishing out of
the disagreeable end of the pond.

00:37:22.650 --> 00:37:25.940
And again, when I say disagreeable, I
don't mean these are normal distributions.

00:37:25.940 --> 00:37:28.809
I don't mean, like 60%
disagreeable or 80% disagreeable.

00:37:28.819 --> 00:37:31.799
The people we're talking
about are 99.99% disagreeable.

00:37:32.000 --> 00:37:33.380
So these are ordinary people.

00:37:34.600 --> 00:37:35.630
So part of it's that.

00:37:36.120 --> 00:37:37.870
And then, of course, they have
the other personality traits.

00:37:38.230 --> 00:37:39.510
They're super conscientious.

00:37:39.510 --> 00:37:40.360
They're super driven.

00:37:40.389 --> 00:37:41.960
As a consequence, they
tend to work really hard.

00:37:41.969 --> 00:37:44.959
They tend to not have a lot of time
for family vacations or other things.

00:37:46.049 --> 00:37:47.799
Then they don't enjoy them if
they're forced to go on them.

00:37:47.799 --> 00:37:50.480
And so, again, that kind of
thing can fray at a relationship.

00:37:50.480 --> 00:37:53.730
So there's a fair amount
in there that's loaded.

00:37:53.870 --> 00:37:55.800
Like, somebody who's going to
partner with one of these people

00:37:55.800 --> 00:37:57.310
needs to be signed up for the ride.

00:37:57.810 --> 00:37:58.930
And that's a hard thing.

00:37:58.930 --> 00:38:00.410
That's a hard thing to do.

00:38:00.730 --> 00:38:03.870
Or you need a true partnership of two
of these, which is also hard to do.

00:38:04.270 --> 00:38:05.130
So I think that's part of it.

00:38:06.200 --> 00:38:09.040
And then, look, I think a big part of
it is people achieve a certain level of

00:38:09.040 --> 00:38:14.870
success, and either in their own minds
or publicly, and then they start to be

00:38:14.870 --> 00:38:16.080
able to get away with things, right?

00:38:16.500 --> 00:38:17.380
And they start to be able to.

00:38:17.380 --> 00:38:20.510
It's like, well, okay, now we're rich
and successful and famous, and now I

00:38:20.660 --> 00:38:22.480
deserve, and this is where you get into...

00:38:22.770 --> 00:38:24.689
I view this now in the
realm of personal choice.

00:38:25.370 --> 00:38:27.840
You get into this thing where people
start to think that they deserve things,

00:38:27.840 --> 00:38:32.100
and so they start to behave in very
bad ways, and then they blow up their

00:38:32.100 --> 00:38:33.660
personal worlds as a consequence.

00:38:33.660 --> 00:38:35.979
And maybe they regret it
later, and maybe they don't.

00:38:36.080 --> 00:38:36.580
Right?

00:38:37.440 --> 00:38:38.160
It's always a question.

00:38:39.429 --> 00:38:40.330
I think there's that.

00:38:41.460 --> 00:38:45.500
And then, I don't know, maybe the other
part of it is that some people just

00:38:45.500 --> 00:38:46.930
need more emotional support than others.

00:38:46.930 --> 00:38:49.620
And I don't know that that's a big, I
don't know that that tilts either way.

00:38:50.429 --> 00:38:53.290
I know some of these people who have
great, loving relationships and seem

00:38:53.290 --> 00:38:56.749
to draw very much on having this
kind of firm foundation to rely upon.

00:38:57.179 --> 00:38:59.179
And then I know other people who
are just like, their personal lives

00:38:59.180 --> 00:39:00.349
are just a continuous train wreck.

00:39:00.359 --> 00:39:03.970
And it doesn't seem to matter,
like, professionally, they just

00:39:03.970 --> 00:39:04.910
keep doing what they're doing.

00:39:04.940 --> 00:39:09.260
And maybe we could talk here
about whatever is the personality

00:39:09.260 --> 00:39:10.090
trait for risk taking.

00:39:10.840 --> 00:39:14.185
Some people are so incredibly risk
prone that they need to take risk in

00:39:14.230 --> 00:39:16.000
all aspects of their lives at all times.

00:39:16.289 --> 00:39:19.100
And if part of their life gets
stable, they find a way to blow it up.

00:39:20.260 --> 00:39:23.580
And that's some of these people you
could describe in those terms also.

00:39:24.140 --> 00:39:25.170
Andrew Huberman: Yeah,
let's talk about that.

00:39:25.810 --> 00:39:30.220
Because I think risk taking and
sensation seeking is something that

00:39:30.270 --> 00:39:34.459
fascinates me for my own reasons
and in my observations of others.

00:39:37.129 --> 00:39:41.950
Does it dovetail with these five traits
in a way that can really serve innovation,

00:39:42.060 --> 00:39:43.470
in ways that can benefit everybody?

00:39:43.480 --> 00:39:47.219
The reason I say to benefit everybody
is because there is a view of how

00:39:47.219 --> 00:39:52.030
we're painting this picture of the
innovator as this really cruel person.

00:39:52.959 --> 00:39:55.520
But oftentimes, what we're talking
about are innovations that make the

00:39:55.520 --> 00:39:58.069
world far better for billions of people.

00:39:59.019 --> 00:39:59.576
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, that's right.

00:39:59.576 --> 00:40:01.500
And by the way, everything we're
talking about also is not just in

00:40:01.500 --> 00:40:03.199
tech or science or in business.

00:40:03.469 --> 00:40:04.989
Everything we're also talking
about is true for the arts.

00:40:06.219 --> 00:40:07.830
The history of artistic expression.

00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:09.859
You have people with all
these same kinds of traits.

00:40:09.880 --> 00:40:12.470
Andrew Huberman: Well, I was thinking
about Picasso and his regular turnover

00:40:12.470 --> 00:40:16.050
of lovers and partners, and he was very
open about the fact that it was one of the

00:40:16.050 --> 00:40:19.890
sources of his productivity, creativity.

00:40:20.260 --> 00:40:21.340
He wasn't shy about that.

00:40:21.660 --> 00:40:24.860
I suppose if he were alive today,
it might be a little bit different.

00:40:24.870 --> 00:40:26.190
He might be judged a little differently.

00:40:26.490 --> 00:40:29.420
Marc Andreessen: Or that was his
story for behaving in a pattern

00:40:29.420 --> 00:40:32.120
that was very awful for the people
around him, and he didn't care.

00:40:32.150 --> 00:40:33.300
Andrew Huberman: Right,
maybe they left him?

00:40:34.250 --> 00:40:34.630
Marc Andreessen: Yeah.
Who knows?

00:40:34.630 --> 00:40:35.130
Right?

00:40:36.500 --> 00:40:38.840
Puts and takes to all this, but no.

00:40:38.849 --> 00:40:39.640
Okay, so I have a theory.

00:40:39.640 --> 00:40:40.460
So here's a theory.

00:40:40.460 --> 00:40:42.320
This is one of these, I keep a
list of things that will get me

00:40:42.320 --> 00:40:46.069
kicked out of a dinner party and
topics at any given point in time.

00:40:46.069 --> 00:40:47.000
Andrew Huberman: Do you
read it before you go in?

00:40:47.029 --> 00:40:47.529
Marc Andreessen: Yeah.

00:40:51.130 --> 00:40:52.410
On auto recall, so that I
can get out of these things.

00:40:52.410 --> 00:40:55.910
Here's the thing that can
get me kicked out of a dinner

00:40:55.910 --> 00:40:57.750
party, especially these days.

00:40:57.760 --> 00:41:03.680
So think of the kind of person where it's
very clear that they're super high, to

00:41:03.680 --> 00:41:06.180
your point, this is somebody who's super
high output in whatever domain they're in.

00:41:06.190 --> 00:41:08.600
They've done things that have
fundamentally changed the world.

00:41:08.600 --> 00:41:11.690
They've brought new, whether it's
businesses or technologies or works

00:41:11.710 --> 00:41:16.570
of art, entire schools of creative
expression, in some cases to the world.

00:41:16.580 --> 00:41:20.060
And then at a certain point, they
blow themselves to smithereens, right?

00:41:20.099 --> 00:41:23.479
And they do that either through
a massive financial scandal.

00:41:23.480 --> 00:41:25.910
They do that through a
massive personal breakdown.

00:41:25.940 --> 00:41:28.920
They do that through some sort
of public expression that causes

00:41:28.920 --> 00:41:29.940
them a huge amount of problems.

00:41:30.510 --> 00:41:33.650
They say the wrong thing, maybe not
once, but several hundred times,

00:41:34.130 --> 00:41:35.389
and blow themselves to smithereens.

00:41:38.030 --> 00:41:40.680
There's this moral arc that people
kind of want to apply, which it's

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:45.210
like the Icarus flying too close to
the sun and he had it coming and he

00:41:45.210 --> 00:41:46.600
needed to keep his ego under control.

00:41:46.600 --> 00:41:50.100
And you get kind of this
judgment that applies.

00:41:50.730 --> 00:41:52.755
So I have a different theory on this.

00:41:52.980 --> 00:41:56.320
So the term I use to describe these
people, and by the way, a lot of other

00:41:56.320 --> 00:41:59.699
people who don't actually blow themselves
up but get close to it, which is a

00:41:59.699 --> 00:42:04.040
whole 'nother set of people, I call
them martyrs to civilizational progress.

00:42:06.290 --> 00:42:07.400
We're backwards, civilizational progress.

00:42:07.580 --> 00:42:11.220
So look, the only way civilization
gets moved forward is when people

00:42:11.220 --> 00:42:12.590
like this do something new.

00:42:12.929 --> 00:42:15.560
Because civilization as a
whole does not do new things.

00:42:16.299 --> 00:42:18.029
Groups of people do not do new things.

00:42:18.340 --> 00:42:20.240
These things don't happen automatically.

00:42:20.560 --> 00:42:22.540
By default nothing changes.

00:42:22.930 --> 00:42:27.410
The only way civilizational change on any
of these axes ever happens is because one

00:42:27.410 --> 00:42:30.540
of these people stands up and says, no,
I'm going to do something different than

00:42:30.540 --> 00:42:31.970
what everybody else has ever done before.

00:42:31.980 --> 00:42:35.810
So, this is progress, like,
this is actually how it happens.

00:42:36.180 --> 00:42:37.810
Sometimes they get lionized or awarded.

00:42:38.190 --> 00:42:39.270
Sometimes they get crucified.

00:42:39.900 --> 00:42:41.620
Sometimes the crucifixion is literal.

00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:43.205
Sometimes it's just symbolic.

00:42:43.540 --> 00:42:49.639
But they are those kinds of people,
and then martyrs when they go down in

00:42:49.639 --> 00:42:53.579
flames and again, this is where it really
screws the people's moral judgments

00:42:53.609 --> 00:42:57.290
because everybody wants to have the sort
of super clear story of like, okay, he

00:42:57.310 --> 00:42:58.960
did a bad thing and he was punished.

00:42:59.540 --> 00:43:03.740
And I'm like, no, he was the kind of
person who was going to do great things

00:43:03.760 --> 00:43:07.539
and also was going to take on a level
of risk and take on a level of sort of

00:43:07.540 --> 00:43:11.215
extreme behavior such that he was going
to expose himself to flying too close to

00:43:11.230 --> 00:43:13.270
the sun, wings melt and crash to ground.

00:43:13.280 --> 00:43:15.340
But it's a package deal.

00:43:16.510 --> 00:43:19.030
The reason you have the Picasso's
and the Beethovens and all these

00:43:19.030 --> 00:43:21.910
people is because they're willing to
take these extreme level of risks.

00:43:22.510 --> 00:43:25.799
They are that creative and original,
not just in their art or their business,

00:43:25.799 --> 00:43:28.489
but in everything else that they
do that they will set themselves up

00:43:28.520 --> 00:43:30.129
to be able to fail psychologically.

00:43:30.379 --> 00:43:32.950
A psychologist would probably, or
psychiatrist would probably say maybe.

00:43:32.950 --> 00:43:36.250
To what extent do they actually
have a death wish at some point.

00:43:36.250 --> 00:43:37.240
Do they want to punish themselves?

00:43:37.240 --> 00:43:37.820
Do they want to fail?

00:43:37.830 --> 00:43:38.650
That I don't know.

00:43:39.330 --> 00:43:40.000
But you see this.

00:43:40.730 --> 00:43:44.054
They deliberately move themselves too
close to the sun, and you can see it

00:43:44.054 --> 00:43:46.630
when it's happening, because if they
get too far away from the sun, they

00:43:46.630 --> 00:43:47.870
deliberately move back towards it.

00:43:47.900 --> 00:43:48.400
Right.

00:43:49.180 --> 00:43:53.290
They come right back, and
they want the risk anyway.

00:43:54.420 --> 00:43:56.140
So martyrs to civilizational progress.

00:43:56.490 --> 00:43:58.100
This is how progress happens.

00:43:58.429 --> 00:44:01.299
When these people crash and
burn, the natural inclination

00:44:01.299 --> 00:44:02.370
is to judge them morally.

00:44:02.520 --> 00:44:05.880
I tend to think we should basically
say, look, and I don't even know if this

00:44:05.900 --> 00:44:09.190
means, like, giving them a moral pass
or whatever, but it's like, look, this

00:44:09.350 --> 00:44:13.180
is how civilization progresses, and we
need to at least understand that there's

00:44:13.180 --> 00:44:17.950
a self sacrificial aspect to this that
may be tragic and often is tragic, but

00:44:18.429 --> 00:44:19.859
it is quite literally self sacrificial.

00:44:20.500 --> 00:44:25.310
Andrew Huberman: Are there any examples
of great innovators who were able to

00:44:25.310 --> 00:44:31.770
compartmentalize their risk taking to
such a degree that they had what seemed

00:44:31.770 --> 00:44:36.800
to be a morally impeccable life in every
domain except in their business pursuits?

00:44:36.860 --> 00:44:37.330
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, that's right.

00:44:37.330 --> 00:44:40.329
So some people are very
highly controlled like that.

00:44:40.690 --> 00:44:44.470
Some people are able to very narrowly,
and I don't really want to set myself

00:44:44.470 --> 00:44:47.779
an example on a lot of this, but I
will tell you as an example, I will

00:44:47.780 --> 00:44:50.200
never use debt in business, number one.

00:44:50.460 --> 00:44:52.840
Number two, I have the most placid
personal life you can imagine.

00:44:52.840 --> 00:44:55.140
Number three, I'm the last
person in the world who is ever

00:44:55.140 --> 00:44:56.480
going to do an extreme sport.

00:44:57.049 --> 00:44:59.009
I mean, I'm not even going to
go in the sauna on the ice bath.

00:45:00.190 --> 00:45:01.109
I'm not doing any of this.

00:45:02.050 --> 00:45:03.060
I'm not tele skiing.

00:45:03.120 --> 00:45:04.649
Andrew Huberman: No obligation.

00:45:04.840 --> 00:45:05.529
Marc Andreessen: I'm not on the Titan.

00:45:06.140 --> 00:45:07.390
I'm not going down to see the Titanic.

00:45:07.390 --> 00:45:09.089
Goodness, you weren't doing any of this.

00:45:09.120 --> 00:45:10.000
I'm not doing any of this stuff.

00:45:10.000 --> 00:45:10.560
I have no interest.

00:45:10.560 --> 00:45:11.150
I don't play golf.

00:45:11.170 --> 00:45:11.680
I don't ski.

00:45:11.710 --> 00:45:13.770
I have no interest in
any of this stuff, right?

00:45:15.000 --> 00:45:17.019
And I know people like this,
right, who are very high achievers.

00:45:17.029 --> 00:45:19.440
It's just like, yeah,
they're completely segmented.

00:45:19.440 --> 00:45:20.530
They're extreme risk takers.

00:45:20.530 --> 00:45:22.900
In business, they're completely buttoned
down on the personal side, they're

00:45:22.900 --> 00:45:24.930
completely buttoned down financially.

00:45:24.960 --> 00:45:28.840
They're scrupulous with following every
rule and law you can possibly imagine,

00:45:29.270 --> 00:45:30.829
but they're still fantastic innovators.

00:45:30.830 --> 00:45:34.690
And then I know many others who are
just like their life is on fire all

00:45:34.690 --> 00:45:36.330
the time, in every possible way.

00:45:36.760 --> 00:45:39.230
And whenever it looks like the fire is
turning into embers, they figure out

00:45:39.230 --> 00:45:44.720
a way to relight the fire, and they
just really want to live on the edge.

00:45:45.190 --> 00:45:48.330
And so I think that's
an independent variable.

00:45:48.670 --> 00:45:49.810
And again, I would apply the same thing.

00:45:49.810 --> 00:45:51.279
I think the same thing
applies to the arts.

00:45:52.670 --> 00:45:53.849
Classical music as an example.

00:45:54.020 --> 00:45:57.410
I think Bach was, as an example,
one of the best musicians of all

00:45:57.420 --> 00:46:01.970
time, had just a completely sedate
personal life, never had any aberrant

00:46:01.970 --> 00:46:03.179
behavior at all in his personal life.

00:46:03.370 --> 00:46:06.459
Family man, tons of kids,
apparently pillar of the community.

00:46:07.139 --> 00:46:07.389
Right.

00:46:07.389 --> 00:46:11.860
And so if Bach could be Bach and yet
not burn his way through 300 mistresses

00:46:11.860 --> 00:46:14.480
or whatever, maybe you can, too.

00:46:15.710 --> 00:46:18.200
Andrew Huberman: So in thinking about
these two different categories of

00:46:18.200 --> 00:46:21.675
innovators, those that take on tremendous
risk in all domains of their life and

00:46:21.900 --> 00:46:25.450
those that take on tremendous risk in
a very compartmentalized way, I don't

00:46:25.450 --> 00:46:30.199
know what the percentages are, but I
have to wonder if in this modern age of

00:46:30.390 --> 00:46:34.750
the public being far less forgivable,
what I'm referring to is cancel culture.

00:46:36.170 --> 00:46:41.440
Do you think that we are limiting
the number of innovations in total

00:46:41.759 --> 00:46:46.609
by just simply frightening or
eliminating an enormous category of

00:46:46.639 --> 00:46:50.200
innovators because they don't have
the confidence or the means or the

00:46:51.580 --> 00:46:53.600
strategies in place to regulate?

00:46:53.800 --> 00:46:56.840
So they're just either bowing out
or they're getting crossed off,

00:46:56.840 --> 00:46:58.120
they're getting canceled one by one.

00:46:58.340 --> 00:47:00.740
Marc Andreessen: So do you think
the public is less tolerant than

00:47:00.740 --> 00:47:02.650
they used to be or more tolerant?

00:47:03.880 --> 00:47:08.189
Andrew Huberman: Well, the systems
that, I'm not going to be careful here.

00:47:08.410 --> 00:47:16.240
I think the large institution systems
are not tolerant of what the public

00:47:16.280 --> 00:47:18.959
tells them they shouldn't be tolerant of.

00:47:20.259 --> 00:47:23.669
And so if there's enough noise,
there's enough noise in the mob.

00:47:23.750 --> 00:47:25.620
I think institutions bow out.

00:47:25.620 --> 00:47:29.090
And here I'm referring not just
to, they essentially say, okay,

00:47:29.090 --> 00:47:30.710
let the cancellation proceed.

00:47:31.780 --> 00:47:35.319
Maybe they're the gavel that
comes down, but they're not the

00:47:35.630 --> 00:47:36.860
lever that got the thing going.

00:47:37.240 --> 00:47:38.990
And so I'm not just
thinking about universities.

00:47:38.990 --> 00:47:40.640
I'm also thinking about advertisers.

00:47:40.640 --> 00:47:44.540
I'm thinking about the big movie
houses that cancel a film that a

00:47:44.540 --> 00:47:47.120
given actor might be in because they
had something in their personal life

00:47:47.120 --> 00:47:48.170
that's still getting worked out.

00:47:48.170 --> 00:47:51.930
I'm thinking about people who
are in a legal process that's not

00:47:51.930 --> 00:47:55.440
yet resolved, but the public has
decided they're a bad person, etc.

00:47:56.139 --> 00:47:57.830
Marc Andreessen: My question is, are
we really talking about the public?

00:47:58.840 --> 00:48:02.880
I agree with your question, and I'm
going to come back to it, but I'm

00:48:02.930 --> 00:48:04.779
going to examine one part of your
question, which is, is this really

00:48:04.780 --> 00:48:05.650
the public we're talking about.

00:48:06.420 --> 00:48:08.540
And I would just say Exhibit A is
who is the current frontrunner for

00:48:09.220 --> 00:48:11.880
the Republican nomination today?

00:48:13.340 --> 00:48:17.710
The public, at least on one side of the
political aisle, seems very on board.

00:48:19.340 --> 00:48:24.410
Number two, like, look, there's a
certain musician who flew too close to

00:48:24.420 --> 00:48:25.860
the sun, blew himself to smithereens.

00:48:25.860 --> 00:48:30.230
He's still hitting all time highs
on music streams every month.

00:48:30.780 --> 00:48:31.650
The public seems fine.

00:48:34.459 --> 00:48:36.970
I would argue the public is actually
more open to these things than

00:48:36.970 --> 00:48:38.140
it actually maybe ever has been.

00:48:39.109 --> 00:48:40.260
And we could talk about
why that's the case.

00:48:40.280 --> 00:48:42.950
I think it's a differentiation,
and this is what your question was

00:48:42.950 --> 00:48:45.350
aiming at, but it's a differentiation
between the public and the elites.

00:48:47.290 --> 00:48:49.819
My view is everything that you just
described is an elite phenomenon.

00:48:50.259 --> 00:48:52.090
And actually, the public is
very much not on board with it.

00:48:53.940 --> 00:48:56.720
So what's actually happening is
what's happened is the public

00:48:56.720 --> 00:48:57.720
and the elites have gapped out.

00:48:58.760 --> 00:49:02.779
The public is more forgiving of what
previously might have been considered kind

00:49:02.780 --> 00:49:06.020
of aberant and extreme behavior, right?

00:49:06.030 --> 00:49:06.140
F.

00:49:06.140 --> 00:49:08.160
Scott Fitzgerald, "there are no
second acts in American lives"

00:49:08.230 --> 00:49:09.229
turns out was completely wrong.

00:49:09.599 --> 00:49:11.220
Turns out there are second
acts, third acts, fourth acts.

00:49:11.420 --> 00:49:12.830
Apparently you can have an
unlimited number of acts.

00:49:12.840 --> 00:49:13.950
The public is actually up for it.

00:49:14.130 --> 00:49:14.340
Yeah.

00:49:14.340 --> 00:49:16.880
Andrew Huberman: I mean, I think
of somebody like Mike Tyson, right?

00:49:16.930 --> 00:49:21.229
I feel like his life
exemplifies everything.

00:49:21.230 --> 00:49:24.840
That's amazing and great and
also terrible about America.

00:49:24.900 --> 00:49:27.960
Marc Andreessen: If we took Mike Tyson to
dinner tonight at any restaurant anywhere

00:49:27.960 --> 00:49:29.299
in the United States, what would happen?

00:49:29.430 --> 00:49:29.970
Andrew Huberman: He would be loved.

00:49:30.109 --> 00:49:34.380
Marc Andreessen: Oh, he would be
like, the outpouring of enthusiasm and

00:49:34.380 --> 00:49:37.150
passion and love would be incredible.

00:49:37.530 --> 00:49:38.670
It would be unbelievable.

00:49:38.720 --> 00:49:39.730
This is a great example.

00:49:40.750 --> 00:49:42.189
And again, I'm not even
going to draw more.

00:49:42.190 --> 00:49:44.190
I'm not even going to say I agree
with that or disagree with that.

00:49:44.190 --> 00:49:48.670
I think we all intuitively know that the
public is just like, 100%, absolutely.

00:49:48.670 --> 00:49:49.340
He's a legend.
He's a living legend.

00:49:49.340 --> 00:49:52.330
He's like a cultural touchstone.

00:49:52.380 --> 00:49:52.810
Absolutely.

00:49:52.810 --> 00:49:54.460
And you see it when he
shows up in movies, right?

00:49:54.790 --> 00:49:57.350
I don't remember the, I mean, the big
breakthrough where I figured this out

00:49:57.350 --> 00:50:00.340
with respect to him because I don't really
follow sports, but when he showed up in

00:50:00.340 --> 00:50:04.439
that, it was that first Hangover movie,
and he shows up and I was in a theater

00:50:04.440 --> 00:50:07.109
and the audience just goes, bananas crazy.

00:50:07.430 --> 00:50:08.750
They're so excited to see him.

00:50:08.930 --> 00:50:09.980
Andrew Huberman: He evokes delight.

00:50:10.120 --> 00:50:12.759
I always say that Mike Tyson is the
only person I'm aware of that can

00:50:12.760 --> 00:50:18.100
wear a shirt with his own name on it,
and it somehow doesn't seem wrong.

00:50:18.150 --> 00:50:20.360
In fact, it just kind of
makes you like him more.

00:50:21.760 --> 00:50:27.990
His ego feels very contoured in a way that
he knows who he is and who he was, and yet

00:50:28.630 --> 00:50:32.470
there's a humbleness woven in, maybe as a
consequence of all that he's been through.

00:50:32.490 --> 00:50:33.140
I don't know.

00:50:33.920 --> 00:50:36.040
But, yeah, people love Mike.

00:50:36.050 --> 00:50:36.979
Marc Andreessen: Public loves him now.

00:50:37.370 --> 00:50:37.830
Exactly.

00:50:37.830 --> 00:50:41.080
Now, if he shows up to lecture at
Harvard, right, I think you're probably

00:50:41.080 --> 00:50:41.959
going to get a different reaction?

00:50:41.989 --> 00:50:42.629
[LAUGHS]
Andrew Huberman: I don't know.

00:50:43.080 --> 00:50:43.720
I don't know!

00:50:44.890 --> 00:50:49.690
You know, the guy who wrote The Wire
gave a talk at Harvard, and it sounded

00:50:49.880 --> 00:50:53.440
to me, based on his report of that,
which is very interesting, in fact,

00:50:54.520 --> 00:51:02.590
that people adore people who are
connected to everybody in that way.

00:51:02.780 --> 00:51:04.080
I feel like everybody loves Mike.

00:51:04.080 --> 00:51:10.189
From above his status, the sides
below his status, he occupies

00:51:10.190 --> 00:51:12.880
this halo of love and adoration.

00:51:12.950 --> 00:51:13.299
Marc Andreessen: Okay.

00:51:13.299 --> 00:51:13.799
Andrew Huberman: All right.

00:51:14.639 --> 00:51:14.839
Marc Andreessen: Yeah.

00:51:14.839 --> 00:51:17.700
Look, the other side of this is
the elites, and you kind of alluded

00:51:17.700 --> 00:51:18.630
to this, of the institution.

00:51:18.840 --> 00:51:21.750
So basically, it's like the people who
are at least nominally in charge or

00:51:21.750 --> 00:51:22.836
feel like that they should be in charge.

00:51:22.970 --> 00:51:24.640
Andrew Huberman: I want to
make sure we define elite.

00:51:24.640 --> 00:51:26.430
So you're not necessarily talking
about people who are wealthy.

00:51:26.430 --> 00:51:29.330
You're talking about people who
have authority within institutions.

00:51:29.330 --> 00:51:31.129
Marc Andreessen: So the ultimate
definition of an elite is

00:51:31.130 --> 00:51:34.499
who can get who fired, right.

00:51:34.809 --> 00:51:35.860
That's the ultimate test.

00:51:35.860 --> 00:51:38.740
Who can get who fired, boycotted,
blacklisted, ostracized.

00:51:38.770 --> 00:51:43.270
Like when push, prosecuted, jailed,
like when push comes to shove.

00:51:43.700 --> 00:51:46.170
I think that's always the question,
who can destroy whose career?

00:51:46.270 --> 00:51:48.520
And of course, you'll notice
that that is heavily asymmetric

00:51:49.290 --> 00:51:50.120
when these fights play out.

00:51:50.130 --> 00:51:53.479
Like, it's very clear which side can get
the other side fired and which side can't.

00:51:54.270 --> 00:51:56.390
And so, yeah, so, look, I think
we live in a period of time where

00:51:56.430 --> 00:51:59.109
the elites have gotten to be
extreme in a number of dimensions.

00:51:59.160 --> 00:52:03.370
I think it's characterized by, for
sure, extreme groupthink, extreme

00:52:04.389 --> 00:52:09.770
sanctimony, extreme moral, I would
say dudgeon, this weird sort of modern

00:52:10.949 --> 00:52:14.470
puritanism, and then an extreme sort
of morality of punishment and terror

00:52:15.190 --> 00:52:16.390
against their perceived enemies.

00:52:17.350 --> 00:52:19.940
But I want to go through that
because I actually think that's

00:52:19.940 --> 00:52:21.069
a very different phenomenon.

00:52:21.069 --> 00:52:22.780
I think what's happening at the
elites is very different than what's

00:52:22.780 --> 00:52:24.420
happening in the population at large.

00:52:24.910 --> 00:52:27.530
And then, of course, I think there's
a feedback loop in there, which

00:52:27.530 --> 00:52:30.560
is, I think the population at large
is not on board with that program.

00:52:31.190 --> 00:52:31.500
Right.

00:52:31.500 --> 00:52:33.549
I think the elites are aware
that the population is not

00:52:33.549 --> 00:52:34.359
on board WIth that program.

00:52:34.360 --> 00:52:37.650
I think they judge the population
negatively as a consequence, that causes

00:52:37.650 --> 00:52:39.340
the elites to harden their own positions.

00:52:39.340 --> 00:52:42.150
That causes them to be even more
alienating to the population.

00:52:42.150 --> 00:52:45.420
And so they're in sort of an
oppositional negative feedback loop.

00:52:45.799 --> 00:52:50.500
But again, it's a sort of question,
okay, who can get who fired?

00:52:50.500 --> 00:52:54.780
And so elites are really good
at getting normal people fired.

00:52:55.320 --> 00:52:58.259
Ostracized, banned, hit pieces
in the press, like, whatever.

00:52:59.490 --> 00:53:03.259
For normal people to get elites fired,
they have to really band together, right.

00:53:03.259 --> 00:53:06.860
And really mount a serious challenge,
which mostly doesn't happen, but might

00:53:06.860 --> 00:53:07.980
be starting to happen in some cases.

00:53:08.280 --> 00:53:14.160
Andrew Huberman: Do you think this
power of the elites over, stemmed

00:53:14.180 --> 00:53:18.010
from social media sort of going
against its original purpose?

00:53:18.010 --> 00:53:20.009
I mean, when you think social
media, you think you're giving each

00:53:20.009 --> 00:53:23.990
and every person their own little
reality TV show, their own voice.

00:53:24.320 --> 00:53:29.750
And yet we've seen a dramatic uptick
in the number of cancellations and

00:53:29.750 --> 00:53:34.500
firings related to immoral behavior
based on things that were either

00:53:34.510 --> 00:53:36.720
done or amplified on social media.

00:53:37.139 --> 00:53:40.439
It's almost as if the public is
holding the wrong end of the knife.

00:53:41.150 --> 00:53:45.800
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, so the way I
describe it, I use these two terms,

00:53:45.800 --> 00:53:48.300
and they're somewhat interchangeable,
but elites and institutions.

00:53:48.300 --> 00:53:50.640
And then they're somewhat interchangeable
because who runs the institutions?

00:53:50.670 --> 00:53:51.379
The elites, right?

00:53:51.379 --> 00:53:53.979
And so it's sort of a
self reinforcing thing.

00:53:55.479 --> 00:53:56.529
And institutions of all kinds.

00:53:56.530 --> 00:53:59.549
Institutions, everything from the
government, bureaucracies, companies,

00:53:59.570 --> 00:54:03.319
nonprofits, foundations, NGOs,
tech companies, on and on and on.

00:54:03.360 --> 00:54:08.450
Like people who are in charge of big
complexes and that carry a lot of,

00:54:08.450 --> 00:54:11.890
basically, power and influence and
capability and money as a consequence

00:54:11.890 --> 00:54:13.030
of their positional authority.

00:54:13.759 --> 00:54:17.440
So the head of a giant foundation
may never have done anything in their

00:54:17.440 --> 00:54:19.739
life that would cause somebody to have
a high opinion of them as a person.

00:54:19.740 --> 00:54:22.160
But they're in charge of this
gigantic multi billion dollar

00:54:22.160 --> 00:54:23.609
complex and have all this power.

00:54:23.610 --> 00:54:27.140
And so that's just defined
terms, at least in institutions.

00:54:28.309 --> 00:54:29.360
So, it's actually interesting.

00:54:29.570 --> 00:54:34.310
Gallup has been doing polls on the
following on the question of trust

00:54:34.310 --> 00:54:37.440
in institutions, which is sort of
therefore a proxy for trust in elites,

00:54:38.230 --> 00:54:39.220
basically since the early 1970s.

00:54:41.490 --> 00:54:45.349
And they do this across all the categories
of big institutions, basically everyone.

00:54:45.349 --> 00:54:46.559
I just talked about a bunch of others.

00:54:46.590 --> 00:54:49.479
Big business, small business,
banks, newspapers, broadcast

00:54:49.480 --> 00:54:52.089
television, the military, police.

00:54:52.230 --> 00:54:53.979
So they've got like 30
categories or something.

00:54:53.979 --> 00:54:57.650
And basically what you see is almost
all the categories basically started in

00:54:57.650 --> 00:55:00.200
the early 70s at like 60 or 70% trust.

00:55:00.200 --> 00:55:04.500
And now almost across the board,
they've just had a complete,

00:55:04.500 --> 00:55:08.020
basically linear slide down for
50 years, basically my whole life.

00:55:09.150 --> 00:55:11.610
And they're now bottoming out.

00:55:11.869 --> 00:55:16.540
Congress and journalists
bottom out at like 10%.

00:55:16.580 --> 00:55:18.480
The two groups everybody hates
are Congress and journalists.

00:55:19.440 --> 00:55:21.855
And then it's like a lot of
other big institutions are

00:55:21.900 --> 00:55:24.020
like, in their 20s, 30s, 40s.

00:55:24.250 --> 00:55:26.000
Actually, big business
actually scores fairly high.

00:55:26.030 --> 00:55:27.380
Tech actually scores quite high.

00:55:27.630 --> 00:55:29.049
The military scores quite high.

00:55:29.080 --> 00:55:31.230
But basically everything
else has really caved in.

00:55:32.350 --> 00:55:35.410
This is sort of my fundamental challenge
to everybody who basically says, and

00:55:35.630 --> 00:55:38.910
you didn't do this, but you'll hear the
simple form of this, which is social

00:55:38.910 --> 00:55:41.099
media caused the current trouble.

00:55:41.540 --> 00:55:45.679
And let's call this an example, collapse
in faith in institutions and elites.

00:55:45.700 --> 00:55:47.050
Let's call that part
of the current trouble.

00:55:47.969 --> 00:55:49.136
Everybody's like, well,
social media caused that.

00:55:49.136 --> 00:55:52.340
I was like, well, no, social
media, social media is new, right?

00:55:52.370 --> 00:55:52.910
In the last...

00:55:53.130 --> 00:55:56.390
social media is effectively new,
practically speaking, since 2010,

00:55:56.390 --> 00:55:57.449
2012 is when it really took off.

00:55:58.189 --> 00:56:02.060
And so, if the trend started in the
early 1970s and has been continuous, then

00:56:02.060 --> 00:56:03.380
we're dealing with something broader.

00:56:04.029 --> 00:56:08.549
Martin Gurri wrote, I think, the best book
on this called the Revolt of the Public

00:56:08.550 --> 00:56:09.720
, where he goes through this in detail.

00:56:11.190 --> 00:56:13.470
He does say that social media
had a lot to do with what's

00:56:13.470 --> 00:56:14.350
happened in the last decade.

00:56:14.799 --> 00:56:16.779
But he says, yeah, if you go
back, you look further, it was

00:56:16.780 --> 00:56:18.530
basically two things coinciding.

00:56:19.210 --> 00:56:21.430
One was just a general change
in the media environment.

00:56:21.469 --> 00:56:25.510
And in particular, the 1970s is when you
started to, and especially in the 1980s,

00:56:25.520 --> 00:56:29.270
is when you started to get specifically
talk radio, which was a new outlet.

00:56:29.270 --> 00:56:31.790
And then you also got cable television.

00:56:32.870 --> 00:56:35.329
And then you also, by the way, it's
actually interesting in that you had

00:56:35.329 --> 00:56:37.630
paperback books, which was another
one of these, which was an outlet.

00:56:37.639 --> 00:56:40.889
So you had like a fracturing in the
media landscape that started in the

00:56:40.889 --> 00:56:44.200
50s through the, then, of course,
the Internet blew it wide open.

00:56:45.160 --> 00:56:47.920
Having said that, if the elites and
the institutions were fantastic,

00:56:47.930 --> 00:56:48.880
you would know it more than ever.

00:56:50.150 --> 00:56:51.270
Information is more accessible.

00:56:51.270 --> 00:56:54.539
And so the other thing that he says,
and I agree with, is the public is

00:56:54.540 --> 00:56:57.260
not being tricked into thinking the
elites and institutions are bad.

00:56:57.260 --> 00:57:01.790
They're learning that they're bad, and
therefore, the mystery of the Gallup

00:57:01.790 --> 00:57:06.420
poll is why those numbers aren't all
just zero, which is arguably, in a

00:57:06.420 --> 00:57:07.410
lot of cases, where they should be.

00:57:08.059 --> 00:57:09.209
Andrew Huberman: I think one reason that--

00:57:09.490 --> 00:57:10.790
Marc Andreessen: --By the
way, he thinks this is bad.

00:57:10.790 --> 00:57:11.879
So he and I have a different view.

00:57:12.139 --> 00:57:13.229
So here's where he and I disagree.

00:57:13.400 --> 00:57:14.300
He thinks this is bad.

00:57:14.309 --> 00:57:17.459
So he basically says, you can't
replace elites with nothing.

00:57:18.059 --> 00:57:21.650
You can't replace institutions with
nothing, because what you're just left

00:57:21.650 --> 00:57:22.720
with is just going to be wreckage.

00:57:22.720 --> 00:57:25.970
You're going to be left with a completely,
basically atomized, out of control

00:57:25.970 --> 00:57:29.689
society that has no ability to marshal
any sort of activity in any direction.

00:57:29.690 --> 00:57:32.189
It's just going to be a
dog eat dog awful world.

00:57:32.960 --> 00:57:35.370
I have a very different view on
that which we can talk about.

00:57:35.670 --> 00:57:37.360
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, I'd love
to hear your views on that.

