00:58:44.799 --> 00:58:48.550
The quick question I was going to ask
before we go there is, I think that

00:58:48.550 --> 00:58:53.350
one reason that I and many other people
sort of reflexively assume that social

00:58:53.350 --> 00:58:59.460
media caused the demise of our faith and
institutions is, well, first of all, I

00:58:59.460 --> 00:59:05.470
wasn't aware of this lack of correlation
between the decline in faith in

00:59:05.470 --> 00:59:07.900
institutions and the rise of social media.

00:59:08.250 --> 00:59:14.379
But secondarily that we've seen
some movements that have essentially

00:59:14.380 --> 00:59:20.450
rooted themselves in tweets, in
comments, in posts that get amplified,

00:59:20.780 --> 00:59:24.349
and those tweets and comments and
posts come from everyday people.

00:59:24.430 --> 00:59:30.270
In fact, I can't name one person who
initiated a given cancellation or

00:59:30.270 --> 00:59:34.160
movement because it was the sort of
dogpiling or mob adding-on to some

00:59:34.160 --> 00:59:35.370
person that was essentially anonymous.

00:59:35.370 --> 00:59:38.430
So I think that for many of us, we
have the, to use neuroscience language,

00:59:38.430 --> 00:59:43.520
as sort of a bottom up perspective,
oh, someone sees something in their

00:59:43.520 --> 00:59:46.870
daily life or experiences something in
their daily life, and they tweet about

00:59:46.870 --> 00:59:51.800
it or they comment about it or they
post about it, and then enough people

00:59:52.230 --> 00:59:57.980
dogpile on the accused that it picks
up force, and then the elites feel

00:59:58.000 --> 01:00:02.220
compelled, obligated to cancel somebody.

01:00:03.220 --> 01:00:04.610
That tends to be the narrative.

01:00:04.680 --> 01:00:07.470
And so I think the logical
conclusion is, oh, social media

01:00:07.480 --> 01:00:08.820
allows for this to happen.

01:00:08.820 --> 01:00:11.270
Whereas normally someone would just
be standing on the corner shouting

01:00:11.270 --> 01:00:14.949
or calling lawyers that don't have
faith in them, and you've got the Erin

01:00:14.950 --> 01:00:18.990
Brockovich model that turns into a movie.

01:00:18.990 --> 01:00:23.230
But that's a rare case of this lone woman
who's got this idea in mind about how a

01:00:23.240 --> 01:00:27.910
big institution is doing wrong or somebody
is doing wrong in the world and then can

01:00:27.910 --> 01:00:29.410
leverage the big institution, excuse me.

01:00:30.219 --> 01:00:36.089
But the way that you describe it is
that the elites are leading this shift.

01:00:36.430 --> 01:00:39.720
So what is the role of the public in it?

01:00:40.210 --> 01:00:47.270
Just to give it a concrete example,
if, for instance, no one tweeted or

01:00:47.270 --> 01:00:53.429
commented on me, too, or no one tweeted
or commented about some ill behavior

01:00:53.430 --> 01:00:57.610
of some, I don't know, university
faculty member or business person,

01:00:58.940 --> 01:01:00.600
would the elite have come down on them?

01:01:00.660 --> 01:01:02.629
Marc Andreessen: Anyway, what's happening?

01:01:04.270 --> 01:01:09.250
Based on what I've seen over the years,
there is so much astroturfing right now.

01:01:10.690 --> 01:01:12.819
There are entire categories of
people who are paid to do this.

01:01:14.020 --> 01:01:16.400
Some of them we call journalists,
some of them we call activists,

01:01:16.410 --> 01:01:18.830
some of them we call NGO nonprofit.

01:01:18.850 --> 01:01:20.929
Some of them we call university
professors, some of them we

01:01:20.929 --> 01:01:23.170
call grad students, whatever,
they're paid to do this.

01:01:24.210 --> 01:01:27.240
I don't know if you've ever looked into
the misinformation industrial complex?

01:01:27.620 --> 01:01:29.969
There's this whole universe of
basically these funded groups

01:01:29.969 --> 01:01:31.549
that basically do misinformation.

01:01:33.250 --> 01:01:35.880
And they're constantly mounting
these kinds of attacks.

01:01:36.110 --> 01:01:38.670
They're constantly trying to gin
up this kind of basically panic

01:01:38.679 --> 01:01:39.649
to cause somebody to get fired.

01:01:39.649 --> 01:01:41.540
Andrew Huberman: So
it's not a grassroots--

01:01:41.550 --> 01:01:42.980
Marc Andreessen: --No.
It's the opposite of grassroots.

01:01:43.009 --> 01:01:43.509
No.

01:01:43.719 --> 01:01:45.209
Almost always going to
trace these things back.

01:01:45.559 --> 01:01:50.710
It was a journalist, it was an activist,
it was a public figure of some kind.

01:01:51.940 --> 01:01:55.360
These are entrepreneurs
in a sort of a weird way.

01:01:55.920 --> 01:02:00.770
Basically their job, mission
calling, is all wrapped up together

01:02:00.770 --> 01:02:02.560
like they're true believers, but
they're also getting paid to do it.

01:02:03.299 --> 01:02:05.619
And there's a giant funding, I
mean, there's a very large funding

01:02:05.650 --> 01:02:09.580
complex for this coming from
certain high profile people who put

01:02:09.580 --> 01:02:10.450
huge amounts of money into this.

01:02:10.460 --> 01:02:11.279
Andrew Huberman: Is this well known?

01:02:11.520 --> 01:02:11.890
Marc Andreessen: Yes.

01:02:11.890 --> 01:02:13.330
Well, it is in my world.

01:02:13.350 --> 01:02:16.039
So this is what the social media
companies have been on the receiving

01:02:16.040 --> 01:02:17.120
end of for the last decade.

01:02:19.570 --> 01:02:23.870
It's basically a political media activism
complex with very deep pockets behind it.

01:02:23.910 --> 01:02:26.580
And you've got people who basically,
literally have people who sit all day

01:02:26.860 --> 01:02:29.700
and watch the TV network on the other
side or watch the Twitter feeds on the

01:02:29.700 --> 01:02:31.500
other side, and they basically wait.

01:02:31.690 --> 01:02:34.129
It's like every politician, this has
been the case for a long time now.

01:02:34.130 --> 01:02:36.659
Every politician who goes out and gives
stump speeches, you'll see there's always

01:02:36.670 --> 01:02:39.549
somebody in the crowd with a camcorder
or now with a phone recording them.

01:02:39.549 --> 01:02:42.830
And that's somebody from the other
campaign who's paid somebody to

01:02:42.830 --> 01:02:45.610
just be there and record every
single thing the politician says.

01:02:46.040 --> 01:02:48.740
So that when a Mitt Romney says,
whatever, the 47% thing, they've

01:02:48.740 --> 01:02:51.740
got it on tape, and then they clip
it, and they try to make it viral.

01:02:51.750 --> 01:02:55.780
And again, look, these people
believe what they're doing.

01:02:55.780 --> 01:02:56.960
I'm not saying it's even dishonest.

01:02:56.960 --> 01:02:58.059
Like, these people believe
what they're doing.

01:02:58.059 --> 01:02:59.189
They think they're fighting a holy war.

01:02:59.190 --> 01:03:00.480
They think they're protecting democracy.

01:03:00.490 --> 01:03:01.910
They think they're
protecting civilization.

01:03:01.910 --> 01:03:04.209
They think they're protecting
whatever it is they're protecting.

01:03:04.700 --> 01:03:08.450
And then they know how to use
the tools, and so they know how

01:03:08.450 --> 01:03:10.190
to try to gin up the outrage.

01:03:10.240 --> 01:03:12.960
And then, by the way, sometimes
it works in social cascades.

01:03:12.969 --> 01:03:14.420
Sometimes it works, sometimes it doesn't.

01:03:14.889 --> 01:03:16.590
Sometimes they cascade,
sometimes they don't.

01:03:16.740 --> 01:03:19.830
But if you follow these people on
Twitter, this is what they do every day.

01:03:20.280 --> 01:03:21.850
They're constantly trying
to, like, light this fire.

01:03:23.430 --> 01:03:26.040
Andrew Huberman: I assume that it was
really bottom up, but it sounds like

01:03:26.040 --> 01:03:30.210
it's sort of middle level, and that
it captures the elites, and then the

01:03:30.210 --> 01:03:31.466
thing takes on a life of its own.

01:03:31.599 --> 01:03:33.359
Marc Andreessen: By the way, it also
intersects with the trust and safety

01:03:33.360 --> 01:03:36.810
groups at the social media firms who are
responsible for figuring out who gets

01:03:36.810 --> 01:03:38.905
promoted and who gets banned across this.

01:03:39.040 --> 01:03:41.690
And you'll notice one large social
media company has recently changed

01:03:41.690 --> 01:03:46.240
hands and has implemented a different
kind of set of trust and safety.

01:03:46.590 --> 01:03:48.539
And all of a sudden, a different
kind of boycott movement has

01:03:48.540 --> 01:03:50.830
all of a sudden started to work
that wasn't working before that.

01:03:50.830 --> 01:03:53.460
And another kind of boycott movement
is not working as well anymore.

01:03:54.160 --> 01:03:57.020
And so, for sure, there's
an intermediation happening.

01:03:57.180 --> 01:03:59.500
Look, the stuff that's happening in
the world today is being intermediated

01:03:59.770 --> 01:04:02.529
through social media, because social
media is the defining media of our time.

01:04:03.349 --> 01:04:06.729
But there are people who know how
to do this and do this for a living.

01:04:06.980 --> 01:04:14.299
No, I view very much the cancellation
wave, like, this whole thing, it's an

01:04:14.300 --> 01:04:19.049
elite phenomenon, and when it appears
to be a grassroots thing, it's either

01:04:19.050 --> 01:04:22.620
grassroots among the elites, which
is possible because there's a fairly

01:04:22.620 --> 01:04:26.579
large number of people who are signed
up for that particular crusade, but

01:04:26.580 --> 01:04:28.700
there's also a lot of astroturfing
that's taking place inside that.

01:04:29.199 --> 01:04:31.290
The question is, okay, at what
point does the population at

01:04:31.290 --> 01:04:32.580
large get pulled into this?

01:04:32.960 --> 01:04:35.980
And maybe there are movements,
certain points in time where they

01:04:35.980 --> 01:04:38.120
do get pulled in, and then maybe
later they get disillusioned.

01:04:38.120 --> 01:04:39.440
And so then there's some question there.

01:04:39.440 --> 01:04:41.920
And then there's another question
of like, well, if the population at

01:04:41.920 --> 01:04:44.060
large is going to decide what these
movements are, are they going to be the

01:04:44.060 --> 01:04:45.830
same movements that the elites want?

01:04:46.310 --> 01:04:48.750
And how are the elites going
to react when the population

01:04:48.760 --> 01:04:50.740
actually fully expresses itself?

01:04:51.770 --> 01:04:54.420
Like I said, there's a feedback loop
between these where the more extreme

01:04:54.420 --> 01:04:56.815
the elites get, they tend to push
the population to more extreme views

01:04:56.815 --> 01:04:58.330
on the other side and vice versa.

01:04:58.360 --> 01:04:59.989
So it ping pongs back and forth.

01:04:59.990 --> 01:05:01.909
And so, yeah, this is our world.

01:05:01.990 --> 01:05:04.070
Andrew Huberman: Yeah,
this explains a lot.

01:05:05.199 --> 01:05:08.970
Marc Andreessen: I want to make sure
that Schellenberger, Matt Taibbi, a bunch

01:05:08.970 --> 01:05:10.399
of these guys have done a lot of work.

01:05:11.570 --> 01:05:14.580
If you just look into what's called
the misinformation industrial complex,

01:05:14.950 --> 01:05:17.919
you'll find a network of money and
power that is really quite amazing.

01:05:18.750 --> 01:05:22.119
Andrew Huberman: I've seen more
and more Schellenberger showing up.

01:05:22.190 --> 01:05:22.450
Marc Andreessen: Right.

01:05:22.450 --> 01:05:24.109
And he's just, look,
he's just on this stuff.

01:05:24.139 --> 01:05:26.720
He, and just, they're literally
just like tracking money.

01:05:27.920 --> 01:05:31.250
It's very clear how the money flows,
including a remarkable amount of money

01:05:31.260 --> 01:05:34.760
out of the government, which is, of
course, in theory, very concerning.

01:05:35.940 --> 01:05:36.630
Andrew Huberman: Very interesting.

01:05:36.630 --> 01:05:39.080
Marc Andreessen: The government should
not be funding programs that take

01:05:39.080 --> 01:05:40.330
away people's constitutional rights.

01:05:40.369 --> 01:05:43.410
And yet somehow that is
what's been happening.

01:05:44.170 --> 01:05:44.980
Andrew Huberman: Very interesting.

01:05:46.130 --> 01:05:50.730
I want to make sure that I hear
your ideas about why the decline

01:05:50.730 --> 01:05:55.669
in confidence in institutions
is not necessarily problematic.

01:05:56.200 --> 01:05:59.839
Is this going to be a total
destruction, burning down of the

01:05:59.840 --> 01:06:01.430
forest that will lead to new life?

01:06:01.630 --> 01:06:02.390
Is that your view?

01:06:03.110 --> 01:06:04.259
Marc Andreessen: Well,
so this is the thing.

01:06:04.270 --> 01:06:06.600
And look, there's a question if you're,
there's a couple of questions in here,

01:06:06.600 --> 01:06:09.984
which is like, how bad is it really?

01:06:09.984 --> 01:06:10.484
How bad are they?

01:06:10.960 --> 01:06:13.200
Right.
And I think they're pretty bad.

01:06:13.530 --> 01:06:14.750
A lot of them are actually pretty bad.

01:06:16.490 --> 01:06:17.459
So that's one big question.

01:06:17.469 --> 01:06:20.710
And then, yeah, look, the other question
is like, okay, if the institution has gone

01:06:20.720 --> 01:06:24.830
bad or a group of elites have gone bad,
it's this wonderful word, reform, right?

01:06:25.330 --> 01:06:26.129
Can they be reformed?

01:06:26.130 --> 01:06:28.629
And everybody always wants to reform
everything, and yet somehow nothing

01:06:28.630 --> 01:06:30.110
ever quite ever gets reformed.

01:06:30.960 --> 01:06:33.560
And so people are trying to reform
housing policy in the Bay Area for

01:06:33.560 --> 01:06:35.520
decades, and we're not building.

01:06:35.529 --> 01:06:36.950
We're building fewer
houses than ever before.

01:06:36.950 --> 01:06:40.420
So somehow reform movements seem
to lead to just more bad stuff.

01:06:40.980 --> 01:06:41.550
But anyway, yeah.

01:06:41.580 --> 01:06:43.660
So if you have an existing
institution, can it be reformed?

01:06:43.660 --> 01:06:44.839
Can it be fixed from the inside?

01:06:45.340 --> 01:06:46.370
What's happened in universities?

01:06:47.010 --> 01:06:49.060
There are professors at Stanford
as an example, who very much

01:06:49.060 --> 01:06:50.259
think that they can fix Stanford.

01:06:51.509 --> 01:06:52.770
Like, I don't know what you think.

01:06:52.770 --> 01:06:56.200
It doesn't seem like it's going in
productive directions right now.

01:06:56.200 --> 01:06:57.900
Andrew Huberman: Well, I mean,
there are many things about Stanford

01:06:57.900 --> 01:06:59.470
that function extremely well.

01:06:59.640 --> 01:07:00.919
It's a big institution.

01:07:00.920 --> 01:07:03.079
It's certainly got its
issues like any other place.

01:07:03.679 --> 01:07:06.280
They're also my employer, Marc's
giving me some interesting looks.

01:07:06.280 --> 01:07:07.669
He wants me to get a little more vocal.

01:07:07.679 --> 01:07:11.393
Marc Andreessen: I didn't
mean to put you on the spot.

01:07:11.580 --> 01:07:11.820
Yeah.

01:07:11.820 --> 01:07:13.490
Andrew Huberman: I mean, one of
the things about being a researcher

01:07:13.500 --> 01:07:16.540
at a big institution like Stanford
is, well, first of all, it meets

01:07:16.540 --> 01:07:17.529
the criteria that you described.

01:07:18.240 --> 01:07:21.110
Know, you look to the left, you look
to the right or anywhere above or

01:07:21.110 --> 01:07:23.590
below you, and you have excellence.

01:07:23.679 --> 01:07:24.009
Right?

01:07:24.009 --> 01:07:26.619
I mean, I've got a Nobel Prize
winner below me whose daddy also won

01:07:26.620 --> 01:07:30.200
a Nobel Prize, and his scientific
offspring is likely to win.

01:07:30.290 --> 01:07:34.610
I mean, it inspires you to
do bigger things than one

01:07:34.650 --> 01:07:36.210
ordinarily would, no matter what.

01:07:36.290 --> 01:07:37.860
So there's that, and that's great.

01:07:37.870 --> 01:07:38.660
And that persists.

01:07:39.480 --> 01:07:43.779
There's all the bureaucratic red tape
about trying to get things done and how

01:07:43.780 --> 01:07:47.590
to implement decisions is very hard,
and there are a lot of reasons for that.

01:07:47.619 --> 01:07:50.480
And then, of course, there are the
things that many people are aware of.

01:07:50.960 --> 01:07:55.510
There are public accusations about
people in positions of great leadership,

01:07:55.510 --> 01:07:56.419
and that's getting played out.

01:07:56.419 --> 01:08:00.100
And the whole thing becomes kind
of overwhelming and a little bit

01:08:00.100 --> 01:08:03.430
opaque when you're just trying to
run your lab or live your life.

01:08:03.430 --> 01:08:06.250
And so I think one of the reasons
for this lack of reform that you're

01:08:06.250 --> 01:08:11.870
referring to is because there's
no position of reformer, right?

01:08:11.900 --> 01:08:14.909
So deans are dealing with a lot of issues.

01:08:14.910 --> 01:08:16.330
Provosts are dealing with a lot of issues.

01:08:16.349 --> 01:08:20.170
Presidents are dealing with a lot of
issues, and then some in some cases.

01:08:20.200 --> 01:08:25.250
And so we don't have a dedicated role
of reformer, someone to go in and

01:08:25.250 --> 01:08:28.970
say, listen, there's just a lot of
fat on this and we need to trim it

01:08:28.970 --> 01:08:30.779
or we need to create this or do that.

01:08:30.779 --> 01:08:32.629
There just isn't a system to do that.

01:08:33.279 --> 01:08:37.370
And that's, I think in part, because
universities are built on old systems,

01:08:37.370 --> 01:08:39.970
and it's like the New York subway.

01:08:40.020 --> 01:08:43.290
It's amazing i t still works as
well as it does, and yet it's

01:08:43.290 --> 01:08:44.830
got a ton of problems also.

01:08:46.220 --> 01:08:48.970
Marc Andreessen: So the point, we could
debate the university specifically,

01:08:48.970 --> 01:08:51.811
but the point is like, look, if you do
think institutions are going bad, and

01:08:51.811 --> 01:08:52.990
then you have to make it number one.

01:08:52.990 --> 01:08:54.740
You have to figure out if you
think institutions are going bad.

01:08:54.830 --> 01:08:58.379
The population largely does think
that at the very least, the people

01:08:58.379 --> 01:09:00.610
who run institutions ought to really
think hard about what that means.

01:09:00.779 --> 01:09:03.830
Andrew Huberman: But people still
strive to go to these places.

01:09:03.850 --> 01:09:07.020
And I still hear from people
who, for instance, did not go to

01:09:07.020 --> 01:09:09.870
college, are talking about how
a university degree is useless.

01:09:10.040 --> 01:09:12.450
They'll tell you how proud they are
that their son or daughter is going

01:09:12.450 --> 01:09:16.310
to Stanford or is going to UCLA
or is going to Urbana Champaign.

01:09:16.310 --> 01:09:21.140
I mean, it's almost like, to me, that's
always the most shocking contradiction,

01:09:21.140 --> 01:09:23.310
is like, these institutions don't matter.

01:09:23.310 --> 01:09:26.149
But then when people want to hold
up a card that says why their

01:09:26.149 --> 01:09:29.529
kid is great, it's not about how
many pushups they can do or that

01:09:29.529 --> 01:09:30.609
they started their own business.

01:09:30.630 --> 01:09:33.720
Most of the time it's they're
going to this university.

01:09:33.759 --> 01:09:35.509
And I think, well, what's going on here?

01:09:35.619 --> 01:09:37.300
Marc Andreessen: So do you think the
median voter in the United States

01:09:37.300 --> 01:09:38.330
can have their kid go to Stanford?

01:09:38.510 --> 01:09:39.010
Andrew Huberman: No.

01:09:40.730 --> 01:09:41.830
Marc Andreessen: Do you think the
median voter in the United States

01:09:41.950 --> 01:09:45.420
could have their kid admitted to
Stanford, even with a perfect SAT?

01:09:45.800 --> 01:09:46.399
Andrew Huberman: No, no.

01:09:47.879 --> 01:09:51.099
In this day and age, the competition
is so fierce that it requires more.

01:09:51.259 --> 01:09:51.529
Marc Andreessen: Yeah.

01:09:51.529 --> 01:09:54.110
So first of all, again,
we're dealing here.

01:09:54.129 --> 01:09:54.400
Yes.

01:09:54.400 --> 01:09:57.010
We're dealing with a small number
of very elite institutions.

01:09:57.260 --> 01:09:58.710
People may admire them or not.

01:09:59.390 --> 01:10:01.540
Most people have no
connectivity to them whatsoever.

01:10:01.930 --> 01:10:05.780
In the statistics, in the polling,
universities are not doing well.

01:10:06.570 --> 01:10:08.861
The population at large, yeah,
they may have fantasies about their

01:10:08.861 --> 01:10:10.959
kid going to Stanford, but the
reality of it is they have a very

01:10:11.839 --> 01:10:13.240
collapsing view of these institutions.

01:10:13.250 --> 01:10:17.629
So anyway, this actually goes straight to
the question of alternatives then, right?

01:10:17.630 --> 01:10:20.239
Which is like, okay, if you believe
that there's collapsing faith in the

01:10:20.240 --> 01:10:23.670
institutions, if you believe that it
is merited, at least in some ways, if

01:10:23.670 --> 01:10:26.440
you believe that reform is effectively
impossible, then you are faced...

01:10:27.940 --> 01:10:30.250
We could debate each of those,
but the population at large

01:10:30.250 --> 01:10:32.400
seems to believe a lot of that.

01:10:32.450 --> 01:10:35.090
Then there's a question of
like, okay, can it be replaced?

01:10:35.090 --> 01:10:38.339
And if so, are you better off
replacing these things basically,

01:10:38.340 --> 01:10:39.490
while the old things still exist?

01:10:39.500 --> 01:10:41.990
Or do you actually need to
basically clear the field to be

01:10:41.990 --> 01:10:43.009
able to have the new thing exist?

01:10:44.059 --> 01:10:46.690
The universities are a great
case study of this because of

01:10:46.690 --> 01:10:48.059
how student loans work, right?

01:10:48.059 --> 01:10:53.410
And the way student loans work is to
be an actual competitive university

01:10:53.410 --> 01:10:55.520
and compete, you need to have
access to federal student lending.

01:10:55.530 --> 01:10:57.470
Because if you don't, everybody
has to pay out of pocket.

01:10:57.470 --> 01:11:00.330
And it's completely out of reach for
anybody other than a certain class of

01:11:00.610 --> 01:11:02.370
either extremely rich or foreign students.

01:11:02.380 --> 01:11:04.789
So you need access to a
federal student loan facility.

01:11:04.789 --> 01:11:06.670
To get access to a federal
student loan facility, you need

01:11:06.670 --> 01:11:08.360
to be an accredited university.

01:11:09.700 --> 01:11:11.240
Guess who runs the accreditation council?

01:11:11.250 --> 01:11:12.380
Andrew Huberman: I don't know.

01:11:12.400 --> 01:11:14.330
Marc Andreessen: The
existing universities, right?

01:11:14.330 --> 01:11:17.400
So it's a self laundering machine.

01:11:17.410 --> 01:11:19.010
Like they decide who the
new universities are.

01:11:19.010 --> 01:11:21.730
Guess how many new universities get
accredited, each year to be able...

01:11:23.190 --> 01:11:23.550
Andrew Huberman: Zero.

01:11:23.550 --> 01:11:24.249
Marc Andreessen: Zero, right?

01:11:25.110 --> 01:11:29.330
And so as long as that system is in place,
and as long as they have the government

01:11:29.330 --> 01:11:32.899
wired the way that they do, and as
long as they control who gets access to

01:11:32.900 --> 01:11:36.270
federal student loan funding, of course
there's not going to be any competition.

01:11:36.650 --> 01:11:39.240
Of course there can't be a new institution
that's going to be able to get to scale.

01:11:39.790 --> 01:11:40.529
It's not, not possible.

01:11:40.529 --> 01:11:44.320
And so if you actually wanted to
create a new system that was better

01:11:44.320 --> 01:11:47.530
in, you know, I would argue dozens or
hundreds of ways, it could obviously be

01:11:47.530 --> 01:11:48.530
better if you were starting it today.

01:11:49.420 --> 01:11:52.580
It probably can't be done as long as the
existing institutions are actually intact.

01:11:53.340 --> 01:11:57.419
And this is my counter to Martin, which
is like, yeah, look, if we're going

01:11:57.420 --> 01:12:00.060
to tear down the old, there may be a
period of disruption before we get to

01:12:00.060 --> 01:12:02.730
the new, but we're never going to get to
the new if we don't tear down the old.

01:12:03.030 --> 01:12:04.660
Andrew Huberman: When you say counter
to Martin, you're talking about

01:12:04.660 --> 01:12:06.159
the author of Revolt of the Public

01:12:06.159 --> 01:12:06.496
?
 Marc Andreessen: Yeah, Martin Gurri.

01:12:06.496 --> 01:12:12.170
What Martin Gurri says is like, look,
he said basically as follows, the elites

01:12:12.170 --> 01:12:17.020
deserve contempt, but the only thing
worse than these elites that deserve

01:12:17.020 --> 01:12:18.380
contempt would be no elites at all.

01:12:20.120 --> 01:12:24.210
And he basically says on the other
side of the destruction of the elites

01:12:24.340 --> 01:12:25.690
and the institutions is nihilism.

01:12:26.250 --> 01:12:27.330
You're basically left with nothing.

01:12:27.330 --> 01:12:28.919
And by the way, there
is a nihilistic streak.

01:12:28.980 --> 01:12:31.490
I mean, there's a nihilistic streak
in the culture and the politics today.

01:12:31.490 --> 01:12:33.480
There are people who basically
would just say, yeah, just tear

01:12:33.660 --> 01:12:37.730
the whole system down without any
particular plan for what follows.

01:12:37.760 --> 01:12:41.650
And so I think he makes a good point
and that you want to be careful that you

01:12:41.650 --> 01:12:44.090
actually have a plan on the other side
that you think is actually achievable.

01:12:44.700 --> 01:12:48.059
But again, the counterargument
to that is if you're not willing

01:12:48.059 --> 01:12:50.199
to actually tear down the old,
you're not going to get to the new.

01:12:50.660 --> 01:12:53.230
Now, what's interesting, of
course, is this is what happens

01:12:53.230 --> 01:12:54.689
every day in business, right?

01:12:54.710 --> 01:12:58.809
So the entire way, how do you know
that the capitalist system works?

01:12:59.130 --> 01:13:02.620
The way that you know is that the old
companies, when they're no longer like

01:13:02.620 --> 01:13:06.219
the best at what they do, they get torn
down and then they ultimately die and

01:13:06.219 --> 01:13:07.500
they get replaced by better companies.

01:13:07.540 --> 01:13:09.179
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, I
haven't seen a Sears in a while.

01:13:09.240 --> 01:13:09.740
Marc Andreessen: Exactly.

01:13:10.650 --> 01:13:13.910
And we know what's so interesting
is we know in capitalism, in a

01:13:13.910 --> 01:13:18.130
market economy, we know that's the
sign of health, that's the sign of

01:13:18.130 --> 01:13:19.519
how the system is working properly.

01:13:19.519 --> 01:13:22.580
And in fact, we get actually
judged by antitrust authorities

01:13:22.590 --> 01:13:23.910
in the government on that basis.

01:13:24.559 --> 01:13:27.410
It's like the best defense against
antitrust charges is no, people

01:13:27.410 --> 01:13:29.605
are coming to kill us and they're
doing a really good job of it.

01:13:29.930 --> 01:13:31.150
That's how we know we're doing our job.

01:13:31.150 --> 01:13:33.950
And in fact, in business we are
specifically, it is specifically

01:13:33.950 --> 01:13:36.890
illegal for companies in the same
industry to get together and plot

01:13:36.890 --> 01:13:40.910
and conspire and plan and have things
like these accreditation bureaus.

01:13:40.929 --> 01:13:43.969
If I created the equivalent in my
companies of the kind of accreditation

01:13:43.969 --> 01:13:46.130
bureau that the universities have, I'd
get sent straight to federal prison

01:13:46.609 --> 01:13:47.880
and a trust violation Sherman Act.

01:13:47.880 --> 01:13:48.420
Straight to prison.

01:13:48.420 --> 01:13:49.930
People have been sent to prison for that.

01:13:50.420 --> 01:13:53.430
So in the business world, we
know that you want everything

01:13:53.430 --> 01:13:54.660
subject to market competition.

01:13:54.660 --> 01:13:56.510
We know that you want
creative destruction.

01:13:56.510 --> 01:13:59.420
We know that you want replacement
of the old with superior new.

01:13:59.889 --> 01:14:02.309
It's just once we get outside of business,
we're like, oh, we don't want any of that.

01:14:02.770 --> 01:14:08.250
We want basically stagnation and log
rolling and basically institutional

01:14:08.260 --> 01:14:11.440
incestuous, like entanglements
and conflicts of interest as

01:14:11.440 --> 01:14:14.099
far as the eye can see, and then
we're surprised by the results.

01:14:14.840 --> 01:14:17.620
Andrew Huberman: So let's play it
out as a bit of a thought experiment.

01:14:17.630 --> 01:14:24.180
So let's say that one small banding
together of people who want to start a new

01:14:24.180 --> 01:14:30.330
university where there is free exchange
of open ideas, where unless somebody has

01:14:30.680 --> 01:14:35.059
egregious behavior, violent behavior,
truly sexually inappropriate behavior

01:14:35.059 --> 01:14:38.559
against somebody that is committing
a crime, they're allowed to be there.

01:14:38.599 --> 01:14:42.519
They're allowed to be a student or
a faculty member or administrator.

01:14:42.940 --> 01:14:47.340
And let's just say this accreditation
bureau allowed student loans for

01:14:47.340 --> 01:14:48.530
this one particular university.

01:14:48.530 --> 01:14:51.360
Or let's say that there was an independent
source of funding for that university

01:14:51.440 --> 01:14:53.090
such that students could just apply there.

01:14:53.160 --> 01:14:57.960
They didn't need to be part of this
elite, accredited group, which sounds

01:14:57.960 --> 01:15:04.720
very mafia-like, frankly, not necessarily
violent, but certainly coercive in

01:15:04.720 --> 01:15:06.030
the way that it walls people out.

01:15:07.200 --> 01:15:12.410
Let's say that then there were
20 or 30 of those or 40 of those.

01:15:12.799 --> 01:15:17.729
Do you think that over time, that model
would overtake the existing model?

01:15:17.840 --> 01:15:19.370
Marc Andreessen: Isn't it
interesting that those don't exist?

01:15:21.730 --> 01:15:23.490
Remember Sherlock Holmes,
The Dog that Didn't Bark

01:15:24.800 --> 01:15:26.210
?
 Andrew Huberman: It is
interesting that they don't exist.

01:15:26.530 --> 01:15:28.049
Marc Andreessen: Right.
So there's two possibilities.

01:15:28.050 --> 01:15:30.370
One is like, nobody wants
that, which I don't believe.

01:15:31.240 --> 01:15:33.139
And then the other is like, the
system is wired in a way that

01:15:33.139 --> 01:15:34.199
will just simply not allow it.

01:15:34.879 --> 01:15:36.940
And you did a hypothetical in
which the system would allow it.

01:15:37.530 --> 01:15:39.840
And my response to that is, no, of
course the system won't allow that.

01:15:39.890 --> 01:15:43.170
Andrew Huberman: Or the people that band
together have enough money or get enough

01:15:43.170 --> 01:15:48.519
resources to say, look, we can afford to
give loans to 10,000 students per year.

01:15:48.519 --> 01:15:51.879
10,000 isn't a trivial number when
thinking about the size of a university.

01:15:51.880 --> 01:15:57.410
And most of them hopefully will graduate
in four years and there'll be a turnover.

01:15:58.780 --> 01:16:03.849
Do you think that the great future
innovators would tend to orient toward

01:16:03.849 --> 01:16:08.040
that model more than they currently
do toward the traditional model?

01:16:08.559 --> 01:16:11.699
What I'm trying to get back to here is
how do you think that the current model

01:16:11.730 --> 01:16:16.870
thwarts innovation, as well as maybe some
ways that it still supports innovation?

01:16:17.530 --> 01:16:21.639
Certainly cancellation and the risk of
cancellation from the way that we framed

01:16:21.639 --> 01:16:27.280
it earlier, is going to discourage risk
takers of the category of risk takers

01:16:27.280 --> 01:16:31.830
that take risk in every domain that
really like to fly close to the sun

01:16:31.830 --> 01:16:33.580
and sometimes into the sun or are--

01:16:33.580 --> 01:16:38.590
Marc Andreessen: --Doing research that
is just not politically palatable.

01:16:39.240 --> 01:16:42.890
Andrew Huberman: Right, that we can't
even talk about on this podcast, probably

01:16:42.890 --> 01:16:46.030
without causing a distraction of what
we're actually trying to talk about.

01:16:46.030 --> 01:16:47.170
Marc Andreessen: That gives
up the whole game right there.

01:16:47.170 --> 01:16:47.670
Exactly.

01:16:50.560 --> 01:16:53.770
Andrew Huberman: I keep a file, and
it's a written file because I'm afraid

01:16:53.770 --> 01:16:57.190
to put it into electronic form of all
the things that I'm afraid to talk

01:16:57.200 --> 01:17:01.260
about publicly because I come from a
lineage of advisors where all three died

01:17:01.260 --> 01:17:04.451
young, and I figure, if nothing else,
I'll die, and then I'll make it into

01:17:04.580 --> 01:17:10.490
the world and let's say 510 years, 20
years, and if not, I know a certainty

01:17:10.490 --> 01:17:13.420
I'm going to die at some point, and then
we'll see where all those issues stand.

01:17:13.740 --> 01:17:14.809
In any event--

01:17:14.809 --> 01:17:16.409
Marc Andreessen: --is that list
getting l onger over time or shorter?

01:17:16.540 --> 01:17:17.959
Andrew Huberman: Oh, it's
definitely getting longer.

01:17:17.969 --> 01:17:18.570
Marc Andreessen: Isn't that interesting?

01:17:18.620 --> 01:17:20.379
Andrew Huberman: Yeah,
it's getting much longer.

01:17:20.380 --> 01:17:24.840
I mean, there are just so many issues
that I would love to explore on this

01:17:24.859 --> 01:17:32.280
podcast with experts and that I can't
explore, just even if I had a panel

01:17:32.280 --> 01:17:35.969
of them, because of the way that
things get soundbited and segmented

01:17:35.969 --> 01:17:39.940
out and taken out of context, it's
like the whole conversation is lost.

01:17:39.940 --> 01:17:43.460
And so, unfortunately, there are an
immense number of equally interesting

01:17:43.480 --> 01:17:48.315
conversations that I'm excited to
have, but it is a little disturbing.

01:17:48.520 --> 01:17:49.750
Marc Andreessen: Do you
remember Lysenkoism?

01:17:51.490 --> 01:17:51.990
Andrew Huberman: No.

01:17:52.769 --> 01:17:54.559
Marc Andreessen: Famous in the
history of the Soviet Union.

01:17:54.590 --> 01:17:55.370
This is the famous thing.

01:17:55.370 --> 01:17:57.379
So there was a geneticist named Lysenko.

01:17:58.019 --> 01:18:00.389
Andrew Huberman: That's why it sounds
familiar, but I'm not calling to--

01:18:00.389 --> 01:18:05.510
Marc Andreessen: --Well, he was the guy
who did communist genetics, the field

01:18:05.510 --> 01:18:08.660
of genetics, the Soviets did not approve
of the field of genetics because, of

01:18:08.660 --> 01:18:11.490
course, they believed in the creation
of the new man and total equality,

01:18:11.490 --> 01:18:13.349
and genetics did not support that.

01:18:13.349 --> 01:18:17.109
And so if you were doing traditional
genetics, you were going to know, at

01:18:17.109 --> 01:18:18.750
the very least be fired, if not killed.

01:18:19.580 --> 01:18:22.610
And so this guy Lysenko stood up and said,
oh, I've got Marxist genetics, right?

01:18:22.610 --> 01:18:24.520
I've got, like a whole new
field of genetics that basically

01:18:24.520 --> 01:18:25.470
is politically compliant.

01:18:25.990 --> 01:18:27.990
And then they actually implemented
that in the agriculture

01:18:27.990 --> 01:18:29.130
system of the Soviet Union.

01:18:29.250 --> 01:18:31.830
And it's the origin of one of the
big reasons that the Soviet Union

01:18:31.830 --> 01:18:34.050
actually fell, which was they
ultimately couldn't feed themselves.

01:18:34.709 --> 01:18:37.920
Andrew Huberman: So create a new notion
of biology as it relates to genetics.

01:18:37.920 --> 01:18:39.190
Marc Andreessen: Politically
correct biology, right?

01:18:40.060 --> 01:18:43.450
They not only created it, they taught it,
they mandated it, they required it, and

01:18:43.450 --> 01:18:45.139
then they implemented it in agriculture.

01:18:45.580 --> 01:18:46.080
Andrew Huberman: Interesting.

01:18:47.360 --> 01:18:48.250
Marc Andreessen: I never understood.

01:18:48.259 --> 01:18:50.320
There was a bunch of things in
history I never understood until the

01:18:50.320 --> 01:18:51.349
last decade, and that's one of them.

01:18:51.880 --> 01:18:54.820
Andrew Huberman: Well, I censor myself
at the level of deleting certain things,

01:18:54.900 --> 01:18:57.490
but I don't contort what I do talk about.

01:18:57.590 --> 01:19:01.150
So I tend to like to play
on lush, open fields.

01:19:02.000 --> 01:19:03.272
Just makes my life a lot easier.

01:19:03.272 --> 01:19:04.170
Marc Andreessen: But this goes to the rot.

01:19:04.200 --> 01:19:06.550
This goes to the rot, and I'll come
back to your question, but this goes

01:19:06.550 --> 01:19:09.390
to the rot in the existing system,
which is, by the way, I'm no different.

01:19:09.390 --> 01:19:09.919
I'm just like you.

01:19:09.929 --> 01:19:11.939
Like, I'm trying not to
light myself on fire either.

01:19:12.279 --> 01:19:15.132
But the rot in the existing system,
and by system, I mean the institutions

01:19:15.132 --> 01:19:19.120
and the elites, the rot is that the set
of things that are no longer allowed.

01:19:19.120 --> 01:19:25.570
I mean, that list is obviously expanding
over time, and that's real, historically

01:19:25.570 --> 01:19:27.480
speaking, that doesn't end in good places.

01:19:27.879 --> 01:19:30.210
Andrew Huberman: Is this group
of a particular generation that

01:19:30.210 --> 01:19:32.620
we can look forward to the time
when they eventually die off.

01:19:33.160 --> 01:19:34.830
Marc Andreessen: It's a third of
the Boomers plus the Millennials.

01:19:35.010 --> 01:19:36.480
Andrew Huberman: So, got a while.

01:19:36.710 --> 01:19:37.400
Marc Andreessen: Good news, bad news.

01:19:38.170 --> 01:19:38.919
Gen X is weird, right?

01:19:38.919 --> 01:19:39.840
I'm Gen X.

01:19:39.840 --> 01:19:41.829
Gen X is weird because we
kind of slipped in the middle.

01:19:41.840 --> 01:19:42.702
We were kind of the, I don't
know how to describe it.

01:19:42.719 --> 01:19:48.230
We were the kind of non-political
generation kind of sandwiched between

01:19:48.230 --> 01:19:49.080
the Boomers and the Millennials.

01:19:49.720 --> 01:19:53.320
Gen Z is a very, I think, open
question right now which way they go.

01:19:53.340 --> 01:19:56.620
I could imagine them being
actually much more intense than the

01:19:56.970 --> 01:19:58.300
Millennials on all these issues.

01:19:58.300 --> 01:20:00.029
I could also imagine them
reacting to the Millennials

01:20:00.030 --> 01:20:01.139
and being far more open minded.

01:20:01.969 --> 01:20:03.029
Andrew Huberman: We don't know
which way it's going to go.

01:20:03.030 --> 01:20:03.799
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, it's going to go.

01:20:03.799 --> 01:20:05.009
It might be different groups of them.

01:20:05.400 --> 01:20:07.360
Andrew Huberman: I'm Gen
X also, I'm 47, you're...?

01:20:08.900 --> 01:20:09.330
Marc Andreessen: 52.

01:20:09.330 --> 01:20:11.730
Andrew Huberman: So I grew up with
some John Hughes films and so where the

01:20:12.140 --> 01:20:16.040
jocks and the hippies and the punks,
and were all divided and they were

01:20:16.040 --> 01:20:20.860
all segmented, but then it all sort of
mishmashed together a few years later.

01:20:21.820 --> 01:20:24.690
And I think that had a lot to do
with, like you said, the sort of

01:20:24.740 --> 01:20:27.830
apolitical aspect of our generation.

01:20:28.580 --> 01:20:30.310
Marc Andreessen: The Gen X just
knew the Boomers were nuts, right?

01:20:30.310 --> 01:20:37.439
Like, one of the great sitcoms of
the era was Family Ties , right?

01:20:37.440 --> 01:20:38.320
With the character Michael P.

01:20:38.320 --> 01:20:38.820
Keaton.

01:20:39.160 --> 01:20:41.450
And he was just like, this guy
is just like, yeah, my Boomer

01:20:41.450 --> 01:20:42.499
hippie parents are crazy.

01:20:42.730 --> 01:20:44.970
I'm just going to go into business
and actually do something productive.

01:20:45.250 --> 01:20:47.880
There was something iconic about
that character in our culture.

01:20:47.880 --> 01:20:50.670
And people like me were like, yeah,
obviously you go into business, you

01:20:50.670 --> 01:20:51.820
don't go into political activism.

01:20:51.880 --> 01:20:54.529
And then it's just like, man,
that came whipping back around

01:20:54.580 --> 01:20:55.440
with the next generation.

01:20:56.150 --> 01:20:57.730
So just to touch real quick
on the university thing.

01:20:57.730 --> 01:20:59.919
So, look, there are people trying to
do, and I'm actually going to do a

01:20:59.920 --> 01:21:03.500
thing this afternoon with the University
of Austin, which is one of these.

01:21:03.500 --> 01:21:05.500
And so there are people
trying to do new universities.

01:21:06.930 --> 01:21:08.190
Like, I would say it's certainly possible.

01:21:08.200 --> 01:21:08.929
I hope they succeed.

01:21:08.929 --> 01:21:09.630
I'm pulling for them.

01:21:09.660 --> 01:21:10.450
I think it'd be great.

01:21:10.450 --> 01:21:12.059
I think it'd be great if there
w ere a lot more of them.

01:21:12.150 --> 01:21:13.280
Andrew Huberman: Who
founded this university?

01:21:13.559 --> 01:21:14.910
Marc Andreessen: This is
a whole group of people.

01:21:14.929 --> 01:21:18.540
I don't want to freelance on that because
I don't know originally who the idea was--

01:21:18.540 --> 01:21:20.550
Andrew Huberman: --University
of Austin, not UT Austin.

01:21:20.590 --> 01:21:21.849
Marc Andreessen: Yeah.
So this is not UT Austin.

01:21:22.139 --> 01:21:23.519
It's called the University of Austin.

01:21:23.520 --> 01:21:24.000
Or they call it.

01:21:24.000 --> 01:21:28.059
I think it's UATX?

01:21:28.119 --> 01:21:31.189
And it's a lot of very sharp
people associated with it.

01:21:33.690 --> 01:21:35.678
They're going to try, very much
exactly like what you described.

01:21:35.678 --> 01:21:36.710
They're going to try to do a new one.

01:21:37.240 --> 01:21:39.710
I would just tell you the wall
of opposition that they're

01:21:39.710 --> 01:21:41.009
up against is profound.

01:21:41.150 --> 01:21:44.359
And part of it is economic,
which is can they ever get access

01:21:44.360 --> 01:21:45.340
to federal student lending?

01:21:45.340 --> 01:21:49.079
And I hope that they can, but it
seems nearly inconceivable the

01:21:49.080 --> 01:21:50.160
way the system is rigged today.

01:21:51.370 --> 01:21:54.619
And then the other is just like they
already have come under, I mean,

01:21:57.120 --> 01:21:59.740
anybody who publicly associates with
them who is in traditional academia

01:21:59.760 --> 01:22:02.579
immediately gets lit on fire, and
there's, you know, cancellation campaigns.

01:22:02.700 --> 01:22:04.540
So they're up against a
wall of social ostracism.

01:22:04.889 --> 01:22:05.389
Andrew Huberman: Wow.

01:22:05.570 --> 01:22:07.330
Marc Andreessen: They're up
against a wall of press attacks.

01:22:07.920 --> 01:22:11.760
They're up against a wall of people
just like doing the thing, pouncing on,

01:22:12.119 --> 01:22:14.520
anytime anybody says anything, they're
going to try to burn the place down.

01:22:14.710 --> 01:22:18.889
Andrew Huberman: This reminds me of
Jerry Springer episodes and Geraldo

01:22:18.890 --> 01:22:27.340
Rivera episodes where it's like if
a teen listened to Danzig or Marilyn

01:22:27.340 --> 01:22:31.360
Manson type music or Metallica, that
they were considered a devil worshiper.

01:22:32.370 --> 01:22:33.830
Now we just laugh, right?

01:22:33.830 --> 01:22:35.210
We're like, that's crazy, right?

01:22:35.210 --> 01:22:38.180
People listen to music with all
sorts of lyrics and ideas and looks.

01:22:39.170 --> 01:22:39.900
That's crazy.

01:22:40.800 --> 01:22:44.780
But there were people
legitimately sent to prison.

01:22:44.780 --> 01:22:46.370
I think it was a West
Memphis three, right?

01:22:46.370 --> 01:22:50.150
These kids out in West Memphis that
looked different, acted different,

01:22:50.150 --> 01:22:54.380
were accused of murders that eventually
was made clear they clearly didn't

01:22:54.390 --> 01:22:58.070
commit, but they were in prison
because of the music they listened to.

01:22:58.070 --> 01:22:59.899
I mean, this sounds very similar to that.

01:22:59.899 --> 01:23:02.530
And I remember seeing bumpersickers,
Free the West Memphis Three!

01:23:02.530 --> 01:23:04.690
And I thought this was some crazy thing.

01:23:04.690 --> 01:23:07.284
And you look into it and this
isn't, it's a little bit niche,

01:23:07.430 --> 01:23:09.889
but these are real lives.

01:23:09.890 --> 01:23:14.230
And there was an active witch
hunt for people that looked

01:23:14.380 --> 01:23:15.610
different and acted different.

01:23:15.610 --> 01:23:21.330
And yet now we're sort of in this inverted
world where on the one hand we're all

01:23:21.330 --> 01:23:24.090
told that we can express ourselves
however we want, but on the other

01:23:24.090 --> 01:23:27.339
hand, you can't get a bunch of people
together to take classes where they learn

01:23:27.340 --> 01:23:31.790
biology and sociology and econ in Texas.

01:23:32.900 --> 01:23:33.519
Wild.

01:23:33.630 --> 01:23:34.120
Marc Andreessen: Yes.

01:23:34.120 --> 01:23:37.620
Well, so the simple explanation
is this is Puritanism, right?

01:23:37.620 --> 01:23:41.439
So this is the original American
Puritanism that just works

01:23:41.459 --> 01:23:43.980
itself out through the system in
different ways at different times.

01:23:44.220 --> 01:23:46.930
There's a religious phenomenon in
America called the Great Awakenings.

01:23:48.270 --> 01:23:50.399
There will be these periods in
American history where there's

01:23:50.400 --> 01:23:52.670
basically religiosity fades and
then there will be this snapback

01:23:52.670 --> 01:23:56.639
effect where you'll have basically
this frenzy basically, of religion.

01:23:56.639 --> 01:23:59.760
In the old days, it would have been
tent revivals and people speaking

01:23:59.760 --> 01:24:01.150
in tongues and all this stuff.

01:24:01.810 --> 01:24:05.290
And then in the modern world, it's of the
form that we're living through right now.

01:24:05.930 --> 01:24:09.929
And so, yeah, it's just basically these
waves of sort of American religious, and

01:24:10.539 --> 01:24:14.160
remember, religion in our time, religious
impulses in our time don't get expressed

01:24:14.380 --> 01:24:16.190
because we live in more advanced times.

01:24:16.389 --> 01:24:17.769
We live in scientifically informed times.

01:24:17.769 --> 01:24:21.040
And so religious impulses in our time
don't show up as overtly religious.

01:24:21.760 --> 01:24:25.809
They show up in a secularized form,
which, of course, conveniently, is

01:24:25.810 --> 01:24:28.240
therefore not subject to the First
Amendment separation of church and state.

01:24:28.700 --> 01:24:31.150
As long as the church is
secular, there's no problem.

01:24:32.030 --> 01:24:34.440
But we're acting out these kind
of religious scripts over and over

01:24:34.440 --> 01:24:36.360
again, and we're in the middle
of another religious frenzy.

01:24:37.480 --> 01:24:41.769
Andrew Huberman: There's a phrase
