01:26:09.600 --> 01:26:12.860
No, look, pendulum swings back is
true only in the cases where the

01:26:12.860 --> 01:26:16.940
pendulum swings back, everybody just
conveniently forgets all the other

01:26:16.940 --> 01:26:18.190
circumstances where that doesn't happen.

01:26:18.410 --> 01:26:22.320
One of the things people, you see this
in business also, people have a really

01:26:22.330 --> 01:26:24.600
hard time confronting really bad news.

01:26:25.430 --> 01:26:26.480
I don't know if you've noticed that.

01:26:27.910 --> 01:26:30.110
I think every doctor who's listening
right now is like, yeah, no shit.

01:26:30.120 --> 01:26:35.719
But have you seen in business,
there are situations, that Star

01:26:35.719 --> 01:26:38.800
Trek , remember Star Trek ? The
Kobayashi Maru simulator, right?

01:26:38.800 --> 01:26:40.999
So the big lesson to become a Star Trek
captain is you had to go through the

01:26:41.000 --> 01:26:43.680
simulation called the Kobayashi Maru,
and the point was, there's no way to win.

01:26:43.700 --> 01:26:44.630
It's a no win scenario.

01:26:45.880 --> 01:26:48.020
And then it turned out like,
Captain Kirk was the only

01:26:48.020 --> 01:26:49.160
person to ever win the scenario.

01:26:49.160 --> 01:26:52.160
And the way that he did it was he went in
ahead of time and hacked the simulator.

01:26:53.010 --> 01:26:54.389
It was the only way to
actually get through.

01:26:54.389 --> 01:26:56.630
And then there was a debate whether
to fire him or make him a captain.

01:26:56.630 --> 01:26:57.439
So they made him a captain.

01:26:59.459 --> 01:27:02.200
You know, the problem is,
in real life, you do get the

01:27:02.200 --> 01:27:03.610
Kobayashi Maru on a regular basis.

01:27:03.610 --> 01:27:06.389
Like, there are actual no win situations
that you can't work your way out of.

01:27:07.049 --> 01:27:09.130
And as a leader, you can't
ever cop to that, right?

01:27:09.130 --> 01:27:11.089
Because you have to carry things
forward, and you have to look for

01:27:11.090 --> 01:27:12.500
every possible choice you can.

01:27:12.500 --> 01:27:14.610
But every once in a while, you
do run into a situation where

01:27:14.630 --> 01:27:15.269
it's really not recoverable.

01:27:15.619 --> 01:27:18.399
And at least I've found people
just cannot cope with that.

01:27:19.650 --> 01:27:22.530
What happens is they basically, then
they basically just exclude it from

01:27:22.530 --> 01:27:23.740
their memory that it ever happened.

01:27:25.390 --> 01:27:27.600
Andrew Huberman: I'm glad you brought up
simulators, because I want to make sure

01:27:27.600 --> 01:27:33.889
that we talk about the new and emerging
landscape of AI artificial intelligence.

01:27:34.830 --> 01:27:41.850
And I could try and smooth our
conversation of a moment ago with this

01:27:41.850 --> 01:27:47.980
one by creating some clever segue, but I'm
not going to, except I'm going to ask, is

01:27:47.980 --> 01:27:52.660
there a possibility that AI is going to
remedy some of what we're talking about?

01:27:53.289 --> 01:27:55.769
Let's make sure that we earmark that
for discussion a little bit later.

01:27:56.120 --> 01:27:59.690
But first off, because some of
the listeners of this podcast

01:27:59.690 --> 01:28:03.060
might not be as familiar with
AI as perhaps they should be.

01:28:03.060 --> 01:28:05.320
We've all heard about
artificial intelligence.

01:28:05.360 --> 01:28:07.440
People hear about machine learning, etc.

01:28:07.440 --> 01:28:11.069
But it'd be great if you could
define for us what AI is.

01:28:12.059 --> 01:28:17.300
People almost immediately hear AI
and think, okay, robots taking over.

01:28:17.410 --> 01:28:20.710
I'm going to wake up, and I'm going to
be strapped to the bed and my organs

01:28:20.710 --> 01:28:21.210
are going to be pulled out of me.

01:28:22.240 --> 01:28:24.120
The robots are going to
be in my bank account.

01:28:24.120 --> 01:28:30.059
They're going to kill all my
children and dystopia for most.

01:28:31.320 --> 01:28:37.020
Clearly, that's not the way it's going
to go if you believe that machines

01:28:37.020 --> 01:28:40.839
can augment human intelligence, and
human intelligence is a good thing.

01:28:41.099 --> 01:28:48.770
So tell us what AI is and where you
think it can take us, both good and bad.

01:28:48.780 --> 01:28:52.570
Marc Andreessen: So, there was a big
debate when the computer was first

01:28:52.570 --> 01:28:56.780
invented, which is in the 1930s,
1940s, people like Alan Turing and

01:28:56.780 --> 01:28:57.870
John von Neumann and these people.

01:28:58.360 --> 01:29:02.979
And the big debate at the time was because
they knew they wanted to build computers.

01:29:02.980 --> 01:29:06.870
They had the basic idea, and there had
been, like, calculating machines before

01:29:06.879 --> 01:29:10.180
that, and there had been these looms that
you basically programmed to punch cards.

01:29:10.180 --> 01:29:13.600
And so there was a prehistory to computers
that had to do with building sort of

01:29:13.600 --> 01:29:15.290
increasingly complex calculating machines.

01:29:15.610 --> 01:29:17.429
So they were kind of on a track,
but they knew they were going to

01:29:17.429 --> 01:29:19.910
be able to build, they called it a
general purpose computer that could

01:29:20.030 --> 01:29:22.460
basically, you could program, in the
way that you program computers today.

01:29:23.300 --> 01:29:25.760
But they had a big debate early on,
which is, should the fundamental

01:29:25.780 --> 01:29:30.430
architecture of the computer be based
on either A, like calculating machines,

01:29:30.460 --> 01:29:34.529
like cache registers and looms and
other things like that, or should it

01:29:34.550 --> 01:29:36.170
be based on a model of the human brain?

01:29:36.790 --> 01:29:39.480
And they actually had this idea
of computers modeled on the human

01:29:39.480 --> 01:29:43.120
brain back then, and this is this
concept of so called neural networks.

01:29:43.610 --> 01:29:46.570
And it's actually fairly astonishing
from a research standpoint.

01:29:46.580 --> 01:29:48.980
The original paper on neural networks
actually was published in 1943.

01:29:51.010 --> 01:29:53.349
So they didn't have our level of
neuroscience, but they actually knew

01:29:53.349 --> 01:29:56.130
about the neuron, and they actually
had a theory of neurons interconnecting

01:29:56.130 --> 01:29:59.359
and synapses and information
processing in the brain even back then.

01:30:00.330 --> 01:30:03.040
And a lot of people at the time
basically said, you know what?

01:30:03.040 --> 01:30:05.390
We should basically have the computer
from the start be modeled after the

01:30:05.390 --> 01:30:08.559
human brain, because if the computer
could do everything that the human

01:30:08.559 --> 01:30:11.160
brain can do, that would be the best
possible general purpose computer.

01:30:11.170 --> 01:30:13.649
And then you could have it do
jobs, and you could have it create

01:30:13.670 --> 01:30:15.620
art, and you could have it do all
kinds of things like humans can do.

01:30:16.850 --> 01:30:18.150
It turns out that didn't happen.

01:30:19.350 --> 01:30:22.849
In our world, what happened instead was
the industry went in the other direction.

01:30:22.850 --> 01:30:25.830
It went basically in the model of the
calculating machine or the cash register.

01:30:25.830 --> 01:30:28.870
And I think, practically speaking, that
kind of had to be the case, because

01:30:28.870 --> 01:30:32.120
that was actually the technology
that was practical at the time.

01:30:33.080 --> 01:30:37.400
But that's the path and so what we all
have experiences with, up to and including

01:30:37.400 --> 01:30:40.700
the iPhone in our pocket, is computers
built on that basically calculating

01:30:40.750 --> 01:30:42.280
machine model, not the human brain model.

01:30:42.830 --> 01:30:45.679
And so what that means is computers,
as we have come to understand

01:30:45.679 --> 01:30:49.830
them, they're basically like
mathematical savants at best.

01:30:50.120 --> 01:30:53.909
So they're really good at doing
lots of mathematical calculations.

01:30:54.260 --> 01:30:57.370
They're really good at executing these
extremely detailed computer programs.

01:30:57.700 --> 01:30:58.870
They're hyper literal.

01:30:59.510 --> 01:31:02.720
One of the things you learn early
when you're a programmer is, as the

01:31:02.720 --> 01:31:04.790
human programmer, you have to get
every single instruction you give

01:31:04.790 --> 01:31:07.669
the computer correct because it will
do exactly what you tell it to do.

01:31:08.280 --> 01:31:11.849
And bugs in computer programs are always
a mistake on the part of the programmer.

01:31:12.059 --> 01:31:12.450
Interesting.

01:31:12.450 --> 01:31:13.360
You never blame the computer.

01:31:13.360 --> 01:31:16.570
You always blame the programmer
because that's the nature of the

01:31:16.570 --> 01:31:17.500
thing that you're dealing with.

01:31:17.550 --> 01:31:19.510
Andrew Huberman: One downscore
off and the whole thing--

01:31:19.650 --> 01:31:20.267
Marc Andreessen: --Yeah, and
it's the programmer's fault.

01:31:20.267 --> 01:31:23.639
And if you talk to any programmer,
they'll agree with this.

01:31:23.639 --> 01:31:25.429
They'll be like, yeah, if
there's a problem, it's my fault.

01:31:25.469 --> 01:31:26.170
I did it.

01:31:26.230 --> 01:31:27.319
I can't blame the computer.

01:31:27.320 --> 01:31:28.489
The computer has no judgment.

01:31:28.520 --> 01:31:32.950
It has no ability to interpret,
synthesize, develop an independent

01:31:32.950 --> 01:31:33.990
understanding of anything.

01:31:33.990 --> 01:31:36.470
It's literally just doing what
I tell it to do step by step.

01:31:37.390 --> 01:31:40.309
So for 80 years we've had this,
just this very kind of hyper

01:31:40.309 --> 01:31:42.110
literal kind of model computers.

01:31:42.110 --> 01:31:45.539
Technically, these are what are called
von Neumann machines, based after

01:31:45.540 --> 01:31:46.820
the mathematician John von Neumann.

01:31:47.400 --> 01:31:50.330
They run in that way, and they've been
very successful and very important,

01:31:50.330 --> 01:31:51.710
and our world has been shaped by them.

01:31:52.160 --> 01:31:55.100
But there was always this other idea
out there, which is, okay, how about

01:31:55.100 --> 01:31:57.790
a completely different approach,
which is based much more on how the

01:31:57.790 --> 01:32:01.430
human brain operates, or at least
our kind of best understanding of

01:32:01.430 --> 01:32:02.829
how the human brain operates, right?

01:32:02.829 --> 01:32:04.019
Because those aren't the same thing.

01:32:05.059 --> 01:32:07.479
It basically says, okay, what
if you could have a computer

01:32:07.479 --> 01:32:08.480
instead of being hyper literal?

01:32:08.480 --> 01:32:13.110
What if you could have it actually
be conceptual and creative and

01:32:13.120 --> 01:32:16.940
able to synthesize information and
able to draw judgments and able

01:32:16.940 --> 01:32:23.939
to behave in ways that are not
deterministic but are rather creative?

01:32:26.030 --> 01:32:28.070
And the applications for
this, of course, are endless.

01:32:28.110 --> 01:32:33.080
And so, for example, the self-driving
car, the only way that you cannot

01:32:33.080 --> 01:32:35.510
program a computer with rules to
make it a self-driving car, you

01:32:35.510 --> 01:32:37.809
have to do what Tesla and Waymo and
these other companies have done.

01:32:37.809 --> 01:32:40.589
Now you have to use, right, you
have to use this other architecture,

01:32:41.199 --> 01:32:43.840
and you have to basically teach
them how to recognize objects in

01:32:43.840 --> 01:32:46.860
images at high speeds, basically
the same way the human brain does.

01:32:46.860 --> 01:32:49.270
And so those are so called
neural networks running inside.

01:32:49.450 --> 01:32:52.650
Andrew Huberman: So, essentially, let
the machine operate based on priors.

01:32:53.750 --> 01:32:59.269
We almost clipped a boulder going up
this particular drive, and so therefore,

01:32:59.270 --> 01:33:02.810
this shape that previously the machine
didn't recognize as a boulder, it now

01:33:03.170 --> 01:33:05.320
introduces to its catalog of boulders.

01:33:05.340 --> 01:33:07.110
Is that a good example?

01:33:07.380 --> 01:33:09.690
Marc Andreessen: Let's even make it
even starker for a self-driving car.

01:33:10.200 --> 01:33:11.279
There's something in the road.

01:33:11.300 --> 01:33:14.530
Is it a small child or a plastic
shopping bag being blown by the wind?

01:33:15.929 --> 01:33:17.470
Very important difference.

01:33:17.840 --> 01:33:20.690
If it's a shopping bag, you definitely
want to go straight through it, because

01:33:20.700 --> 01:33:24.960
if you deviate off course, you're
going to make a fast, it's the same

01:33:24.960 --> 01:33:25.889
challenge we have when we're driving.

01:33:26.010 --> 01:33:28.239
You don't want to swerve to avoid a
shopping bag because you might hit

01:33:28.309 --> 01:33:29.510
something that you didn't see on the side.

01:33:29.510 --> 01:33:31.920
But if it's a small child for
sure you want to swerve, right?

01:33:32.630 --> 01:33:37.150
But in that moment, small children come
in different shapes and descriptions and

01:33:37.150 --> 01:33:37.920
are wearing different kinds of clothes.

01:33:37.930 --> 01:33:40.590
Andrew Huberman: They might tumble onto
the road the same way a bag would tumble.

01:33:40.610 --> 01:33:41.810
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, they
might look like they're tumbling.

01:33:41.810 --> 01:33:44.520
And by the way, they might
be wearing a Halloween mask.

01:33:44.660 --> 01:33:45.160
Right.

01:33:45.380 --> 01:33:47.250
They might not have a
recognizable human face.

01:33:48.000 --> 01:33:50.299
It might be a kid with one leg.

01:33:50.849 --> 01:33:52.290
You definitely want to not hit those.

01:33:55.100 --> 01:33:58.690
This is what basically we figured out
is you can't apply the rules based

01:33:58.690 --> 01:34:02.100
approach of a Von Neumann machine to
basically real life and expect the

01:34:02.100 --> 01:34:05.580
computer to be in any way understanding
or resilient, to change to basically

01:34:05.580 --> 01:34:06.840
things happening in real life.

01:34:06.840 --> 01:34:09.040
And this is why there's always been
such a stark divide between what the

01:34:09.040 --> 01:34:11.179
machine can do and what the human can do.

01:34:12.159 --> 01:34:15.220
And so, basically, what's happened is
in the last decade, that second type

01:34:15.220 --> 01:34:18.170
of computer, the neural network based
computer, has started to actually work.

01:34:18.820 --> 01:34:21.910
It started to work, actually, first,
interestingly, in vision, recognizing

01:34:21.920 --> 01:34:24.179
objects and images, which is why the
self-driving car is starting to work.

01:34:24.480 --> 01:34:25.249
Andrew Huberman: Face recognition.

01:34:25.250 --> 01:34:25.850
Marc Andreessen: Face recognition.

01:34:25.850 --> 01:34:28.040
Andrew Huberman: I mean, when I
started off in visual neuroscience,

01:34:28.040 --> 01:34:33.110
which is really my original home in
neuroscience, the idea that a computer

01:34:33.540 --> 01:34:38.299
or a camera could do face recognition
better than a human was like a very

01:34:38.300 --> 01:34:42.670
low probability event based on the
technology we had at the time, based

01:34:42.670 --> 01:34:45.690
on the understanding of the face
recognition cells and the fusiform gyrus.

01:34:45.770 --> 01:34:51.350
Now, you would be smartest to put
all your money on the machine.

01:34:51.390 --> 01:34:55.960
You want to find faces in airports,
even with masks on and at profile

01:34:55.970 --> 01:35:00.639
versus straight on, machines can do
it far better than almost all people.

01:35:00.639 --> 01:35:01.470
I mean, they're the super recognizers.

01:35:02.429 --> 01:35:05.469
But even they can't
match the best machines.

01:35:05.629 --> 01:35:09.023
Now, ten years ago, what I just
said was the exact reverse, right?

01:35:09.023 --> 01:35:09.639
Marc Andreessen: That's right, yeah.

01:35:10.270 --> 01:35:15.230
So faces, handwriting, and
then voice, being able to

01:35:15.230 --> 01:35:17.680
understand voice just as a user.

01:35:17.680 --> 01:35:19.990
If you use Google Docs, it has
a built-in voice transcription.

01:35:19.990 --> 01:35:22.190
They have sort of the best industry
leading kind of voice transcription.

01:35:22.590 --> 01:35:25.250
If you use a voice transcription in
Google Docs, it's breathtakingly good.

01:35:25.430 --> 01:35:28.280
You just speak into it and it
just types what you're saying.

01:35:28.290 --> 01:35:29.830
Andrew Huberman: Well, that's good,
because in my phone, every once in

01:35:29.830 --> 01:35:32.170
a while, I'll say I need to go pick
up a f ew things and it'll say,

01:35:32.170 --> 01:35:33.620
I need to pick up a few thongs.

01:35:34.140 --> 01:35:37.290
And so Apple needs to get on board.

01:35:37.520 --> 01:35:39.729
Whatever the voice recognition
is that Google's using--

01:35:39.750 --> 01:35:41.090
Marc Andreessen: --Maybe it
knows you better than you think.

01:35:43.110 --> 01:35:45.690
Andrew Huberman: [LAUGHS] That was not
the topic I was avoiding discussing.

01:35:45.840 --> 01:35:46.170
Marc Andreessen: No.

01:35:46.170 --> 01:35:47.070
So that's on the list, right?

01:35:47.070 --> 01:35:47.570
That's on your...

01:35:50.139 --> 01:35:52.839
Actually, there's a reason, actually,
why Google's so good and Apple is

01:35:52.840 --> 01:35:54.089
not right now at that kind of thing.

01:35:54.090 --> 01:35:57.729
And it actually goes to actually an
ideological thing, of all things.

01:35:59.199 --> 01:36:03.650
Apple does not permit pooling of
data for any purpose, including

01:36:03.650 --> 01:36:05.620
training AI, whereas Google does.

01:36:06.400 --> 01:36:08.810
And Apple's just like, stake
their brand on privacy.

01:36:08.810 --> 01:36:11.330
And among that is sort of a pledge
that they don't pool your data.

01:36:12.080 --> 01:36:15.549
And so all of Apple's AI is like, AI
that has to happen locally on your phone.

01:36:16.070 --> 01:36:17.820
Whereas Google's AI can
happen in the cloud.

01:36:18.010 --> 01:36:19.079
Right?
It can happen across pool data.

01:36:19.120 --> 01:36:20.840
Now, by the way, some people
think that that's bad because

01:36:20.930 --> 01:36:21.990
they think pooling data is bad.

01:36:22.370 --> 01:36:25.160
But that's an example of the shift that's
happening in the industry right now,

01:36:25.160 --> 01:36:28.580
which is you have this separation between
the people who are embracing the new

01:36:28.580 --> 01:36:31.849
way of training AIs and the people who
basically, for whatever reason, are not.

01:36:32.620 --> 01:36:34.669
Andrew Huberman: Excuse me, you
say that some people think it's

01:36:34.670 --> 01:36:37.070
bad because of privacy issues or
they think it's bad because of the

01:36:37.920 --> 01:36:39.810
reduced functionality of that AI.

01:36:40.150 --> 01:36:41.770
Marc Andreessen: Oh, no.
So you're definitely going to get...

01:36:43.420 --> 01:36:45.370
there's three reasons
AIs have started to work.

01:36:45.380 --> 01:36:48.930
One of them is just simply larger
data sets, larger amounts of data.

01:36:49.790 --> 01:36:53.380
Specifically, the reason why objects
and images are now, the reason

01:36:53.380 --> 01:36:55.710
machines are now better than humans
at recognizing objects, images or

01:36:55.710 --> 01:37:00.520
recognizing faces is because modern
facial recognition AIs are trained across

01:37:00.710 --> 01:37:02.139
all photos on the Internet of people.

01:37:02.800 --> 01:37:04.630
Billions and billions and
billions of photos, right?

01:37:04.690 --> 01:37:06.279
Unlimited number of photos
of people on the Internet.

01:37:06.939 --> 01:37:09.020
Attempts to train facial
recognition systems.

01:37:09.020 --> 01:37:12.710
Ten or 20 years ago, they'd be trained on
thousands or tens of thousands of photos.

01:37:13.010 --> 01:37:15.472
Andrew Huberman: So the input
data is simply much m ore vast

01:37:15.570 --> 01:37:16.099
.
 Marc Andreessen: Much larger.

01:37:16.100 --> 01:37:17.960
This is the reason to get
to the conclusion on this.

01:37:17.960 --> 01:37:19.990
This is the reason why
ChatGPT works so well.

01:37:21.080 --> 01:37:23.309
One of the reasons ChatGPT
works so well is it's trained

01:37:23.309 --> 01:37:24.670
on the entire Internet of text.

01:37:25.240 --> 01:37:27.950
And the entire Internet of text was
not something that was available for

01:37:27.950 --> 01:37:31.170
you to train an AI on until it came
to actually exist itself, which is

01:37:31.170 --> 01:37:32.769
new in the last, basically decade.

01:37:33.010 --> 01:37:35.650
Andrew Huberman: So in the case of
face recognition, I could see how

01:37:35.650 --> 01:37:39.390
having a much larger input data set
would be beneficial if the goal is

01:37:39.390 --> 01:37:43.090
to recognize Marc Andreessen's face,
because you are looking for signal to

01:37:43.090 --> 01:37:45.090
noise against everything else, right?

01:37:45.370 --> 01:37:50.640
But in the case of ChatGPT, when you're
pooling all text on the internet and you

01:37:50.700 --> 01:37:56.460
ask ChatGPT to, say, construct a paragraph
about Marc Andreessen's prediction of

01:37:56.460 --> 01:38:02.940
the future of human beings over the
next ten years and the likely to be most

01:38:02.940 --> 01:38:05.509
successful industries, give ChatGPT that.

01:38:06.080 --> 01:38:10.680
If it's pooling across all
text, how does it know what is

01:38:10.680 --> 01:38:12.730
authentically Marc Andreessen's text?

01:38:12.910 --> 01:38:18.670
Because in the case of face recognition,
you've got a standard to work from a

01:38:18.679 --> 01:38:21.189
verified image versus everything else.

01:38:22.309 --> 01:38:26.310
In the case of text, you have to make
sure that what you're starting with is

01:38:26.320 --> 01:38:30.960
verified text from your mouth, which
makes sense if it's coming from video.

01:38:30.960 --> 01:38:36.090
But then if that video is deep
faked, all of a sudden, what's true?

01:38:36.259 --> 01:38:41.119
Your valid Marc Andreessen is in question.

01:38:41.170 --> 01:38:45.110
And then everything ChatGPT is
producing, that is then of question.

01:38:45.830 --> 01:38:48.010
Marc Andreessen: So I would say
there's a before and after thing here.

01:38:48.010 --> 01:38:52.550
There's like a before ChatGPT and after
GPT question, because the existence

01:38:52.550 --> 01:38:55.050
of GPT itself changes the answer.

01:38:55.050 --> 01:38:56.670
So before ChatGPT.

01:38:56.670 --> 01:38:59.759
So the version you're using today is
trained on data up till September 2021.

01:39:00.850 --> 01:39:02.080
They're cut off with the training set.

01:39:02.090 --> 01:39:06.030
Up till September 2021, almost all text on
the Internet was written by a human being.

01:39:07.150 --> 01:39:09.429
And then most of that was written
by people under their own names.

01:39:09.510 --> 01:39:11.259
Some of it wasn't, but a lot of it was.

01:39:11.660 --> 01:39:13.709
And why do you know it's for me is
because it was published in a magazine

01:39:13.710 --> 01:39:16.530
under my name, or it's a podcast
transcript and it's under my name.

01:39:16.879 --> 01:39:19.720
And generally speaking, if you just
did a search on what are things Marc

01:39:19.720 --> 01:39:23.530
Andreessen has written and said,
90% plus of that would be correct,

01:39:23.800 --> 01:39:27.390
and somebody might have written a
fake parody article or something.

01:39:27.390 --> 01:39:27.810
Like that.

01:39:27.810 --> 01:39:30.839
But not that many people were
spending that much time writing fake

01:39:30.840 --> 01:39:32.040
articles about things that I said.

01:39:32.150 --> 01:39:33.750
Andrew Huberman: Right now, so
many people can pretend to be you.

01:39:34.000 --> 01:39:34.710
Marc Andreessen: Exactly right.

01:39:34.710 --> 01:39:37.089
And so, generally speaking, you
can kind of get your arms around

01:39:37.090 --> 01:39:39.309
the idea that there's a corpus
of material associated with me.

01:39:39.310 --> 01:39:40.280
Or by the way, same thing with you.

01:39:40.280 --> 01:39:43.230
There's a corpus of YouTube transcripts
and other, your academic papers

01:39:43.230 --> 01:39:45.490
and talks you've given, and you can
kind of get your hands around that.

01:39:45.490 --> 01:39:46.849
And that's how these systems are trained.

01:39:46.860 --> 01:39:48.939
They take all that data
collectively, they put it in there.

01:39:48.940 --> 01:39:50.910
And that's why this
works as well as it does.

01:39:50.960 --> 01:39:56.016
And that's why if you ask ChatGPT to
speak or write like me or like you or like

01:39:56.070 --> 01:39:59.900
somebody else, it will actually generally
do a really good job because it has all

01:39:59.900 --> 01:40:01.850
of our prior text in its training data.

01:40:03.590 --> 01:40:05.150
That said, from here on
out, this gets harder.

01:40:05.340 --> 01:40:07.706
And of course, the reason this gets
harder is because now we have AI that

01:40:07.849 --> 01:40:11.890
can create text and we have AI that
can create text at industrial scale.

01:40:12.850 --> 01:40:14.490
Andrew Huberman: Is it
watermarked as AI generated text?

01:40:14.510 --> 01:40:14.620
Marc Andreessen: No.

01:40:14.620 --> 01:40:16.195
Andrew Huberman: How hard
would it be to do that?

01:40:16.260 --> 01:40:17.080
Marc Andreessen: I think it's impossible.

01:40:17.750 --> 01:40:18.629
I think it's impossible.

01:40:18.630 --> 01:40:20.020
There are people who
are trying to do that.

01:40:20.330 --> 01:40:21.710
This is a hot topic in the classroom.

01:40:21.710 --> 01:40:24.450
I was just talking to a friend who's got
like a 14 year old kid in a class, and

01:40:24.450 --> 01:40:25.670
there's like these recurring scandals.

01:40:25.679 --> 01:40:29.730
Every kid in the class is using ChatGPT to
write their essays or to help them write

01:40:29.730 --> 01:40:35.240
their essays, and then the teacher is
using one of, there's a tool that you can

01:40:35.240 --> 01:40:39.540
use that purports to be able to tell you
whether something was written by ChatGPT.

01:40:39.929 --> 01:40:42.090
But it's like, only right
like 60% of the time.

01:40:42.530 --> 01:40:45.500
And so there was this case where the
student wrote an essay where their

01:40:45.500 --> 01:40:48.850
parent sat and watched them write the
essay, and then they submitted it, and

01:40:48.950 --> 01:40:50.700
this tool got the conclusion incorrect.

01:40:50.700 --> 01:40:53.240
And then the student feels outraged
because he got unfairly cheated.

01:40:53.240 --> 01:40:55.040
But the teacher is like, well,
you're all using the tool.

01:40:55.220 --> 01:40:57.620
Then it turns out there's another
tool that basically you feed in

01:40:57.620 --> 01:40:58.090
text, and they call it a summarizer.

01:40:58.090 --> 01:41:04.860
But what it really is is it's a
cheating mechanism to basically

01:41:05.289 --> 01:41:08.010
just shuffle the words around
enough so that it sheds whatever

01:41:08.170 --> 01:41:09.400
characteristics were associated with AI.

01:41:09.940 --> 01:41:12.860
So, there's like an arms race going
on in educational settings right

01:41:12.860 --> 01:41:14.090
now around this exact question.

01:41:14.540 --> 01:41:16.409
I don't think it's possible to do.

01:41:16.420 --> 01:41:17.383
There are people working
on the watermarking.

01:41:17.383 --> 01:41:18.787
I don't think it's possible
to do the watermarking.

01:41:18.787 --> 01:41:21.639
And I think it's just kind of obvious why
it's not possible to do that, which is

01:41:21.650 --> 01:41:23.510
you can just read the output for yourself.

01:41:24.379 --> 01:41:25.700
It's really good.

01:41:25.710 --> 01:41:28.429
How are you actually going to tell
the difference between that and

01:41:28.430 --> 01:41:29.690
something that a real person wrote?

01:41:29.690 --> 01:41:32.290
And then, by the way, you
can also ask ChatGPT to write

01:41:32.290 --> 01:41:33.550
in different styles, right?

01:41:33.559 --> 01:41:36.450
So you can tell it, like, write
in the style of a 15 year old.

01:41:37.120 --> 01:41:39.959
You can tell it to write in the style
of a non native English speaker.

01:41:40.369 --> 01:41:42.080
Or if you're a non native English
speaker, you can tell it to

01:41:42.080 --> 01:41:44.780
write in the style of an English
speaker, native English speaker.

01:41:45.030 --> 01:41:47.669
And so the tool itself
will help you evade.

01:41:48.080 --> 01:41:51.720
I think there's a lot of
people who are going to want to

01:41:51.720 --> 01:41:54.109
distinguish, "real" versus fake.

01:41:55.170 --> 01:41:56.059
I think those days are over.

01:41:56.090 --> 01:41:56.450
Andrew Huberman: Genie's
out of the bottle.

01:41:56.450 --> 01:41:56.722
Marc Andreessen: Genie is
completely out of the bottle.

01:41:56.722 --> 01:42:00.140
And by the way, I actually
think this is good.

01:42:00.230 --> 01:42:03.280
This doesn't map to my worldview
of how we use this technology

01:42:03.290 --> 01:42:04.800
anyway, which we can come back to.

01:42:06.480 --> 01:42:10.059
So there's that, and then there's
the problem, therefore of the

01:42:10.059 --> 01:42:11.099
so-called deep fake problem.

01:42:11.130 --> 01:42:13.889
So then there's the problem of, like,
deliberate basically, manipulation.

01:42:14.299 --> 01:42:19.170
And that's like one of your many
enemies, one of your increasingly

01:42:19.570 --> 01:42:24.139
long list of enemies like mine,
who basically is like, wow, I know

01:42:24.140 --> 01:42:25.250
how I'm going to get him, right?

01:42:25.280 --> 01:42:29.370
I'm going to use it to create
something that looks like a Huberman

01:42:29.380 --> 01:42:31.190
transcript and I'm going to have
him say all these bad things.

01:42:31.810 --> 01:42:32.280
Andrew Huberman: Or a video.

01:42:32.280 --> 01:42:32.848
Marc Andreessen: Or a video, or a video.

01:42:32.929 --> 01:42:35.679
Andrew Huberman: I mean, Joe Rogan
and I were deep faked in a video.

01:42:36.219 --> 01:42:40.080
I don't want to flag people to it, so I
won't talk about what it was about, but

01:42:41.440 --> 01:42:45.799
where it, for all the world looked like
a conversation that we were having and

01:42:45.800 --> 01:42:47.910
we never had that specific conversation.

01:42:47.940 --> 01:42:48.440
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, that's right.

01:42:48.630 --> 01:42:49.570
So that's going to happen for sure.

01:42:50.340 --> 01:42:52.189
So what there's going to need to
be is there need to be basically

01:42:52.199 --> 01:42:58.180
registries where basically in your
case, you will submit your legitimate

01:42:58.610 --> 01:43:02.490
content into a registry under your
unique cryptographic key, right.

01:43:02.490 --> 01:43:05.100
And then basically there will be a
way to check against that registry to

01:43:05.100 --> 01:43:06.179
see whether that was the real thing.

01:43:06.179 --> 01:43:07.959
And I think this needs
to be done for sure.

01:43:07.969 --> 01:43:09.839
For public figures, it needs
to be done for politicians,

01:43:09.839 --> 01:43:11.400
it needs to be done for music.

01:43:11.680 --> 01:43:14.280
Andrew Huberman: What about taking what's
already out there and being able to

01:43:14.280 --> 01:43:18.800
authenticate it or not in the same way
that many times per week, I get asked,

01:43:18.830 --> 01:43:22.559
is this your account about a direct
message that somebody got on Instagram?

01:43:22.559 --> 01:43:26.100
And I always tell them, look,
I only have the one account,

01:43:26.589 --> 01:43:28.219
this one verified account.

01:43:28.219 --> 01:43:32.400
Although now, with the advent of
pay to play, verification makes it

01:43:32.440 --> 01:43:36.520
a little less potent as a security
blanket for knowing if it's not

01:43:36.520 --> 01:43:38.929
this account, then it's not me.

01:43:38.929 --> 01:43:42.599
But in any case, these accounts pop
up all the time pretending to be me.

01:43:42.599 --> 01:43:46.049
And I'm relatively low on the scale.

01:43:47.209 --> 01:43:49.770
Not low, but relatively low on
the scale to say, like a Beyonce

01:43:51.430 --> 01:43:54.209
or something like that, who has
hundreds of millions of followers.

01:43:54.210 --> 01:43:59.270
So is there a system in mind
where people could go in and

01:43:59.280 --> 01:44:01.342
verify text, click yes or no.

01:44:01.342 --> 01:44:01.900
This is me.

01:44:01.900 --> 01:44:02.460
This is not me.

01:44:02.460 --> 01:44:06.600
And even there, there's the opportunity
for people to fudge, to eliminate things

01:44:06.600 --> 01:44:09.269
about themselves that they don't want
out there, by saying, no, that's not me.

01:44:10.080 --> 01:44:11.190
I didn't actually say that.

01:44:11.190 --> 01:44:11.679
Or create that.

01:44:11.679 --> 01:44:12.290
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, no, that's right.

01:44:13.130 --> 01:44:14.760
Technologically, it's actually
pretty straightforward.

01:44:14.780 --> 01:44:17.030
So the way to implement this
technologically is with a public key.

01:44:17.200 --> 01:44:19.619
It's called public key cryptography,
which is the basis for how cryptography

01:44:20.339 --> 01:44:22.230
information is secured in the world today.

01:44:22.230 --> 01:44:26.000
And so basically, the implementation form
of this would be, you would pick whatever

01:44:26.000 --> 01:44:28.420
is your most trusted channel, and let's
say it's your YouTube channel as an

01:44:28.420 --> 01:44:31.270
example, where just everybody just knows
that it's you on your YouTube channel

01:44:31.320 --> 01:44:33.529
because you've been doing it for ten
years or whatever, and it's just obvious.

01:44:34.059 --> 01:44:36.600
And you would just publish in
the about me page on YouTube, you

01:44:36.600 --> 01:44:40.250
would just publish your public
cryptographic key that's unique to you.

01:44:40.670 --> 01:44:40.940
Right.

01:44:40.940 --> 01:44:43.690
And then anytime anybody wants
to check to see whether any piece

01:44:43.690 --> 01:44:47.270
of content is actually you, they
go to a registry in the cloud

01:44:47.270 --> 01:44:48.849
somewhere, and they basically submit.

01:44:48.880 --> 01:44:50.479
They basically say, okay, is this him?

01:44:50.910 --> 01:44:54.239
And then they can basically see
whether somebody with your public

01:44:54.240 --> 01:44:57.429
key, you had actually certified that
this was something that you made.

01:44:58.359 --> 01:45:00.430
Now, who runs that registry
is an interesting question.

01:45:01.090 --> 01:45:04.200
If that registry is run by the government,
we will call that the Ministry of Truth.

01:45:04.650 --> 01:45:05.890
I think that's probably a bad idea.

01:45:05.900 --> 01:45:10.610
If that registry is run by a company,
we would call that basically the

01:45:10.610 --> 01:45:12.510
equivalent of, like, a credit
bureau or something like that.

01:45:12.900 --> 01:45:13.869
Maybe that's how it happens.

01:45:13.870 --> 01:45:16.909
The problem with that is that company
now becomes hacking target number one,

01:45:16.980 --> 01:45:18.580
right, of every bad person on Earth.

01:45:18.830 --> 01:45:22.120
Because if anybody breaks
into that company, they can

01:45:22.120 --> 01:45:22.850
fake all kinds of things.

01:45:23.010 --> 01:45:24.040
Andrew Huberman: They own the truth.

01:45:24.090 --> 01:45:24.840
Marc Andreessen: Right.
They own the truth.

01:45:24.840 --> 01:45:27.580
And by the way, insider threat, also,
their employees can monkey with it.

01:45:27.580 --> 01:45:29.039
So you have to really trust that company.

01:45:29.610 --> 01:45:31.039
The third way to do it
is with a blockchain.

01:45:31.480 --> 01:45:33.999
And so this, with the crypto
blockchain technology, you could have

01:45:34.000 --> 01:45:37.460
a distributed system, basically, a
distributed database in the cloud

01:45:37.469 --> 01:45:38.535
that is run through a blockchain.

01:45:38.850 --> 01:45:42.549
And then it implements this cryptography
and this certification process.

01:45:42.590 --> 01:45:43.810
Andrew Huberman: What
about quantum Internet?

01:45:44.360 --> 01:45:45.880
Is that another way to
encrypt these things?

01:45:45.880 --> 01:45:48.103
I know most of our listeners are
probably not familiar with quantum

01:45:48.103 --> 01:45:52.950
Internet, but put simply, it's a way to
secure communications on the Internet.

01:45:53.250 --> 01:45:54.169
Let's just leave it at that.

01:45:54.610 --> 01:45:57.530
It's sophisticated, and we'll probably do
a whole episode about this at some point.

01:45:57.530 --> 01:46:00.070
But maybe you have a succinct way
of describing quantum Internet,

01:46:00.070 --> 01:46:02.820
but that would be better.

01:46:02.820 --> 01:46:04.519
And if so, please offer it up.

01:46:04.530 --> 01:46:07.720
But is quantum Internet going
to be one way to secure these

01:46:07.720 --> 01:46:09.849
kinds of data and resources?

01:46:10.230 --> 01:46:12.459
Marc Andreessen: Maybe in the
future, years in the future?

01:46:12.460 --> 01:46:15.040
We don't yet have working quantum
computers in practice, so it's

01:46:15.040 --> 01:46:17.950
not currently something you could
do, but maybe in a decade or two?

01:46:18.750 --> 01:46:19.250
Andrew Huberman: Tell me.

01:46:19.250 --> 01:46:21.630
I'm going to take a stab at defining
quantum Internet in one sentence.

01:46:21.630 --> 01:46:24.860
It's a way in which if anyone were to
try and peer in on a conversation on the

01:46:24.860 --> 01:46:30.980
Internet, it essentially would be futile
because of the way that quantum Internet

01:46:31.860 --> 01:46:36.019
changes the way that the communication is
happening so fast and so many times in any

01:46:36.020 --> 01:46:40.135
one conversation, essentially changing the
translation or the language so fast that

01:46:40.160 --> 01:46:41.450
there's just no way to keep up with it.

01:46:41.480 --> 01:46:42.659
Is that more or less accurate?

01:46:42.759 --> 01:46:45.690
Marc Andreessen: Yeah,
conceivably not yet, but someday.

01:46:45.809 --> 01:46:48.755
Andrew Huberman: So, going
back to AI, most people who

01:46:48.755 --> 01:46:52.400
hear about AI are afraid of AI.

01:46:52.420 --> 01:46:52.780
Marc Andreessen: Well?

01:46:52.780 --> 01:46:54.620
Andrew Huberman: I think most
people who aren't informed--

01:46:54.620 --> 01:46:57.059
Marc Andreessen: --This goes back
to our elites versus masses thing.

01:46:57.130 --> 01:46:57.819
Andrew Huberman: Oh, interesting.

01:46:57.819 --> 01:47:04.889
Well, I heard you say that, a his is from
a really wonderful tweet thread that we

01:47:04.889 --> 01:47:09.480
will link in the show note captions that
you put out not long ago and that I've

01:47:09.480 --> 01:47:13.750
read now several times, and that everyone
really should take the time to read it.

01:47:13.750 --> 01:47:17.330
Probably takes about 20 minutes to
read it carefully and to think about

01:47:17.330 --> 01:47:19.240
each piece, and I highly recommend it.

01:47:19.759 --> 01:47:27.010
But you said, and I'm quoting
here, "Let's address the fifth, the

01:47:27.010 --> 01:47:30.440
one thing I actually agree with,
which is AI will make it easier

01:47:30.440 --> 01:47:32.140
for bad people to do bad things."

01:47:36.809 --> 01:47:38.739
Marc Andreessen: First of all, there is
a general freak out happening around AI.

01:47:38.809 --> 01:47:41.519
I think it's primarily, it's one of these,
again, it's an elite driven freak out.

01:47:41.519 --> 01:47:44.290
I don't think the man in the street knows,
cares, or feels one way or the other.

01:47:44.290 --> 01:47:47.139
It's just not a relevant concept, and it
probably just sounds like science fiction.

01:47:47.530 --> 01:47:51.680
So I think there's an elite driven
freak out that's happening right now.

01:47:52.030 --> 01:47:55.339
I think that elite driven freak out
has many aspects to it that I think

01:47:55.340 --> 01:47:58.009
are incorrect, which is not surprising.

01:47:58.010 --> 01:47:58.690
I would think that, given that.

01:47:58.690 --> 01:48:00.933
I think the elites are incorrect
about a lot of things, but I think

01:48:00.960 --> 01:48:03.860
they're very wrong about a number
of things they're saying about AI.

01:48:03.860 --> 01:48:06.980
But that said, look, this is a very
powerful new technology, right?

01:48:06.980 --> 01:48:10.110
This is like a new general
purpose thinking technology.

01:48:10.110 --> 01:48:12.099
So what if machines could think?

01:48:12.099 --> 01:48:15.110
And what if you could use machines
that think, and what if you

01:48:15.110 --> 01:48:16.190
could have them think for you?

01:48:16.330 --> 01:48:18.730
There's obviously a lot of
good that could come from that.

01:48:19.190 --> 01:48:23.270
But also, people, look, criminals
could use them to plan better crimes.

01:48:24.290 --> 01:48:26.490
Terrorists could use them to plan
better terror attacks and so forth.

01:48:26.490 --> 01:48:29.359
And so these are going to be
tools that bad people can use

01:48:29.370 --> 01:48:30.339
to do bad things, for sure.

01:48:31.509 --> 01:48:33.100
Andrew Huberman: I can think
of some ways that AI could be

01:48:33.589 --> 01:48:35.179
leveraged to do fantastic things.

01:48:35.180 --> 01:48:44.780
Like in the realm of medicine, an AI
pathologist perhaps, can scan 10,000

01:48:44.780 --> 01:48:51.130
slides of histology and find the one
micro tumor, cellular aberration, that

01:48:51.380 --> 01:48:56.480
would turn into a full blown tumor,
whereas the even mildly fatigued or

01:48:56.480 --> 01:49:01.310
well rested human pathologists, as
great as they come, might miss that.

01:49:01.920 --> 01:49:05.580
And perhaps the best solution is
for both of them to do it, and then

01:49:05.580 --> 01:49:08.973
for the human to verify what the
AI has found and vice versa, right?

01:49:08.973 --> 01:49:09.629
Marc Andreessen: That's right.

01:49:09.629 --> 01:49:10.950
Andrew Huberman: And
that's just one example.

01:49:10.950 --> 01:49:14.960
I mean, I can come up with thousands of
examples where this would be wonderful.

01:49:16.200 --> 01:49:17.269
Marc Andreessen: I'll give you
another one, by the way, medicine.

01:49:17.280 --> 01:49:20.130
So you're talking about an analytic
result, which is good and important.

01:49:20.139 --> 01:49:22.639
The other is like, the machines are going
to be much better at bedside manner.

01:49:24.040 --> 01:49:25.610
They're going to be much better
at dealing with the patient.

01:49:26.130 --> 01:49:27.570
And we already know there's
already been a study.

01:49:27.570 --> 01:49:28.484
There's already been a study on this.

01:49:28.559 --> 01:49:33.530
So there was already a study done on
this where there was a study team that

01:49:33.530 --> 01:49:36.550
scraped thousands of medical questions
off of an Internet forum, and then they

01:49:36.550 --> 01:49:40.090
had real doctors answer the questions,
and then they had basically GPT4 answer

01:49:40.090 --> 01:49:43.619
the questions, and then they had another
panel of doctors score the responses.

01:49:44.509 --> 01:49:46.409
So there were no patients
experimented on here.

01:49:46.410 --> 01:49:49.069
This was a test contained
within the medical world.

01:49:51.350 --> 01:49:54.059
The judges, the panel of doctors
who are the judges, scored the

01:49:54.060 --> 01:49:57.900
answers in both factual accuracy
and on bedside manner, on empathy.

01:49:58.540 --> 01:50:04.200
And the GPT4 was equal or better
on most of the factual questions

01:50:04.240 --> 01:50:09.219
analytically, already, and it's not even
a specifically trained medical AI, but

01:50:09.240 --> 01:50:11.250
it was overwhelmingly better on empathy.

01:50:12.110 --> 01:50:12.610
Andrew Huberman: Amazing,

01:50:12.610 --> 01:50:13.110
Marc Andreessen: Right?

01:50:15.929 --> 01:50:18.434
Do you treat patients
directly in your work?

01:50:18.630 --> 01:50:19.130
You don't?

01:50:19.269 --> 01:50:19.769
Andrew Huberman: No, I don't.

01:50:19.830 --> 01:50:21.299
We run clinical trials.

01:50:21.360 --> 01:50:21.860
Marc Andreessen: Right.

01:50:22.190 --> 01:50:24.389
Andrew Huberman: But I don't
do any direct clinical work.

01:50:25.680 --> 01:50:26.710
Marc Andreessen: I've no
direct experience with this.

01:50:26.710 --> 01:50:30.250
But from the surgeons, if you talk
to surgeons or you talk to people who

01:50:30.250 --> 01:50:33.229
train surgeons, what they'll tell you
is surgeons need to have an emotional

01:50:33.230 --> 01:50:35.960
remove from their patients in order
to do a good job with the surgery.

01:50:35.960 --> 01:50:38.809
The side effect of that, and by the way,
look, it's a hell of a job to have to

01:50:38.809 --> 01:50:41.405
go in and tell somebody that they're
going to die or that they have so you're

01:50:41.539 --> 01:50:43.720
never going to recover, they're never
going to walk again or whatever it is.

01:50:43.720 --> 01:50:46.910
And so there's sort of something
inherent in that job where they need

01:50:46.990 --> 01:50:50.600
to keep an emotional reserve from
the patient to be able to do the job.

01:50:50.600 --> 01:50:52.249
And it's expected of
them as professionals.

01:50:53.170 --> 01:50:54.759
The machine has no such limitation.

01:50:55.469 --> 01:50:58.319
The machine can be as sympathetic
as you want it to be for as

01:50:58.320 --> 01:50:59.240
long as you want it to be.

01:50:59.309 --> 01:51:00.469
It can be infinitely sympathetic.

01:51:00.469 --> 01:51:02.010
It's happy to talk to you
at four in the morning.

01:51:02.010 --> 01:51:03.160
It's happy to sympathize with you.

01:51:03.490 --> 01:51:06.700
And by the way, it's not just
sympathizing with you in the way

01:51:06.700 --> 01:51:09.980
that, oh, it's just making up words
to lie to you to make you feel good.

01:51:10.389 --> 01:51:12.960
It can also sympathize with you in
terms of helping you through all

01:51:12.960 --> 01:51:15.259
the things that you can actually
do to improve your situation.

01:51:15.610 --> 01:51:20.500
And so, boy, can you keep a
patient actually on track with

01:51:20.500 --> 01:51:21.570
a physical therapy program.

01:51:21.570 --> 01:51:23.809
Can you keep a patient on track
with a nutritional program?

01:51:23.810 --> 01:51:25.690
Can you keep a patient
off of drugs or alcohol?

01:51:26.370 --> 01:51:29.599
And if they have a machine medical
companion that's with them all the

01:51:29.610 --> 01:51:31.950
time that they're talking to all
the time, that's infinitely patient,

01:51:32.400 --> 01:51:37.090
infinitely wise, infinitely loving,
and it's just going to be there all the

01:51:37.090 --> 01:51:39.120
time and it's going to be encouraging
and it's going to be, you know, you

01:51:39.120 --> 01:51:41.470
did such a great job yesterday, I
know you can do this again today.

01:51:42.050 --> 01:51:44.520
Cognitive behavioral therapy
is an obvious fit here.

01:51:44.530 --> 01:51:47.220
These things are going to be great
at CBT and that's already starting.

01:51:47.360 --> 01:51:50.940
You can already use ChatGPT as
a CBT therapist if you want.

01:51:50.940 --> 01:51:51.799
It's actually quite good at it.

01:51:53.330 --> 01:51:55.760
There's, there's a universe here
that's, it goes to what you said,

01:51:55.810 --> 01:51:58.990
there's a universe here that's opening
up, which is what I believe is it's

01:51:58.990 --> 01:52:00.470
partnership between man and machine.

01:52:01.320 --> 01:52:04.220
It's a symbiotic relationship,
not an adversarial relationship.

01:52:04.230 --> 01:52:07.850
And so the doctor is going to pair
with the AI to do all the things

01:52:07.850 --> 01:52:10.910
that you described, but the patient
is also going to pair with the AI.

01:52:11.129 --> 01:52:15.530
And I think this partnership that's
going to emerge is going to lead,

01:52:15.530 --> 01:52:17.429
among other things, to actually
much better health outcomes.

01:52:18.560 --> 01:52:23.429
Andrew Huberman: I've relied for so much
of my life on excellent mentors from a

01:52:23.820 --> 01:52:29.610
very young age, and still now, in order
to make the best decisions possible with

01:52:29.610 --> 01:52:34.450
the information I had, and rarely were
they available at four in the morning

01:52:34.520 --> 01:52:36.820
sometimes, but not on a frequent basis.

01:52:36.820 --> 01:52:41.820
And they fatigue like anybody else, and
they have their own stuff like anybody

01:52:41.820 --> 01:52:44.379
else, baggage, events in their life, etc.

01:52:45.509 --> 01:52:50.440
What you're describing is a sort of
AI coach or therapist of sorts, that

01:52:50.450 --> 01:52:56.340
hopefully would learn to identify our best
self and encourage us to be our best self.

01:52:56.340 --> 01:52:59.879
And when I say best self, I don't mean
that in any kind of pop psychology way.

01:52:59.900 --> 01:53:04.680
I could imagine AI very easily knowing
how well I slept the night before and

01:53:04.680 --> 01:53:08.940
what types of good or bad decisions I tend
to make at 02:00 in the afternoon when

01:53:08.940 --> 01:53:13.050
I've only had 5 hours of sleep, or maybe
just less REM sleep the night before.

01:53:13.050 --> 01:53:16.069
It might encourage me to take a little
more time to think about something.

01:53:16.620 --> 01:53:19.810
Might give me a little tap on the
wrist through a device that no one else

01:53:19.810 --> 01:53:23.130
would detect to refrain from something.

01:53:23.500 --> 01:53:24.330
Marc Andreessen: Never going to judge you.

01:53:24.900 --> 01:53:25.980
It's never going to be resentful.

01:53:26.120 --> 01:53:27.730
It's never going to be upset
that you didn't listen to it.

01:53:28.930 --> 01:53:30.200
It's never going to go on vacation.

01:53:31.000 --> 01:53:31.790
It's going to be there for you.

01:53:31.990 --> 01:53:34.000
I think this is the way
people are going to live.

01:53:34.000 --> 01:53:35.969
It's going to start with kids, and
then over time it's going to be adults.

01:53:35.969 --> 01:53:37.760
I think the way people are going
to live is they're going to have

01:53:38.429 --> 01:53:43.239
a friend, therapist, companion,
mentor, coach, teacher, assistant.

01:53:43.250 --> 01:53:46.420
Or, by the way, maybe multiple of those.

01:53:46.560 --> 01:53:48.519
It may be that we're actually talking
about six, like, different personas

01:53:48.520 --> 01:53:51.590
interacting, which is a whole 'nother
possibility, but they're going to have--

01:53:51.950 --> 01:53:52.310
Andrew Huberman: --A committee!

01:53:52.310 --> 01:53:53.650
Marc Andreessen: A
committee, yeah, exactly.

01:53:53.830 --> 01:53:54.509
Actually different personas.

01:53:54.509 --> 01:53:56.679
And maybe, by the way, when there are
difficult decisions to be made in your

01:53:56.679 --> 01:54:00.720
life, maybe what you want to hear is the
argument among the different personas.

01:54:00.730 --> 01:54:04.550
And so you're just going to grow up,
you're just going to have this in

01:54:04.550 --> 01:54:07.420
your life and you're going to always
be able to talk to it and always

01:54:07.420 --> 01:54:11.470
be able to learn from it and always
be able to help it make, it's going

01:54:11.470 --> 01:54:13.690
to be a symbiotic relationship.

01:54:14.020 --> 01:54:15.150
I think it's going to be
a much better way to live.

01:54:15.150 --> 01:54:16.259
I think people are going
to get a lot out of it.

01:54:16.740 --> 01:54:18.650
Andrew Huberman: What
modalities will it include?

01:54:18.660 --> 01:54:23.670
So I can imagine my phone has
this engine in it, this AI

01:54:23.710 --> 01:54:27.630
companion, and I'm listening in
headphones as I walk into work.

01:54:27.630 --> 01:54:31.349
And it's giving me some, not just
encouragement, some warnings, some

01:54:31.969 --> 01:54:35.199
thoughts that things that I might
ask Marc Andreessen today that I

01:54:35.199 --> 01:54:37.170
might not have thought of and so on.

01:54:37.730 --> 01:54:40.500
I could also imagine it
having a more human form.

01:54:40.730 --> 01:54:44.690
I could imagine it being tactile,
having some haptic, so tapping to

01:54:44.690 --> 01:54:47.540
remind me so that it's not going
to enter our conversation in a way

01:54:47.540 --> 01:54:50.140
that interferes or distracts you.

01:54:50.140 --> 01:54:50.830
But I would be aware.

01:54:50.860 --> 01:54:51.429
Oh, right.

01:54:52.379 --> 01:54:53.420
Things of that sort.

01:54:53.459 --> 01:54:57.910
I mean, how many different modalities
are we going to allow these AI

01:54:57.910 --> 01:54:59.720
coaches to approach us with?

01:54:59.810 --> 01:55:02.880
And is anyone actually thinking
about the hardware piece right now?

01:55:03.190 --> 01:55:04.980
Because I'm hearing a lot
about the software piece.

01:55:05.380 --> 01:55:06.910
What does the hardware piece look like?

01:55:07.090 --> 01:55:09.439
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, so this is where
Silicon Valley is going to kick in.

01:55:09.440 --> 01:55:12.349
So the entrepreneurial community is
going to try all of those, right?

01:55:12.620 --> 01:55:15.000
By the way, the big companies and
startups are going to try all those.

01:55:15.000 --> 01:55:18.330
And so obviously there's big
companies that are working, the big

01:55:18.340 --> 01:55:20.519
companies that have talked about a
variety of these, including heads

01:55:20.520 --> 01:55:23.269
up displays, AR, VR kinds of things.

01:55:23.889 --> 01:55:25.339
There's lots of people doing voice.

01:55:25.339 --> 01:55:27.550
Thing is, voice is a real possibility.

01:55:27.550 --> 01:55:29.089
It may just be an earpiece.

01:55:30.010 --> 01:55:35.210
There's a new startup that just unveiled
a new thing where they actually project.

01:55:35.220 --> 01:55:37.910
So you'll have like a pendant you wear
on like a necklace, and it actually

01:55:38.059 --> 01:55:41.589
projects, literally, it'll project
images on your hand or on the table

01:55:41.590 --> 01:55:42.459
or on the wall in front of you.

01:55:42.459 --> 01:55:43.929
So maybe that's how it shows up.

01:55:44.910 --> 01:55:45.099
Yeah.

01:55:45.099 --> 01:55:48.790
There are people working on so-called
haptic or touch based kinds of things.

01:55:48.820 --> 01:55:51.110
There are people working on
actually picking up nerve

01:55:51.110 --> 01:55:52.710
signals, like out of your arm.

01:55:57.670 --> 01:56:00.400
There's some science for being able
to do basically like subvocalization.

01:56:01.360 --> 01:56:07.440
So maybe you could pick up
that way by bone conduction.

01:56:07.450 --> 01:56:08.650
These are all going to be tried.

01:56:08.650 --> 01:56:11.630
So that's one question is the physical
form of it, and then the other

01:56:11.630 --> 01:56:14.140
question is the software version of
it, which is like, okay, what's the

01:56:14.140 --> 01:56:18.639
level of abstraction that you want to
deal with these things in right now?

01:56:18.639 --> 01:56:21.889
It's like a question answer paradigm, so
called chatbot, like, ask a question, get

01:56:21.889 --> 01:56:23.049
an answer, ask a question, get an answer.

01:56:23.699 --> 01:56:26.400
Well, you want that to go for sure
to more of a fluid conversation.

01:56:26.400 --> 01:56:28.365
You want it to build up more
knowledge of who you are, and

01:56:28.420 --> 01:56:30.830
you don't want to have to explain
yourself a second time and so forth.

01:56:30.830 --> 01:56:33.290
And then you want to be able to tell
it things like, well, remind me this,

01:56:33.290 --> 01:56:35.160
that, or be sure and tell me when X.

01:56:36.160 --> 01:56:39.229
But then maybe over time, more and
more, you want it actually deciding

01:56:39.230 --> 01:56:40.800
when it's going to talk to you, right?

01:56:40.800 --> 01:56:42.844
And when it thinks it has
something to say, it says it,

01:56:42.900 --> 01:56:43.980
and otherwise it stays silent.

01:56:45.170 --> 01:56:48.130
Andrew Huberman: Normally, at
least in my head, unless I make a

01:56:48.130 --> 01:56:51.729
concerted effort to do otherwise, I
don't think in complete sentences.

01:56:51.740 --> 01:57:01.679
So presumably these machines could learn
my style of fragmented internal dialogue.

01:57:02.039 --> 01:57:06.570
And maybe I have an earpiece, and
I'm walking in and I start hearing

01:57:06.570 --> 01:57:08.379
something, but it's some advice,
etc, encouragement, discouragement.

01:57:08.379 --> 01:57:17.859
But at some point, those sounds
that I hear in an earphone are very

01:57:17.859 --> 01:57:20.140
different than seeing something
or hearing something in the room.

01:57:20.150 --> 01:57:23.910
We know this based on the
neuroscience of musical perception

01:57:23.920 --> 01:57:24.720
and language perception.

01:57:24.730 --> 01:57:27.250
Hearing something in your
head is very different.

01:57:27.740 --> 01:57:31.820
And I could imagine at some point that
the AI will cross a precipice where if

01:57:31.820 --> 01:57:36.570
it has inline wiring to actually control
neural activity in specific brain areas,

01:57:36.590 --> 01:57:40.240
and I don't mean very precisely, even
just stimulating a little more prefrontal

01:57:40.240 --> 01:57:42.860
cortical activity, for instance, through
the earpiece, a little ultrasound wave

01:57:42.880 --> 01:57:46.389
now can stimulate prefrontal cortex
in a non invasive way that's being

01:57:46.389 --> 01:57:52.190
used clinically and experimentally,
that the AI could decide that I need

01:57:52.190 --> 01:57:55.200
to be a little bit more context aware.

01:57:56.350 --> 01:57:59.720
This is something that is very beneficial
for those listening that are trying to

01:57:59.720 --> 01:58:01.219
figure out how to navigate through life.

01:58:01.220 --> 01:58:04.600
It's like, you know, the context you're
in and know the catalog of behaviors

01:58:04.600 --> 01:58:10.040
and words that are appropriate for that
situation and not, you know, this would

01:58:10.040 --> 01:58:13.460
go along with agreeableness, perhaps,
but strategic agreeableness, right.

01:58:13.790 --> 01:58:14.769
Context is important.

01:58:15.780 --> 01:58:17.040
There's nothing diabolical about that.

01:58:17.040 --> 01:58:20.260
Context is important, but I could
imagine the AI recognizing we're

01:58:20.270 --> 01:58:22.130
entering a particular environment.

01:58:22.730 --> 01:58:26.029
I'm now actually going to ramp up activity
in prefrontal cortex a little bit in a

01:58:26.030 --> 01:58:30.620
certain way that allows you to be more
situationally aware of yourself and

01:58:30.620 --> 01:58:36.800
others, which is great, unless I can't
necessarily short circuit that influence,

01:58:37.190 --> 01:58:42.540
because at some point, the AI is actually
then controlling my brain activity

01:58:42.540 --> 01:58:44.059
and my decision making and my speech.

01:58:44.360 --> 01:58:48.619
I think that's what people fear is that
once we cross that precipice that we

01:58:48.620 --> 01:58:52.349
are giving up control to the artificial
versions of our human intelligence.

01:58:52.780 --> 01:58:55.280
Marc Andreessen: And look, I think
we have to decide, we collectively,

01:58:55.280 --> 01:58:57.700
and we as individuals, I think, have
to decide exactly how to do that.

01:58:57.700 --> 01:58:59.423
And this is the big thing
that I believe about AI.

01:58:59.423 --> 01:59:01.800
That's just a much more, I would
say, practical view of the world than

01:59:01.800 --> 01:59:03.359
a lot of the panic that you hear.

01:59:03.369 --> 01:59:04.380
It's just like, these are machines.

01:59:05.209 --> 01:59:07.330
They're able to do things that
increasingly are like the things that

01:59:07.370 --> 01:59:08.660
people can do in some circumstances.

01:59:08.660 --> 01:59:09.360
But these are machines.

01:59:09.360 --> 01:59:11.480
We built a machine, means we
decide how to use the machines.

01:59:12.130 --> 01:59:13.850
When we want the machines turned
on, they're turned on, we want them

01:59:13.850 --> 01:59:14.730
turned off, they're turned off.

01:59:15.710 --> 01:59:18.330
I think that's absolutely the kind
of thing that the individual person

01:59:18.330 --> 01:59:19.930
should always be in charge of.

01:59:19.960 --> 01:59:20.880
Andrew Huberman: Everyone was.

01:59:20.880 --> 01:59:24.400
And I have to imagine some people are
still afraid of CRISPR, of gene editing.

01:59:25.130 --> 01:59:28.500
But gene editing stands to revolutionize
our treatment of all sorts of disease,

01:59:29.480 --> 01:59:32.820
you know, inserting and deleting
particular genes in adulthood.

01:59:32.830 --> 01:59:35.200
Not having to recombine in the womb.

01:59:35.200 --> 01:59:38.240
A new organism is an
immensely powerful tool.

01:59:38.820 --> 01:59:42.969
And yet the Chinese scientist who
did CRISPR on humans, this has been

01:59:42.969 --> 01:59:46.809
done, actually did his postdoc at
Stanford with Steve Quake, then

01:59:46.810 --> 01:59:49.340
went to China, did CRISPR on babies.

01:59:49.340 --> 01:59:50.620
Mutated something.

01:59:50.690 --> 01:59:53.460
I believe it was one of the HIV receptors.

01:59:53.830 --> 01:59:57.379
I'm told it was with the intention
of augmenting human memory.

01:59:57.710 --> 02:00:01.285
It had very little to do, in fact,
with limiting susceptibility to

02:00:01.285 --> 02:00:05.220
HIV per se, to do with the way that
receptor is involved in human memory.

02:00:06.700 --> 02:00:09.910
The world demonized that person.

02:00:10.000 --> 02:00:11.409
We actually don't know
what happened to them.

02:00:11.410 --> 02:00:14.310
Whether or not they have a laboratory now
or they're sitting in jail, it's unclear.

02:00:14.589 --> 02:00:18.590
But in China and elsewhere,
people are doing CRISPR on humans.

02:00:18.610 --> 02:00:19.549
We know this.

02:00:20.000 --> 02:00:24.879
It's not legal in the US and other
countries, but it's happening.

02:00:27.020 --> 02:00:30.990
Do you think it's a mistake for us to fear
these technologies so much that we back

02:00:30.990 --> 02:00:35.130
away from them and end up 10, 20 years
behind other countries that could use it

02:00:35.130 --> 02:00:38.460
for both benevolent or malevolent reasons?

02:00:38.610 --> 02:00:41.360
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, the details matter.

02:00:41.360 --> 02:00:42.970
So it's technology by technology.

02:00:42.970 --> 02:00:45.559
But I would say there's two things
you always have to think about in

02:00:45.559 --> 02:00:48.300
these questions, I think, in terms of
counterfactuals and opportunity cost.

02:00:48.300 --> 02:00:51.440
CRISPR is an interesting one.

02:00:51.450 --> 02:00:52.949
CRISPR manipulates the human genome.

02:00:53.279 --> 02:00:58.286
Nature manipulates the human,
like, in all kinds of ways.

02:00:58.286 --> 02:00:58.599
[LAUGHS]
Andrew Huberman: Yeah.

02:00:58.599 --> 02:00:58.602
[LAUGHS]

02:00:58.602 --> 02:00:59.546
Marc Andreessen: When you
pick a spouse and you--

02:00:59.546 --> 02:01:00.630
Andrew Huberman: --Have a
child with that spouse--

02:01:00.640 --> 02:01:01.140
Marc Andreessen: --Oh, boy--

02:01:01.380 --> 02:01:03.000
Andrew Huberman: --You're
doing genetic recombination.

02:01:03.000 --> 02:01:03.500
Marc Andreessen: Yes, you are.

02:01:04.059 --> 02:01:07.000
Quite possibly, if you're Genghis
Khan, you're determining the future

02:01:07.000 --> 02:01:12.899
of humanity by those mutations.

02:01:12.909 --> 02:01:17.930
This is the old question of,
basically, this is all state of

02:01:17.930 --> 02:01:19.430
nature, state of grace, basically.

02:01:20.000 --> 02:01:20.710
Is nature good?

02:01:20.710 --> 02:01:23.960
And then therefore, artificial things
are bad, which is kind of shot.

02:01:23.969 --> 02:01:25.870
A lot of people have
ethical views like that.

02:01:25.900 --> 02:01:29.600
I'm always of the view that nature
is a bitch and wants us dead.

02:01:31.080 --> 02:01:32.240
Nature is out to get us, man.

02:01:32.240 --> 02:01:32.310
Nature wants to kill us, right?

02:01:32.310 --> 02:01:36.800
Like, nature wants to evolve
all kinds of horrible viruses.

02:01:36.800 --> 02:01:37.870
Nature wants plagues.

02:01:37.920 --> 02:01:39.470
Nature wants to do weather.

02:01:40.540 --> 02:01:41.660
Nature wants to do all kinds of stuff.

02:01:42.030 --> 02:01:44.709
I mean, look, nature religion
was the original religion, right?

02:01:44.710 --> 02:01:46.209
Like, that was the original
thing people worshiped.

02:01:46.600 --> 02:01:50.060
And the reason was because nature was the
thing that was out to get you right before

02:01:50.060 --> 02:01:53.640
you had scientific and technological
methods to be able to deal with it.

02:01:54.750 --> 02:01:57.849
So, the idea of not doing these
things, to me is just saying, oh,

02:01:57.849 --> 02:02:00.120
we're just going to turn over the
future of everything to nature.

02:02:00.120 --> 02:02:03.019
And I think that there's no reason
to believe that that leads in a

02:02:03.020 --> 02:02:07.230
particularly good direction or that
that's not a value neutral decision.

02:02:08.400 --> 02:02:11.090
And then the related thing that comes
from that is always this question around

02:02:11.100 --> 02:02:14.570
what's called the precautionary principle,
which shows up in all these conversations

02:02:14.570 --> 02:02:19.100
on things like CRISPR, which basically is
this principle that basically says, the

02:02:19.100 --> 02:02:21.969
inventors of a new technology should be
required to prove that it will not have

02:02:21.969 --> 02:02:23.510
negative effects before they roll it out.

02:02:24.690 --> 02:02:26.809
This, of course, is a very new idea.

02:02:26.809 --> 02:02:28.549
This is actually a new idea in the 1970s.

02:02:28.549 --> 02:02:30.580
It's actually invented
by the German Greens.

02:02:30.600 --> 02:02:31.690
The 1970s.

02:02:31.700 --> 02:02:33.700
Before that, people didn't
think in those terms.

02:02:33.750 --> 02:02:36.650
People just invented
things and rolled them out.

02:02:36.650 --> 02:02:39.420
And we got all of modern
civilization by people inventing

02:02:39.429 --> 02:02:40.859
things and rolling them out.

02:02:41.789 --> 02:02:43.590
The German Greens came up with
the precautionary principle

02:02:43.590 --> 02:02:44.770
for one specific purpose.

02:02:44.770 --> 02:02:45.830
I'll bet you can guess what it is.

02:02:47.620 --> 02:02:48.290
It was to prevent...?

02:02:49.490 --> 02:02:49.960
Andrew Huberman: Famine?

02:02:49.960 --> 02:02:50.550
Marc Andreessen: Nuclear power.

02:02:51.420 --> 02:02:53.709
It was to shut down attempts
to do civilian nuclear power.

02:02:54.580 --> 02:02:58.379
And if you fast forward 50 years later,
you're like, wow, that was a big mistake.

02:02:59.159 --> 02:03:01.740
So what they said at the time was,
you have to prove that nuclear

02:03:01.740 --> 02:03:04.230
reactors are not going to melt down
and cause all kinds of problems.

02:03:04.230 --> 02:03:06.760
And, of course, as an engineer, can
you prove that will never happen?

02:03:07.030 --> 02:03:07.530
You can't.

02:03:07.690 --> 02:03:10.059
You can't rule out things that
might happen in the future.

02:03:10.910 --> 02:03:15.270
And so that philosophy was used to
stop nuclear power by the way, not

02:03:15.270 --> 02:03:18.570
just in Europe, but also in the US and
around much of the rest of the world.

02:03:18.590 --> 02:03:21.099
If you're somebody who's concerned
about carbon emissions, of course, this

02:03:21.100 --> 02:03:23.840
is the worst thing that happened in
the last 50 years in terms of energy.

02:03:24.180 --> 02:03:27.329
We actually have the silver bullet
answer to unlimited energy with zero

02:03:27.330 --> 02:03:28.980
carbon emissions, nuclear power.

02:03:28.980 --> 02:03:30.640
We choose not to do it.

02:03:30.640 --> 02:03:32.919
Not only do we choose not to do it,
we're actually shutting down the

02:03:32.920 --> 02:03:35.439
plants that we have now in California.

02:03:35.459 --> 02:03:37.230
We just shut down the big plant.

02:03:37.510 --> 02:03:39.100
Germany just shut down their plants.

02:03:39.110 --> 02:03:42.549
Germany is in the middle of an energy
war with Russia that, we are informed,

02:03:42.550 --> 02:03:44.139
is existential for the future of Europe.

02:03:44.240 --> 02:03:48.039
Andrew Huberman: But unless the risk
of nuclear power plant meltdown has

02:03:48.039 --> 02:03:52.139
increased, and I have to imagine
it's gone the other way, what is

02:03:52.139 --> 02:03:54.910
the rationale behind shutting down
these plants and not expanding?

02:03:54.910 --> 02:03:55.800
Marc Andreessen: Because nuclear is bad.

02:03:55.960 --> 02:03:56.710
Right.
Nuclear is icky.

02:03:57.900 --> 02:03:58.930
Nuclear has been tagged.

02:03:58.930 --> 02:03:59.950
Andrew Huberman: It just sounds bad.

02:04:00.340 --> 02:04:00.840
Nuclear.

02:04:00.840 --> 02:04:01.340
Marc Andreessen: Yeah.

02:04:02.570 --> 02:04:02.980
Andrew Huberman: Go nuclear.

02:04:02.980 --> 02:04:03.647
Marc Andreessen: Well, so what happened?

02:04:03.647 --> 02:04:06.059
Andrew Huberman: We didn't shut down
postal offices and you hear go postal.

02:04:06.070 --> 02:04:08.829
Marc Andreessen: So what happened
was, so nuclear technology arrived

02:04:08.849 --> 02:04:10.490
on planet Earth as a weapon, right?

02:04:10.500 --> 02:04:11.889
So it arrived in the form of.

02:04:11.889 --> 02:04:13.730
The first thing they did was
in the middle of World War II.

02:04:13.920 --> 02:04:16.000
The first thing they did was the
atomic bomb they dropped on Japan.

02:04:16.260 --> 02:04:18.040
And then there were all the
debates that followed around

02:04:18.040 --> 02:04:19.559
nuclear weapons and disarmament.

02:04:19.560 --> 02:04:21.360
And there's a whole conversation
to be had, by the way, about

02:04:21.420 --> 02:04:23.610
that, because there's different
views you could have on that.

02:04:24.500 --> 02:04:25.190
And then it was in the.

02:04:25.880 --> 02:04:27.479
Where they started to roll
out civilian nuclear power.

02:04:27.480 --> 02:04:29.249
And then there were accidents.

02:04:29.250 --> 02:04:33.140
There was like, three Mile island
melted down, and then Chernobyl melted

02:04:33.160 --> 02:04:36.170
down in the Soviet Union, and then
even recently, Fukushima melted down.

02:04:36.650 --> 02:04:37.490
And so there have been meltdowns.

02:04:37.960 --> 02:04:40.400
And so I think it was a
combination of it's a weapon.

02:04:40.410 --> 02:04:47.690
It is sort of icky scientists
sometimes with the ick factor, right.

02:04:47.719 --> 02:04:48.530
It glows green.

02:04:48.530 --> 02:04:53.330
And by the way, it becomes like
a mythical fictional thing.

02:04:53.330 --> 02:04:56.209
And so you have all these movies of
horrible supervillains powered by

02:04:56.209 --> 02:04:58.039
nuclear energy and all this stuff.

02:04:58.070 --> 02:04:59.710
Andrew Huberman: Well, the
intro to the Simpsons, right.

02:04:59.710 --> 02:05:04.989
Is the nuclear power plant and the
three eyed fish and all the negative

02:05:04.990 --> 02:05:09.690
implications of this nuclear power plant
run by, at least in the Simpsons idiots.

02:05:10.080 --> 02:05:17.269
And that is the dystopia, where
people are unaware of just how bad it.

02:05:17.270 --> 02:05:18.830
Marc Andreessen: Is and who
owns the nuclear power plant.

02:05:18.860 --> 02:05:21.419
Right.
This evil capitalist.

02:05:21.420 --> 02:05:21.920
Right.

02:05:21.960 --> 02:05:23.890
So it's connected to capitalism.

02:05:23.920 --> 02:05:24.420
Right.

02:05:25.080 --> 02:05:27.670
Andrew Huberman: We're blaming Matt
Gronig for the demise of a particular--

02:05:28.190 --> 02:05:29.380
Marc Andreessen: --He
certainly didn't help.

02:05:31.430 --> 02:05:33.960
But it's literally, this amazing thing
where if you're just like, thinking.

02:05:33.960 --> 02:05:36.300
If you're just thinking rationally,
scientifically, you're like, okay,

02:05:36.389 --> 02:05:37.329
we want to get rid of carbon.

02:05:37.349 --> 02:05:38.480
This is the obvious way to do it.

02:05:38.960 --> 02:05:39.629
Okay, fun fact.

02:05:40.500 --> 02:05:43.735
Richard Nixon did two things
that really mattered on this.

02:05:43.780 --> 02:05:47.450
So one is he defined in 1971 something
called Project Independence, which

02:05:47.450 --> 02:05:50.200
was to create 1000 new state of
the art nuclear plants, civilian

02:05:50.200 --> 02:05:51.880
nuclear plants, in the US by 1980.

02:05:52.130 --> 02:05:56.040
And to get the US completely off of
oil and cut the entire US energy grid

02:05:56.040 --> 02:05:58.669
over to nuclear power, electricity,
cut over to electric cars, the whole

02:05:58.680 --> 02:06:00.229
thing, like, detach from carbon.

02:06:01.340 --> 02:06:02.500
You'll notice that didn't happen.

02:06:03.450 --> 02:06:04.160
Why did that not happen?

02:06:04.350 --> 02:06:07.740
Because he also created the EPA and the
Nuclear Regulatory Commission, which

02:06:07.740 --> 02:06:08.860
then prevented that from happening.

02:06:08.889 --> 02:06:09.119
Right.

02:06:09.119 --> 02:06:11.709
And the Nuclear Regulatory Commission
did not authorize a new nuclear

02:06:11.710 --> 02:06:13.070
plant in the US for 40 years.

02:06:13.190 --> 02:06:14.949
Andrew Huberman: Why would he
hamstring himself like that?

02:06:16.219 --> 02:06:21.470
Marc Andreessen: He got distracted
by Watergate in Vietnam.

02:06:21.880 --> 02:06:23.810
Andrew Huberman: I think Ellsberg
just died recently, right?

02:06:24.020 --> 02:06:25.390
The guy who released the Pentagon papers.

02:06:25.390 --> 02:06:27.360
Marc Andreessen: Yeah.
Andrew Huberman: So complicated.

02:06:27.910 --> 02:06:28.690
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, exactly.

02:06:28.690 --> 02:06:29.219
It's this thing.

02:06:30.070 --> 02:06:31.229
He left office shortly thereafter.

02:06:31.230 --> 02:06:33.299
He didn't have time to
fully figure this out.

02:06:33.299 --> 02:06:35.509
I don't know whether he would
have figured it out or know.

02:06:35.510 --> 02:06:36.610
Look, Ford could have figured it out.

02:06:36.610 --> 02:06:37.550
Carter could have figured it out.

02:06:37.560 --> 02:06:38.530
Reagan could have figured it out.

02:06:38.530 --> 02:06:39.750
Any of these guys could
have figured it out.

02:06:39.760 --> 02:06:40.490
It's like the most obvious.

02:06:40.720 --> 02:06:43.150
Knowing what we know today, it's
the most obvious thing in the world.

02:06:43.590 --> 02:06:44.849
The Russia thing is the amazing thing.

02:06:44.849 --> 02:06:47.370
It's like Europe is literally
funding Russia's invasion of Ukraine

02:06:47.380 --> 02:06:49.380
by paying them for oil, right?

02:06:49.400 --> 02:06:52.460
And they can't shut off the oil because
they won't cut over to nuclear, right?

02:06:52.639 --> 02:06:53.749
And then, of course, what happens?

02:06:53.799 --> 02:06:55.759
Okay, so then here's the other
kicker of what happens, right?

02:06:55.760 --> 02:06:59.349
Which is they won't do nuclear, but
they want to do renewables, right?

02:06:59.350 --> 02:07:00.240
Sustainable energy.

02:07:00.240 --> 02:07:02.420
And so what they do is
they do solar and wind.

02:07:02.910 --> 02:07:06.480
Solar and wind are not reliable
because it sometimes gets dark out

02:07:06.490 --> 02:07:08.139
and sometimes the wind doesn't blow.

02:07:08.139 --> 02:07:11.230
And so then what happens is they
fire up the coal plants, right?

02:07:11.230 --> 02:07:14.680
And so the actual consequence of
the precautionary principle for

02:07:14.680 --> 02:07:17.780
the purpose it was invented is
a massive spike in use of coal.

02:07:18.010 --> 02:07:19.780
Andrew Huberman: That's
taking us back over 100 years.

02:07:19.790 --> 02:07:20.190
Marc Andreessen: Yes.

02:07:20.190 --> 02:07:20.719
Correct.

02:07:20.820 --> 02:07:23.129
That is the consequence of
the cautionary principle.

02:07:23.360 --> 02:07:25.040
That's the consequence of that mentality.

02:07:25.660 --> 02:07:28.050
And so it's a failure of a
principle on its own merits

02:07:28.059 --> 02:07:28.990
for the thing it was designed.

02:07:28.990 --> 02:07:32.300
Then, you know, there's a whole
movement of people who want to

02:07:32.300 --> 02:07:33.280
apply it to every new thing.

02:07:33.280 --> 02:07:37.040
And this is the hot topic on AI right
now in Washington, which is like, oh

02:07:37.040 --> 02:07:39.570
my God, these people have to prove that
this can never get used for bad things.

02:07:39.870 --> 02:07:41.679
Andrew Huberman: Sorry, I'm
hung up on this nuclear thing.

02:07:41.900 --> 02:07:45.349
And I wonder, can it just be?

02:07:46.289 --> 02:07:50.420
I mean, there is something
about the naming of things.

02:07:50.420 --> 02:07:56.130
We know this in, I mean, you know,
Lamarckian evolution and things like that.

02:07:56.150 --> 02:07:57.790
These are bad words in biology.

02:07:57.790 --> 02:08:00.570
But we had a guest on this podcast,
Oded Rechavii, who's over in Israel,

02:08:00.570 --> 02:08:02.840
who's shown inherited traits.

02:08:03.219 --> 02:08:07.290
But if you talk about his Lamarckian, then
it has all sorts of negative implications.

02:08:07.290 --> 02:08:11.180
But his discoveries have important
implications for everything from

02:08:11.180 --> 02:08:14.230
inherited trauma to treatment of disease.

02:08:14.230 --> 02:08:18.170
I mean, there's all sorts of positives
that await us if we are able to reframe

02:08:18.170 --> 02:08:21.080
our thinking around something that,
yes, indeed, could be used for evil,

02:08:22.340 --> 02:08:27.230
but that has enormous potential and
that is in agreement with nature, right?

02:08:27.230 --> 02:08:30.980
This fundamental truth that at least
to my knowledge, no one is revising

02:08:30.980 --> 02:08:32.790
in any significant way anytime soon.

02:08:33.070 --> 02:08:34.840
So what if it were called something else?

02:08:35.340 --> 02:08:36.340
It could be nuclear.

02:08:36.340 --> 02:08:39.679
It's called sustainable, right?

02:08:39.679 --> 02:08:42.629
I mean, it's amazing how marketing
can shift our perspective

02:08:42.709 --> 02:08:44.530
of robots, for instance.

02:08:44.530 --> 02:08:47.770
Or anyway, I'm sure you can come
up with better examples than I

02:08:47.770 --> 02:08:54.650
can, but is there a good, solid PR
firm working from the nuclear side?

02:08:55.110 --> 02:08:55.610
Marc Andreessen: Thunbergian.

02:08:57.010 --> 02:08:57.580
Greta Thunberg.

02:08:58.120 --> 02:08:58.570
Andrew Huberman: Thunbergian.

02:08:58.570 --> 02:08:59.070
Marc Andreessen: Thunbergian.

02:09:00.470 --> 02:09:03.440
Like if she was in favor of it,
which by the way, she's not.

02:09:03.470 --> 02:09:04.420
She's dead set against it.

02:09:04.770 --> 02:09:06.020
Andrew Huberman: She said that 100%.

02:09:06.020 --> 02:09:06.520
Marc Andreessen: Yeah.

02:09:06.680 --> 02:09:07.370
Andrew Huberman: Based on.

02:09:07.380 --> 02:09:09.550
Marc Andreessen: Based on
Thunbergian principles.

02:09:09.580 --> 02:09:13.579
The prevailing ethic in environmentalism
for 50 years is that nuclear is evil.

02:09:13.590 --> 02:09:14.520
Like, they won't consider it.

02:09:14.880 --> 02:09:17.156
There are, by the way, certain
environmentalists who disagree with this.

02:09:17.156 --> 02:09:19.570
And so Stuart Brand is the one that's
been the most public, and he has

02:09:19.570 --> 02:09:20.840
impeccable credentials in the space.

02:09:20.840 --> 02:09:22.120
Andrew Huberman: And he
wrote Whole Earth Catalog

02:09:22.490 --> 02:09:22.990
.
 Marc Andreessen: Whole Earth Catalog guy.

02:09:23.050 --> 02:09:23.360
Yeah.

02:09:23.360 --> 02:09:25.530
And he's written a whole bunch
of really interesting book since.

02:09:25.530 --> 02:09:27.630
And he wrote a recent book
that goes through in detail.

02:09:27.639 --> 02:09:29.739
He's like, yes, obviously
the correct environmental

02:09:29.740 --> 02:09:30.950
thing to do is nuclear power.

02:09:32.010 --> 02:09:33.980
And we should be implementing
project independence.

02:09:33.980 --> 02:09:34.909
We should be building a thousand.

02:09:35.459 --> 02:09:37.620
Specifically, he didn't say this,
