02:20:51.700 --> 02:20:55.529
going to allow it in the ways that
their system wants it to happen.

02:20:55.530 --> 02:20:55.700
Right.

02:20:55.700 --> 02:21:00.419
Which is much more for population control
and to implement authoritarianism.

02:21:00.459 --> 02:21:03.210
And then, of course, they are
going to spread their technology

02:21:03.210 --> 02:21:05.790
and their vision of how society
should run across the world.

02:21:06.370 --> 02:21:09.359
So we're back in a Cold War dynamic
like we were with the Soviet Union,

02:21:09.360 --> 02:21:12.070
where there are two different systems
that have fundamentally different views

02:21:12.300 --> 02:21:15.980
on issues, concepts like freedom and
individual choice and freedom of speech.

02:21:15.980 --> 02:21:19.120
And so, you know, we know
where the Chinese stand.

02:21:19.180 --> 02:21:20.480
We're still figuring out where we stand.

02:21:20.720 --> 02:21:25.390
I'm having specifically a lot of
schizophrenic conversations with

02:21:25.400 --> 02:21:28.490
people in DC right now, where if
I talk to them and China doesn't

02:21:28.490 --> 02:21:30.350
come up, they just hate tech.

02:21:30.389 --> 02:21:33.539
They hate American tech companies,
they hate AI, they hate social media,

02:21:33.540 --> 02:21:35.629
they hate this, they hate that, they
hate crypto, they hate everything,

02:21:35.629 --> 02:21:39.320
and they just want to punish and
ban, and they're just very negative.

02:21:39.860 --> 02:21:43.360
But then if we have a conversation a half
hour later and we talk about China, then

02:21:43.370 --> 02:21:44.600
the conversation is totally different.

02:21:44.620 --> 02:21:46.930
Now we need a partnership between
the US government and American

02:21:46.930 --> 02:21:48.119
tech companies to defeat China.

02:21:48.959 --> 02:21:51.210
It's like the exact opposite discussion.

02:21:51.250 --> 02:21:51.400
Right?

02:21:51.400 --> 02:21:54.049
Andrew Huberman: Is that fear or
competitiveness on China specifically

02:21:54.049 --> 02:21:59.950
in terms of the US response in, you
know, you bring up these technologies,

02:22:00.680 --> 02:22:04.000
know, I'll lump CRISPR in there
things like CRISPR, nuclear power, AI.

02:22:04.340 --> 02:22:07.569
It all sounds very cold, very
dystopian to a lot of people.

02:22:07.969 --> 02:22:12.010
And yet there are all these benevolent
uses as we've been talking about.

02:22:12.549 --> 02:22:15.230
And then you say you raise the
issue of China and then it sounds

02:22:15.230 --> 02:22:17.520
like this big dark cloud emerging.

02:22:17.520 --> 02:22:22.000
And then all of a sudden, we need
to galvanize and develop these

02:22:22.000 --> 02:22:23.630
technologies to counter their effort.

02:22:23.840 --> 02:22:27.789
So is it fear of them or is
it competitiveness or both?

02:22:28.150 --> 02:22:31.230
Marc Andreessen: Well, so without them
in the picture, you just have this.

02:22:31.590 --> 02:22:36.190
Basically there's an old Bedouin saying
as me against my brother, me and my

02:22:36.190 --> 02:22:39.700
brother against my cousin, me and my
brother and my cousin against the world.

02:22:40.200 --> 02:22:44.430
It's actually, it's evolution in
action, I think we'd think about it,

02:22:44.430 --> 02:22:48.140
is if there's no external threat, then
the conflict turns inward, and then

02:22:48.140 --> 02:22:51.750
at that point, there's a big fight
between specifically, tech, and then

02:22:51.780 --> 02:22:53.180
I was just say, generally politics.

02:22:53.210 --> 02:22:56.060
And my interpretation of that
fight is it's a fight for status.

02:22:56.080 --> 02:22:59.200
It's fundamentally a fight for status
and for power, which is like, if you're

02:22:59.200 --> 02:23:03.370
in politics, you like the status quo of
how power and status work in our society.

02:23:03.770 --> 02:23:06.339
You don't want these new technologies
to show up and change things,

02:23:06.370 --> 02:23:08.220
because change is bad, right?

02:23:08.230 --> 02:23:09.680
Change threatens your position.

02:23:09.680 --> 02:23:13.240
It threatens the respect that people have
for you and your control over things.

02:23:13.240 --> 02:23:16.439
And so I think it's primarily a status
fight, which we could talk about.

02:23:17.520 --> 02:23:21.109
But the China thing is just like a
straight up geopolitical us versus them.

02:23:21.509 --> 02:23:23.080
Like I said, it's like
a Cold War scenario.

02:23:23.100 --> 02:23:26.610
And look, 20 years ago, the prevailing
view in Washington was, we need

02:23:26.610 --> 02:23:27.800
to be friends with China, right?

02:23:27.800 --> 02:23:29.300
And we're going to be
trading partners with China.

02:23:29.300 --> 02:23:32.319
And yes, they're a totalitarian
dictatorship, but if we trade with them,

02:23:32.320 --> 02:23:33.700
over time, they'll become more democratic.

02:23:34.200 --> 02:23:37.239
In the last five to ten years,
it's become more and more clear

02:23:37.240 --> 02:23:38.380
that that's just not true.

02:23:39.370 --> 02:23:43.230
And now there's a lot of people in both
political parties in DC who very much

02:23:43.230 --> 02:23:46.590
regret that and want to change too much,
more of a sort of a Cold War footing.

02:23:47.180 --> 02:23:50.560
Andrew Huberman: Are you willing to
comment on TikTok and technologies

02:23:50.570 --> 02:23:54.700
that emerge from China that are in
widespread use within the US, like how

02:23:54.700 --> 02:23:56.100
much you trust them or don't trust them?

02:23:56.320 --> 02:24:01.410
I can go on record myself by saying
that early on, when TikTok was released,

02:24:01.620 --> 02:24:05.619
we were told, as Stanford faculty,
that we should not and could not have

02:24:05.660 --> 02:24:07.760
TikTok accounts nor WeChat accounts.

02:24:09.429 --> 02:24:11.840
Marc Andreessen: So to start with,
there are a lot of really bright

02:24:12.000 --> 02:24:14.949
Chinese tech entrepreneurs and engineers
who are trying to do good things.

02:24:14.950 --> 02:24:16.770
I'm totally positive about that.

02:24:16.770 --> 02:24:21.029
So I think many of the people mean
very well, but the Chinese have

02:24:21.030 --> 02:24:25.120
a specific system, and the system
is very clear and unambiguous.

02:24:25.520 --> 02:24:27.519
And the system is, everything
in China is owned by the party.

02:24:28.329 --> 02:24:29.963
It's not even owned by the state.

02:24:29.963 --> 02:24:31.210
It's owned by the party.
It's owned by the Chinese Communist Party.

02:24:31.210 --> 02:24:33.609
So the Chinese Communist Party owns
everything, and they control everything.

02:24:33.920 --> 02:24:35.549
By the way, it's actually
illegal to this day.

02:24:35.549 --> 02:24:38.550
It's illegal for an investor to
buy equity in a Chinese company.

02:24:39.070 --> 02:24:42.450
There's all these basically legal
machinations that people do to try

02:24:42.450 --> 02:24:44.540
to do something that's like the
economic equivalent to that, but it's

02:24:44.540 --> 02:24:45.920
actually still illegal to do that.

02:24:47.280 --> 02:24:49.469
The Chinese Communist Party
has no intention of letting

02:24:49.469 --> 02:24:50.680
foreigners own any of China.

02:24:51.130 --> 02:24:52.549
Like, zero intention of that.

02:24:52.549 --> 02:24:54.919
And they regularly move to make
sure that that doesn't happen.

02:24:56.450 --> 02:24:57.280
So they own everything.

02:24:57.280 --> 02:24:58.000
They control everything.

02:24:58.000 --> 02:25:01.289
Andrew Huberman: Sorry to interrupt
you, but people in China can invest

02:25:01.290 --> 02:25:02.870
in American companies all the time.

02:25:02.880 --> 02:25:05.360
Marc Andreessen: Well, they can,
subject to US government constraints.

02:25:05.389 --> 02:25:09.720
There is a US government system
that attempts to mediate that called

02:25:09.720 --> 02:25:13.270
CFIUS, and there are more and more
limitations being put on that.

02:25:13.300 --> 02:25:16.250
But if you can get through that
approval process, then legally you

02:25:16.250 --> 02:25:19.060
can do that, whereas the same is
not true with respect to China.

02:25:19.670 --> 02:25:23.480
So they just have a system.

02:25:23.480 --> 02:25:27.270
And so if you're the CEO of a
Chinese company, it's not optional.

02:25:27.280 --> 02:25:30.710
If you're the CEO of ByteDance,
CEO of Tencent, your relationship

02:25:30.710 --> 02:25:33.890
with the Chinese Communist Party
is not optional, it's required.

02:25:33.930 --> 02:25:37.400
And what's required is you are a
unit of the party and you and your

02:25:37.400 --> 02:25:38.750
company do what the party says.

02:25:38.750 --> 02:25:42.699
And when the party says we get full access
to all user data in America, you say yes.

02:25:43.160 --> 02:25:46.060
When the party says you change the
algorithm to optimize to a certain

02:25:46.060 --> 02:25:49.890
social result, you say whatever.

02:25:49.900 --> 02:25:53.890
It's whatever Xi Jinping and
his party cadres decide, and

02:25:53.890 --> 02:25:55.190
that's what gets implemented.

02:25:55.660 --> 02:25:58.310
If you're the CEO of a Chinese
tech company, there is a political

02:25:58.310 --> 02:26:01.219
officer assigned to you who
has an office down the hall.

02:26:01.740 --> 02:26:04.789
And at any given time, he can come
down the hall, he can grab you out of

02:26:04.789 --> 02:26:06.749
your staff meeting or board meeting,
and he can take you down the hall

02:26:06.749 --> 02:26:10.180
and he can make you sit for hours and
study Marxism and Xi Jinping thought

02:26:10.180 --> 02:26:14.360
and quiz you on it and test you on it,
and you'd better pass the test, Right?

02:26:14.380 --> 02:26:17.280
So it's like a straight
political control thing.

02:26:17.280 --> 02:26:19.430
And then, by the way, if you
get crossways with them, like...

02:26:22.379 --> 02:26:25.940
Andrew Huberman: So when we see
tech founders getting called up

02:26:25.940 --> 02:26:30.610
to Congress for what looks like
interrogation, but it's probably

02:26:30.610 --> 02:26:33.570
pretty light interrogation compared
to what happens in other countries.

02:26:33.580 --> 02:26:35.730
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, it's state power.

02:26:35.730 --> 02:26:38.660
They just have this view of top down
state power, and they view it's that

02:26:38.660 --> 02:26:41.150
their system, and they view that
it's necessary for lots of historical

02:26:41.150 --> 02:26:43.090
and moral reasons that they've
defined, and that's how they run.

02:26:43.090 --> 02:26:45.179
And then they've got a view that
says how they want to propagate

02:26:45.180 --> 02:26:46.240
that vision outside the country.

02:26:46.700 --> 02:26:50.210
And they have these programs like Belt
and Road that basically are intended to

02:26:50.210 --> 02:26:51.759
propagate kind of their vision worldwide.

02:26:52.900 --> 02:26:55.500
And so they are who they are.

02:26:55.580 --> 02:26:57.270
I will say that they don't lie about it.

02:26:57.679 --> 02:26:58.560
They're very straightforward.

02:26:59.059 --> 02:27:00.239
They give speeches, they write books.

02:27:00.240 --> 02:27:01.480
You can buy Xi Jinping speeches.

02:27:01.480 --> 02:27:02.359
He goes through the whole thing.

02:27:02.359 --> 02:27:04.070
They have their tech 2025 plan.

02:27:04.070 --> 02:27:05.440
This is like ten years ago.

02:27:05.720 --> 02:27:07.160
Their whole AI agenda, it's all in there.

02:27:07.810 --> 02:27:12.040
Andrew Huberman: And is their goal that
in 200 years, 300 years, that China is

02:27:12.040 --> 02:27:14.449
the superpower controlling everything?

02:27:14.790 --> 02:27:15.030
Marc Andreessen: Yeah.

02:27:15.030 --> 02:27:17.160
Or 20 years, 30 years, or
two years, three years.

02:27:17.160 --> 02:27:18.359
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, but
they've got a shorter horizon.

02:27:18.359 --> 02:27:21.589
Marc Andreessen: I don't know.

02:27:21.859 --> 02:27:24.980
Everybody's a little bit like this,
I guess, but, yeah, they want to win.

02:27:25.930 --> 02:27:28.940
Andrew Huberman: Well, the CRISPR in
humans example that I gave earlier

02:27:28.940 --> 02:27:31.874
was interesting to me because, first
of all, I'm a neuroscientist and

02:27:31.889 --> 02:27:36.780
they could have edited any genes,
but they chose to edit the genes

02:27:36.780 --> 02:27:41.759
involved in the attempt to create
super memory babies, which presumably

02:27:41.760 --> 02:27:43.070
would grow into super memory adults.

02:27:45.230 --> 02:27:48.190
And whether or not they
succeeded in that isn't clear.

02:27:48.190 --> 02:27:52.770
Those babies are alive and
presumably by now, walking, talking.

02:27:52.770 --> 02:27:55.560
As far as I know, whether or not
they have super memories isn't clear.

02:27:55.560 --> 02:28:01.950
But China is clearly unafraid
to augment biology in that way.

02:28:03.309 --> 02:28:10.259
And I believe that that's inevitable,
that's going to happen elsewhere, probably

02:28:10.260 --> 02:28:11.619
first for the treatment of disease.

02:28:11.869 --> 02:28:15.640
But at some point, I'm assuming people
are going to augment biology to make

02:28:15.640 --> 02:28:21.340
smarter kids, not always, but often will
select mates based on the traits they

02:28:21.340 --> 02:28:22.880
would like their children to inherit.

02:28:23.400 --> 02:28:27.359
So this happens far more frequently
than could be deemed bad.

02:28:27.809 --> 02:28:30.279
Either that or people are bad,
because people do this all the time,

02:28:30.279 --> 02:28:34.400
selecting mates that have physical and
psychological and cognitive traits that

02:28:34.410 --> 02:28:36.330
you would like your offspring to have.

02:28:36.750 --> 02:28:38.119
CRISPR is a more targeted approach.

02:28:38.120 --> 02:28:41.469
Of course, the reason I'm kind of
giving this example and examples

02:28:41.759 --> 02:28:46.570
like it is that I feel like so much
of the way that governments and

02:28:46.799 --> 02:28:51.380
the public react to technologies
is to just take that first glimpse.

02:28:51.380 --> 02:28:53.140
And it just feels scary.

02:28:53.260 --> 02:28:57.990
You think about the old
Apple ad of the 1984 Ad.

02:28:58.050 --> 02:29:01.079
I mean, there was one very scary
version of the personal computer

02:29:01.080 --> 02:29:04.420
and computers and robots taking
over and everyone like automatons.

02:29:04.500 --> 02:29:07.550
And then there was the Apple version
where it's all about creativity,

02:29:07.550 --> 02:29:11.279
love and peace, and it had the pseudo
psychedelic California thing going for it.

02:29:12.370 --> 02:29:18.460
Again, great marketing seems to convert
people's thinking about technology

02:29:18.670 --> 02:29:24.099
such that what was once viewed as
very scary and dangerous and dystopian

02:29:24.549 --> 02:29:26.570
is like an oasis of opportunity.

02:29:26.930 --> 02:29:29.610
So why are people so
afraid of new technologies?

02:29:30.150 --> 02:29:32.230
Marc Andreessen: So this is the
thing I've tried to understand for a

02:29:32.230 --> 02:29:36.554
long time, because the history is so
clear and the history basically is

02:29:36.554 --> 02:29:39.210
that every new technology is greeted
by what's called a moral panic.

02:29:39.910 --> 02:29:44.160
And so it's basically this hysterical
freak out of some kind that causes people

02:29:44.160 --> 02:29:45.480
to basically predict the end of the world.

02:29:45.480 --> 02:29:49.090
And you go back in time, and actually,
this is a historical sort of effect,

02:29:49.260 --> 02:29:51.609
it happens even in things now where
you just look back and it's ludicrous.

02:29:51.790 --> 02:29:55.660
And so you mentioned earlier
the satanic panic of the concern

02:29:55.660 --> 02:29:56.749
around, like, heavy metal music.

02:29:57.660 --> 02:30:00.050
Before that, there was, like,
a freak out around comic books.

02:30:00.050 --> 02:30:02.900
In the 50s, there was a freak
out around jazz music in the

02:30:02.910 --> 02:30:05.030
20s and 30s, it's devil music.

02:30:05.900 --> 02:30:08.630
There was a freak out, the arrival
of bicycles caused a moral panic

02:30:08.630 --> 02:30:09.970
in the, like, 1860s, 1870s.

02:30:09.970 --> 02:30:10.539
Bicycles?

02:30:10.590 --> 02:30:11.250
Bicycles, yeah.

02:30:11.250 --> 02:30:12.580
So there was this thing at the time.

02:30:12.620 --> 02:30:13.910
So bicycles were the first.

02:30:15.099 --> 02:30:17.419
They were the first very easy to use
personal transportation thing that

02:30:17.420 --> 02:30:22.480
basically let kids travel between
towns quickly without any overhead.

02:30:22.480 --> 02:30:24.196
You have to take care of a horse.

02:30:24.196 --> 02:30:24.870
You just jump on a bike and go.

02:30:25.880 --> 02:30:28.850
And so there was a historical panic,
specifically around at the time,

02:30:28.850 --> 02:30:32.729
young women who for the first time,
were able to venture outside the

02:30:32.759 --> 02:30:35.520
confines of the town to maybe go
have a boyfriend, another town.

02:30:36.510 --> 02:30:39.220
And so the magazines at the time read
all these stories on this phenomenon,

02:30:39.560 --> 02:30:41.130
medical phenomenon, called bicycle face.

02:30:41.900 --> 02:30:45.070
And the idea of bicycle face was
the exertion caused by pedaling

02:30:45.070 --> 02:30:46.439
a bicycle would cause your face.

02:30:46.460 --> 02:30:48.910
Your face would grimace, and then
if you were on the bicycle for too

02:30:48.910 --> 02:30:50.340
long, your face would lock into place.

02:30:53.940 --> 02:30:54.110
Andrew Huberman: [LAUGHS] Sorry.

02:30:54.110 --> 02:30:55.990
Marc Andreessen: And then you would
be unattractive, and therefore, of

02:30:55.990 --> 02:30:57.000
course, unable to then get married.

02:30:59.110 --> 02:31:02.720
Cars, there was a moral
panic around red flag laws.

02:31:02.750 --> 02:31:04.580
There are all these laws
that created the automobile.

02:31:04.590 --> 02:31:05.730
Automobiles freaked people out.

02:31:06.210 --> 02:31:09.020
So there are all these laws in the
early days of the automobile, in a

02:31:09.020 --> 02:31:13.699
lot of places, you would take a ride
in an automobile and automobiles,

02:31:13.700 --> 02:31:14.550
they broke down all the time.

02:31:14.550 --> 02:31:16.530
So only rich people had automobiles.

02:31:16.540 --> 02:31:18.920
It'd be you and your mechanic in the car.

02:31:18.920 --> 02:31:20.130
Right, for when it broke down.

02:31:20.599 --> 02:31:25.090
And then you had to hire another guy to
walk 200 yards in front of the car with a

02:31:25.090 --> 02:31:28.020
red flag, and he had to wave the red flag.

02:31:28.020 --> 02:31:30.639
And so you could only drive as fast as
he could walk because the red flag was

02:31:30.640 --> 02:31:33.010
to warn people that the car was coming.

02:31:34.260 --> 02:31:35.230
I think it was Pennsylvania.

02:31:35.230 --> 02:31:38.480
They had the most draconian version,
which was they were very worried

02:31:38.480 --> 02:31:40.020
about the car scaring the horses.

02:31:40.090 --> 02:31:43.380
And so there was a law that
said if you saw a horse coming,

02:31:43.719 --> 02:31:45.009
you needed to stop the car.

02:31:45.009 --> 02:31:48.199
You had to disassemble the car, and
you had to hide the pieces of the

02:31:48.199 --> 02:31:52.580
car behind the nearest hay bale, wait
for the horse to go by, and then you

02:31:52.580 --> 02:31:53.859
could put your car back together.

02:31:54.760 --> 02:31:56.800
Anyways, an example is electric lighting.

02:31:56.809 --> 02:31:58.425
There was a panic around, like, whether
this is going to become complete ruin.

02:31:58.425 --> 02:32:01.710
This is going to completely
ruin the romance of the dark.

02:32:01.710 --> 02:32:05.119
And it was going to cause a whole new
kind of terrible civilization where

02:32:05.119 --> 02:32:06.169
everything is always brightly lit.

02:32:06.660 --> 02:32:08.460
So there's just all these examples.

02:32:08.460 --> 02:32:10.240
And so it's like, okay,
what on earth is happening?

02:32:10.240 --> 02:32:11.790
That this is always what happens?

02:32:12.420 --> 02:32:15.240
And so I finally found this book
that I think has a good model for it.

02:32:15.960 --> 02:32:18.310
A book is called Men, Machines, and
Modern Times . And it's written by

02:32:18.310 --> 02:32:20.329
this MIT professor, like, 60 years ago.

02:32:20.330 --> 02:32:24.019
So it predates the Internet, but it
uses a lot of historical examples.

02:32:25.059 --> 02:32:28.199
And what he says, basically, is, he says
there's actually a three stage response.

02:32:28.200 --> 02:32:30.480
There's a three stage societal
response to new technologies.

02:32:30.560 --> 02:32:31.510
It's very predictable.

02:32:31.510 --> 02:32:34.280
He said, stage one is
basically just denial.

02:32:34.690 --> 02:32:35.319
Just ignore.

02:32:35.389 --> 02:32:36.899
Like, we just don't pay attention to this.

02:32:36.900 --> 02:32:37.809
Nobody takes it seriously.

02:32:37.920 --> 02:32:39.769
There's just a blackout
on the whole topic.

02:32:40.789 --> 02:32:42.350
He says, that's stage one.

02:32:42.350 --> 02:32:43.850
Stage two is rational counterargument.

02:32:45.210 --> 02:32:47.470
So stage two is where you line
up all the different reasons

02:32:47.470 --> 02:32:48.600
why this can't possibly work.

02:32:48.600 --> 02:32:52.469
It can't possibly ever get cheap,
or this, that it's not fast

02:32:52.469 --> 02:32:53.470
enough, or whatever the thing is.

02:32:54.360 --> 02:32:57.190
And then he says, stage three, he
says, is when the name calling begins.

02:32:57.540 --> 02:33:03.470
So he says, stage three is like when
they fail to ignore it and they've

02:33:03.480 --> 02:33:05.100
failed to argue society out of it.

02:33:05.100 --> 02:33:05.625
Andrew Huberman: I love it.

02:33:05.800 --> 02:33:06.730
Marc Andreessen: They
move to the name calling.

02:33:07.100 --> 02:33:08.109
And what's the name calling?

02:33:08.110 --> 02:33:09.369
The name calling is, this is evil.

02:33:09.410 --> 02:33:10.100
This is moral panic.

02:33:10.130 --> 02:33:10.679
This is evil.

02:33:10.679 --> 02:33:11.260
This is terrible.

02:33:11.260 --> 02:33:11.799
This is awful.

02:33:11.800 --> 02:33:13.080
This is going to destroy everything.

02:33:13.259 --> 02:33:14.090
Don't you understand?

02:33:14.599 --> 02:33:17.249
All this is horrifying.

02:33:17.260 --> 02:33:20.270
And you, the person working on it,
are being reckless and evil and all

02:33:20.430 --> 02:33:21.510
this stuff, and you must be stopped.

02:33:22.150 --> 02:33:24.699
And he said the reason for
that is because, basically,

02:33:24.700 --> 02:33:27.429
fundamentally, what these things
are is they're a war over status.

02:33:28.179 --> 02:33:30.729
It's a war over status, and
therefore a war over power.

02:33:30.730 --> 02:33:32.679
And then, of course, ultimately money.

02:33:32.679 --> 02:33:37.580
But human status is the thing,
because what he says is, what is the

02:33:37.580 --> 02:33:39.490
societal impact of a new technology?

02:33:39.530 --> 02:33:43.070
The societal impact of a new technology
is it reorders status in the society.

02:33:43.510 --> 02:33:47.280
So the people who are specialists in that
technology become high status, and the

02:33:47.280 --> 02:33:50.570
people who are specialists in the previous
way of doing things become low status.

02:33:50.620 --> 02:33:52.340
And generally, people don't adapt.

02:33:53.049 --> 02:33:55.940
Generally, if you're the kind of
person who is high status because

02:33:55.970 --> 02:33:59.680
you're an evolved adaptation to an
existing technology, you're probably

02:33:59.680 --> 02:34:02.779
not the kind of person that's going
to enthusiastically try to replant

02:34:02.780 --> 02:34:04.020
yourself onto a new technology.

02:34:05.120 --> 02:34:07.289
This is like every politician
who's just like in a complete

02:34:07.290 --> 02:34:08.559
state of panic about social media.

02:34:08.559 --> 02:34:10.090
Like, why are they so freaked
out about social media?

02:34:10.090 --> 02:34:13.150
Is, because they all know that the whole
nature of modern politics has changed.

02:34:13.160 --> 02:34:15.820
The entire battery of techniques
that you use to get elected before

02:34:15.820 --> 02:34:17.109
social media are now obsolete.

02:34:17.710 --> 02:34:20.070
Obviously, the best new politicians
of the future are going to be

02:34:20.270 --> 02:34:21.650
100% creations of social media.

02:34:21.940 --> 02:34:22.580
Andrew Huberman: And podcasts.

02:34:22.580 --> 02:34:23.350
Marc Andreessen: And podcasts.

02:34:23.410 --> 02:34:25.360
Andrew Huberman: And we're seeing
this now as we head towards

02:34:25.360 --> 02:34:26.580
the next presidential election.

02:34:26.580 --> 02:34:30.780
That podcasts clearly are going to
be featured very heavily in that next

02:34:30.780 --> 02:34:34.780
election, because long form content
is a whole different landscape.

02:34:37.680 --> 02:34:38.360
Marc Andreessen: Rogan's had, like, what?

02:34:38.510 --> 02:34:41.210
He's had, like Bernie, he's had like
Tulsi, he's had like a whole series.

02:34:41.210 --> 02:34:42.620
Andrew Huberman: Of RFK most recently.

02:34:42.639 --> 02:34:43.999
And that's created a lot of controversy.

02:34:44.090 --> 02:34:44.450
Marc Andreessen: A lot of controversy.

02:34:44.450 --> 02:34:47.459
But also my understanding, I'm
sure he's invited everybody.

02:34:47.459 --> 02:34:47.920
I'm sure he'd love to have Biden on.

02:34:47.920 --> 02:34:50.029
I'm sure he'd love to have Trump on.

02:34:50.030 --> 02:34:51.150
Andrew Huberman: I'm sure
he'd have to ask him.

02:34:51.180 --> 02:34:56.369
I mean, I think every podcaster
