on things, I have to imagine that he

02:35:12.310 --> 02:35:14.110
would be in favor of that sort of thing.

02:35:14.120 --> 02:35:15.220
Marc Andreessen: Yeah.
Or somebody else would, right?

02:35:15.849 --> 02:35:17.740
Some other top podcaster would.

02:35:19.950 --> 02:35:20.320
Exactly.

02:35:20.320 --> 02:35:21.020
I totally agree with you.

02:35:21.020 --> 02:35:23.879
But my point is, if you're a
politician, if you're a legacy

02:35:23.879 --> 02:35:27.550
politician, you have the option
of embracing the new technology.

02:35:27.550 --> 02:35:28.750
You can do it anytime you want.

02:35:28.930 --> 02:35:29.430
Right.

02:35:29.830 --> 02:35:30.850
But you don't.

02:35:31.150 --> 02:35:32.479
They're not, they won't.

02:35:33.010 --> 02:35:33.920
They won't do it.

02:35:33.950 --> 02:35:34.720
And why won't they do it?

02:35:34.750 --> 02:35:36.380
Well, okay, first of all,
they want to ignore it.

02:35:36.590 --> 02:35:37.920
They want to pretend that
things aren't changing.

02:35:38.530 --> 02:35:41.359
Second is they want to have rational
counterarguments for why the

02:35:41.359 --> 02:35:43.465
existing campaign system works the
way that it does, and this and that

02:35:43.465 --> 02:35:44.789
and the existing media networks.

02:35:44.790 --> 02:35:47.620
And here's how you do things, and here's
how you give speeches, and here's the

02:35:47.620 --> 02:35:50.949
clothes you wear and the tie and the thing
and the pocket square, and you've, that.

02:35:51.070 --> 02:35:52.989
It's how you succeeded was
coming up through that system.

02:35:52.990 --> 02:35:55.019
So you've got all your arguments
as to why that won't work anymore.

02:35:55.400 --> 02:35:58.560
And then we've now proceeded
to the name calling phase,

02:35:58.580 --> 02:35:59.860
which is now it's evil, right?

02:35:59.869 --> 02:36:04.650
Now it's evil for somebody to show
up on a stream, God forbid, for three

02:36:04.650 --> 02:36:05.880
hours and actually say what they think.

02:36:06.700 --> 02:36:07.860
It's going to destroy society, right?

02:36:07.860 --> 02:36:11.829
So it's exactly like, it's a
classic example of this pattern.

02:36:11.929 --> 02:36:16.100
Anyway, so Morrison says in the book,
basically, this is the forever pattern.

02:36:16.549 --> 02:36:17.759
This will never change.

02:36:17.789 --> 02:36:20.904
This is one of those things where you
can learn about it and still nothing,

02:36:21.050 --> 02:36:23.370
the entire world could learn about
this, and still nothing changes.

02:36:23.800 --> 02:36:27.159
Because at the end of the day, it's
not the tech that's the question,

02:36:27.170 --> 02:36:28.720
it's the reordering of status.

02:36:31.060 --> 02:36:32.860
Andrew Huberman: I have a lot of
thoughts about the podcast component.

02:36:32.960 --> 02:36:36.640
I'll just say this because I
want to get back to the topic

02:36:36.640 --> 02:36:38.600
of innovation of technology.

02:36:39.530 --> 02:36:44.140
But on a long form podcast,
there's no safe zone.

02:36:45.300 --> 02:36:46.900
The person can get up and walk out.

02:36:47.120 --> 02:36:52.339
But if the person interviewing them, and
certainly Joe is the best of the very

02:36:52.340 --> 02:36:58.190
best, if not the most skilled podcaster
in the entire universe at continuing

02:36:58.190 --> 02:37:02.229
to press people on specific topics when
they're trying to bob and weave and

02:37:03.070 --> 02:37:07.890
wriggle out, he'll just keep either
drilling or alter the question somewhat

02:37:08.250 --> 02:37:12.039
in a way that forces them to finally
come up with an answer of some sort.

02:37:12.070 --> 02:37:16.689
And I think that probably puts
certain people's cortisol levels

02:37:16.800 --> 02:37:20.210
through the roof, such that they
just would never go on there.

02:37:20.340 --> 02:37:22.429
Marc Andreessen: I think there's another
deeper question also, or another question

02:37:22.429 --> 02:37:24.920
along with that, which is how many
people actually have something to say.

02:37:27.690 --> 02:37:28.370
Andrew Huberman: Real substance.

02:37:28.420 --> 02:37:28.850
Marc Andreessen: Right.

02:37:28.850 --> 02:37:31.690
Like how many people can actually talk
in a way that's actually interesting

02:37:31.690 --> 02:37:33.540
to anybody else for any length of time.

02:37:34.320 --> 02:37:35.470
How much substance is there, really?

02:37:35.470 --> 02:37:39.710
And a lot of historical politics was to
be able to manufacture a facade where you

02:37:39.770 --> 02:37:44.240
honestly, as far as you can't tell how
deep the thoughts are, even if they have

02:37:44.240 --> 02:37:46.009
deep thoughts, it's kept away from you.

02:37:46.010 --> 02:37:47.050
They would certainly never cop to it.

02:37:47.920 --> 02:37:49.760
Andrew Huberman: It's going to
be an interesting next, what

02:37:49.760 --> 02:37:52.190
is it, about 20 months or so.

02:37:53.050 --> 02:37:55.390
Marc Andreessen: So panic and the
name calling have already started?

02:37:55.900 --> 02:37:58.600
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, I was going to
say this list of three things, denial,

02:38:00.480 --> 02:38:02.209
the counterargument, and name calling.

02:38:02.210 --> 02:38:06.449
It seems like with AI, it's already
just jumped to numbers two and three.

02:38:06.500 --> 02:38:07.000
Marc Andreessen: Yes, correct.

02:38:07.129 --> 02:38:09.590
Andrew Huberman: We're already at two and
three, and it's kind of leaning three.

02:38:10.640 --> 02:38:10.990
Marc Andreessen: That's correct.

02:38:10.990 --> 02:38:16.240
AI is unusual just because new
technologies that take off, they

02:38:16.240 --> 02:38:17.283
almost always have a prehistory.

02:38:17.490 --> 02:38:20.220
They almost always have a 30 or 40 year
history where people tried and failed to

02:38:20.220 --> 02:38:21.539
get them to work before they took off.

02:38:21.750 --> 02:38:24.119
AI has an 80 year prehistory,
so it has a very long one.

02:38:24.959 --> 02:38:28.439
And then it all of a sudden
started to work dramatically

02:38:28.590 --> 02:38:30.410
well, seemingly overnight.

02:38:31.020 --> 02:38:34.109
And so it went from basically as
far as most people were concerned,

02:38:34.110 --> 02:38:36.776
it went from it doesn't work at all
to it works incredibly well in one

02:38:36.776 --> 02:38:38.409
step, and that almost never happens.

02:38:39.240 --> 02:38:40.619
I actually think that's
exactly what's happening.

02:38:40.619 --> 02:38:43.470
I think it's actually speed running
this progression just because if you

02:38:43.470 --> 02:38:46.960
use Midjourney or you use GPT or any of
these things for five minutes, you're

02:38:46.960 --> 02:38:50.760
just like, wow, obviously this thing is
going to be like, obviously in my life,

02:38:50.760 --> 02:38:51.860
this is going to be the best thing ever.

02:38:51.990 --> 02:38:52.580
This is amazing.

02:38:52.580 --> 02:38:53.859
There's all these ways that I can use it.

02:38:53.880 --> 02:38:56.900
And then therefore, immediately
you're like, oh my God, this is

02:38:56.900 --> 02:38:57.820
going to transform everything.

02:38:57.820 --> 02:39:02.369
Therefore, step three,
straight to the name calling.

02:39:03.980 --> 02:39:05.090
Andrew Huberman: In the face of all this.

02:39:05.400 --> 02:39:07.640
There are innovators out there.

02:39:07.800 --> 02:39:09.600
Maybe they are aware they are innovators.

02:39:10.860 --> 02:39:14.770
Maybe they are already starting
companies, or maybe they are just some

02:39:15.330 --> 02:39:20.900
young or older person who has these
five traits in abundance or doesn't,

02:39:21.100 --> 02:39:24.730
but knows somebody who does and is
partnering with them in some sort of idea.

02:39:26.310 --> 02:39:30.210
And you have an amazing track
record at identifying these people.

02:39:30.969 --> 02:39:34.190
I think in part because you
have those same traits yourself.

02:39:35.320 --> 02:39:39.570
I've heard you say the following:
the world is a very malleable place.

02:39:39.870 --> 02:39:43.460
If you know what you want and you go
for it with maximum energy and drive

02:39:43.460 --> 02:39:47.459
and passion, the world will often
reconfigure itself around you much more

02:39:47.460 --> 02:39:49.330
quickly and easily than you would think.

02:39:49.980 --> 02:39:55.199
That's a remarkable quote because
it says at least two things to me.

02:39:55.439 --> 02:39:59.140
One is that you have a very
clear understanding of the inner

02:39:59.140 --> 02:40:01.620
workings of these great innovators.

02:40:02.309 --> 02:40:05.970
We talked a little bit about that
earlier, these five traits, etc.,

02:40:06.530 --> 02:40:11.300
but that also you have an intense
understanding of the world landscape.

02:40:11.549 --> 02:40:13.660
And the way that we've been talking
a bout it for the last hour or

02:40:13.660 --> 02:40:17.810
so is that it is a really intense
and kind of oppressive landscape.

02:40:17.810 --> 02:40:23.470
You've got countries and organizations
and elites and journalists that are

02:40:23.759 --> 02:40:28.500
trying to, not necessarily trying, but
are suppressing the innovation process.

02:40:28.529 --> 02:40:30.269
I mean, that's sort of the
picture that I'm getting.

02:40:30.270 --> 02:40:33.780
So it's like we're trying to
innovate inside of a vise that's

02:40:33.790 --> 02:40:35.000
getting progressively tighter.

02:40:35.480 --> 02:40:42.569
And yet this quote argues that it is
the person, the boy or girl, man or

02:40:42.570 --> 02:40:44.860
woman, who says, well, you know what?

02:40:45.139 --> 02:40:49.590
That all might be true, but my view
of the world is the way the world's

02:40:49.590 --> 02:40:53.390
going to bend, or I'm going to create
a dent in that vise that allows

02:40:53.390 --> 02:40:54.890
me to exist the way that I want.

02:40:55.120 --> 02:40:58.420
Or you know what, I'm actually going to
uncurl the vise in the other direction.

02:40:58.740 --> 02:41:06.259
And so I'm at once picking up a sort of
pessimistic, glass half empty view of the

02:41:06.270 --> 02:41:08.730
world, as well as a glass half full view.

02:41:09.950 --> 02:41:10.960
So tell me about that.

02:41:12.620 --> 02:41:16.070
Could you tell us about that from the
perspective of someone listening who is

02:41:16.070 --> 02:41:22.060
thinking, I've got an idea, and I know
it's a really good one, because I just

02:41:22.060 --> 02:41:26.370
know I might not have the confidence
of extrinsic reward yet, but I just

02:41:26.370 --> 02:41:27.930
know there's a seed of something.

02:41:28.860 --> 02:41:30.390
What does it take to foster that?

02:41:30.820 --> 02:41:35.690
And how do we foster real innovation in
the landscape that we're talking about?

02:41:36.200 --> 02:41:38.920
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, so part is, I
think, one of the ways to square it is,

02:41:38.920 --> 02:41:42.660
I think you as the innovator need to
be signed up to fight the fight, right?

02:41:43.210 --> 02:41:45.820
And again, this is where the fictional
portrayals of startups, I think, take

02:41:45.820 --> 02:41:48.810
people off course, or even scientists
or whatever, because when there's

02:41:48.810 --> 02:41:52.980
great success stories, they get kind
of prettified after the fact and

02:41:52.980 --> 02:41:56.265
they get made to be cute and fun,
and it's like, yeah, no, if you talk

02:41:56.265 --> 02:42:00.110
to anybody who actually did any of
these, like, these things are always

02:42:00.110 --> 02:42:03.440
just like brutal exercises and just
like sheer willpower and fighting

02:42:03.440 --> 02:42:03.513
forces that are trying to get you.

02:42:03.513 --> 02:42:03.830
So part of it is you have to
be signed up for the fight.

02:42:03.830 --> 02:42:10.530
And this kind of goes
to the conscientiousness

02:42:10.540 --> 02:42:12.680
thing we're talking also.

02:42:12.690 --> 02:42:16.579
My partner, Ben, uses the term courage
a lot, which is some combination of

02:42:16.879 --> 02:42:21.680
just stubbornness, but coupled with a
willingness to take pain and not stop

02:42:21.680 --> 02:42:26.029
and have people think very bad things of
you for a long time until it turns out

02:42:26.040 --> 02:42:28.010
you hopefully prove yourself correct.

02:42:28.880 --> 02:42:29.970
And so you have to be willing to do that.

02:42:31.490 --> 02:42:32.430
It's a contact sport.

02:42:32.830 --> 02:42:34.109
These aren't easy roads, right?

02:42:34.109 --> 02:42:36.769
It's a contact sport, so you have
to be signed up for the fight.

02:42:37.380 --> 02:42:42.480
The advantage that you have as an
innovator is that at the end of the

02:42:42.480 --> 02:42:44.170
day, the truth actually matters.

02:42:44.820 --> 02:42:48.630
And all the arguments in the world, the
classic Victor Hugo quote is, "There's

02:42:48.630 --> 02:42:51.299
nothing more powerful in the world
than an idea whose time has come."

02:42:52.840 --> 02:42:54.450
If it's real, right?

02:42:54.630 --> 02:42:58.460
And this is just pure substance, if
the thing is real, if the idea is

02:42:58.460 --> 02:43:02.890
real, if it's a legitimately good
scientific discovery about how the

02:43:02.920 --> 02:43:06.960
nature works, if it's a new invention,
if it's a new work of art, and if

02:43:06.960 --> 02:43:11.910
it's real, then you do, at the end of
the day, you have that on your side.

02:43:12.340 --> 02:43:15.140
And all of the people who are fighting
you and arguing with you and telling you

02:43:15.150 --> 02:43:16.930
no, they don't have that on their side.

02:43:17.270 --> 02:43:20.500
It's not that they're showing up with
some other thing and they're like,

02:43:20.500 --> 02:43:21.620
my thing is better than your thing.

02:43:21.750 --> 02:43:22.689
That's not the main problem.

02:43:23.400 --> 02:43:25.599
The main problem is I have a thing.

02:43:25.630 --> 02:43:28.399
I'm convinced everybody else is telling
me it's stupid, wrong, it should

02:43:28.410 --> 02:43:29.959
be illegal, whatever the thing is.

02:43:30.000 --> 02:43:32.800
But at the end of the day, I
still have the thing, right?

02:43:33.450 --> 02:43:36.060
So at the end of the day,
the truth really matters.

02:43:36.060 --> 02:43:37.679
The substance really matters if it's real.

02:43:37.679 --> 02:43:38.179
I'll give you an example.

02:43:38.230 --> 02:43:43.600
It's really hard historically to find
an example of a new technology that came

02:43:43.600 --> 02:43:45.430
into the world that was then pulled back.

02:43:47.320 --> 02:43:49.350
Nuclear is maybe an example of that.

02:43:49.350 --> 02:43:52.680
But even still, there are still
nuclear plants, like, running today.

02:43:53.750 --> 02:43:54.770
That still exists.

02:43:55.310 --> 02:43:58.670
I would say the same thing as
scientific, at least I may ask you this.

02:43:58.670 --> 02:44:01.329
I don't know of any scientific
discovery that was made, and then

02:44:01.370 --> 02:44:05.050
people like, I know there are areas
of science that are not politically

02:44:05.050 --> 02:44:09.110
correct to talk about today, but
every scientist knows the truth.

02:44:09.830 --> 02:44:11.140
The truth is still the truth.

02:44:11.140 --> 02:44:14.789
I mean, even the geneticists in the Soviet
Union who were forced to buy in, like,

02:44:14.790 --> 02:44:15.860
knew the whole time that it was wrong.

02:44:16.400 --> 02:44:17.399
That I'm completely convinced of.

02:44:18.120 --> 02:44:20.590
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, they couldn't
delude themselves, especially because

02:44:20.590 --> 02:44:24.320
the basic training that one gets in any
field establishes some core truths upon

02:44:24.320 --> 02:44:26.540
which even the crazy ideas have to rest.

02:44:26.540 --> 02:44:29.970
And if they don't, as you pointed
out, things fall to pieces.

02:44:29.990 --> 02:44:34.660
I would say that even the technologies
that did not pan out and in some cases

02:44:34.660 --> 02:44:42.219
were disastrous, but that were great ideas
at the beginning, are starting to pan out.

02:44:42.219 --> 02:44:45.720
So the example I'll give is that most
people are aware of the Elizabeth Holmes

02:44:45.730 --> 02:44:52.740
Theranos debacle, to put it lightly,
analyzing what's in a single drop of

02:44:52.740 --> 02:44:56.500
blood as a way to analyze hormones
and disease and antibodies, etc.

02:44:56.500 --> 02:45:00.489
I mean, that's a great
idea, it's a terrific idea.

02:45:00.640 --> 02:45:02.890
As opposed to having a phlebotomist
come to your house or you have to

02:45:02.900 --> 02:45:06.140
go in and you get tapped and then
pulling vials and the whole thing.

02:45:07.150 --> 02:45:12.520
There's now a company born out of
Stanford that is doing exactly what she

02:45:12.520 --> 02:45:17.569
sought to do, except that at least the
courts ruled that she fudged the thing,

02:45:17.569 --> 02:45:19.300
and that's why she's in jail right now.

02:45:20.000 --> 02:45:26.160
But the idea of getting a wide array
of markers from a single drop of blood

02:45:26.160 --> 02:45:28.069
is an absolutely spectacular idea.

02:45:28.170 --> 02:45:30.840
The biggest challenge that company
has is going to confront is the idea

02:45:30.840 --> 02:45:32.170
that it's just the next Theranos.

02:45:32.179 --> 02:45:36.109
But if they've got the thing and t
hey're not fudging it, as apparently

02:45:36.129 --> 02:45:42.820
Theranos was, I think everything
will work out ala Victor Hugo.

02:45:42.910 --> 02:45:43.550
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, exactly.

02:45:44.260 --> 02:45:48.280
Because who wants to go back if
they get to the work, if it's real?

02:45:50.180 --> 02:45:50.720
This is the thing.

02:45:50.940 --> 02:45:54.200
The opponents, they're not
bringing their own ideas.

02:45:55.730 --> 02:45:57.660
They're not bringing their, oh,
my idea is better than yours.

02:45:57.800 --> 02:45:58.570
That's not what's happening.

02:45:58.910 --> 02:46:04.070
They're bringing the silence or
counterargument or name calling.

02:46:04.880 --> 02:46:08.930
Andrew Huberman: Well, this is why I
think people who need to be loved probably

02:46:08.940 --> 02:46:11.299
stand a reduced chance of success.

02:46:12.590 --> 02:46:15.380
And maybe that's also why having
people close to you that do

02:46:15.380 --> 02:46:18.910
love you and allowing that to be
sufficient can be very beneficial.

02:46:18.910 --> 02:46:23.990
This gets back to the idea of partnership
and family around innovators, because

02:46:24.170 --> 02:46:29.649
if you feel filled up by those people
local to you in your home, then you don't

02:46:29.660 --> 02:46:33.240
need people on the Internet saying nice
things about you or your ideas, because

02:46:33.300 --> 02:46:35.400
you're good and you can forge forward.

02:46:36.770 --> 02:46:40.279
Another question about innovation is
the teams that you assemble around you,

02:46:40.280 --> 02:46:45.249
and you've talked before about the sort
of small squadron model, sort of David

02:46:45.339 --> 02:46:51.330
and Goliath examples as well, where a
small group of individuals can create a

02:46:51.350 --> 02:46:57.220
technology that frankly outdoes what a
giant like Facebook might be doing or what

02:46:58.140 --> 02:46:59.780
any other large company might be doing.

02:47:00.960 --> 02:47:03.890
There are a lot of theories as to
why that would happen, but I know

02:47:03.890 --> 02:47:05.470
you have some unique theories.

02:47:06.299 --> 02:47:10.920
Why do you think small groups
can defeat large organizations?

02:47:11.310 --> 02:47:13.880
Marc Andreessen: So the conventional
explanation is, I think, correct, and

02:47:13.880 --> 02:47:17.680
it's just that large organizations have
a lot of advantages, but they just have

02:47:17.680 --> 02:47:22.940
a very hard time actually executing
anything because of all the overhead.

02:47:23.020 --> 02:47:26.940
So large organizations have
combinatorial communication overhead.

02:47:27.370 --> 02:47:29.360
The number of people who have to
be consulted, who have to agree

02:47:29.360 --> 02:47:31.170
on things, gets to be staggering.

02:47:31.180 --> 02:47:34.339
The amount of time it takes to schedule
the meeting gets to be staggering.

02:47:34.710 --> 02:47:37.320
You get these really big companies and
they have some issue they're dealing

02:47:37.320 --> 02:47:40.490
with, and it takes like a month to
schedule the pre meeting, to plan for

02:47:40.490 --> 02:47:42.929
the meeting, which is going to happen
two months later, which is then going to

02:47:42.930 --> 02:47:46.300
result in a post meeting, which will then
result in a board presentation, which

02:47:46.300 --> 02:47:48.080
will then result in a planning off site.

02:47:48.880 --> 02:47:50.019
Andrew Huberman: I
thought academia was bad.

02:47:50.020 --> 02:47:51.789
But what you're describing
is giving me hives.

02:47:51.790 --> 02:47:53.650
Marc Andreessen: Kafka was a documentary.

02:47:53.660 --> 02:47:54.160
Yeah.

02:47:57.170 --> 02:48:00.170
Look, you'd have these organizations
at 100,000 people are more like you're

02:48:00.190 --> 02:48:02.550
more of a nation state than a company.

02:48:03.700 --> 02:48:06.035
And you've got all these competing
internal, it's the Bedouin

02:48:06.210 --> 02:48:06.860
thing I was saying before.

02:48:06.860 --> 02:48:09.470
You've got all these internal, at
most big companies, your internal

02:48:09.470 --> 02:48:11.690
enemies are way more dangerous to
you than anybody on the outside.

02:48:12.820 --> 02:48:13.630
Andrew Huberman: Can
you elaborate on that?

02:48:13.630 --> 02:48:14.010
Marc Andreessen: Oh, yeah.

02:48:14.010 --> 02:48:18.520
At a big company, the big competition
is for the next promotion, right?

02:48:18.530 --> 02:48:22.600
And the enemy for the next promotion is
the next executive over in your company.

02:48:23.100 --> 02:48:23.770
That's your enemy.

02:48:24.850 --> 02:48:27.080
The competitor on the outside
is like an abstraction.

02:48:27.110 --> 02:48:28.810
Like, maybe they'll
matter someday, whatever.

02:48:28.810 --> 02:48:31.040
I've got to beat that guy
inside my own company.

02:48:31.179 --> 02:48:31.679
Right?

02:48:31.779 --> 02:48:35.020
And so the internal warfare is at least
as intense as the external warfare.

02:48:35.480 --> 02:48:40.950
This is just all the iron law of all these
big bureaucracies and how they function.

02:48:40.970 --> 02:48:44.380
So if a big bureaucracy ever does anything
productive, I think it's like a miracle.

02:48:44.639 --> 02:48:46.760
It's like a miracle to the point where
there should be like a celebration,

02:48:48.170 --> 02:48:50.829
there should be parties, there should be
like ticker tape parades for big, large

02:48:50.830 --> 02:48:52.050
organizations that actually do things.

02:48:52.590 --> 02:48:54.859
That's great because it's so rare.

02:48:54.930 --> 02:48:57.699
It doesn't happen very often anyway.

02:48:57.700 --> 02:49:00.369
So that's the conventional explanation,
whereas, look, small companies, small

02:49:00.980 --> 02:49:03.879
teams, there's a lot that they can't
do because they're not operating

02:49:03.880 --> 02:49:06.860
at scale and they don't have global
coverage and all these kind of, they

02:49:06.860 --> 02:49:08.180
don't have the resources and so forth.

02:49:08.190 --> 02:49:10.369
But at least they can move quickly, right?

02:49:10.369 --> 02:49:11.740
They can organize fast.

02:49:11.969 --> 02:49:14.710
If there's an issue today,
they can have a meeting today,

02:49:14.710 --> 02:49:15.900
they can solve the issue today.

02:49:16.320 --> 02:49:18.610
And everybody they need to solve
the issue is in the room today.

02:49:19.170 --> 02:49:20.390
So they can just move a lot faster.

02:49:21.200 --> 02:49:21.990
I think that's part of it.

02:49:21.990 --> 02:49:24.530
But I think there's another deeper
thing underneath that, that people

02:49:24.530 --> 02:49:25.700
really don't like to talk about.

02:49:25.750 --> 02:49:28.180
That takes us back full circle to
where we started, which is just the

02:49:28.180 --> 02:49:31.050
sheer number of people in the world
who are capable of doing new things

02:49:31.070 --> 02:49:32.730
is just a very small set of people.

02:49:33.830 --> 02:49:37.779
And so you're not going to have 100 of
them in a company or 1000 or 10,000.

02:49:37.830 --> 02:49:42.410
You're going to have
three, eight or ten, maybe.

02:49:42.410 --> 02:49:44.560
Andrew Huberman: And some of them
are flying too close to the sun.

02:49:44.650 --> 02:49:46.009
Marc Andreessen: Some of them
are blowing themselves up, right?

02:49:46.549 --> 02:49:47.189
Some of them are.

02:49:47.189 --> 02:49:47.729
So IBM.

02:49:47.769 --> 02:49:49.233
I actually first learned this at IBM.

02:49:49.400 --> 02:49:53.400
My first actual job job was at IBM
when IBM was still on top of the world

02:49:53.400 --> 02:49:54.915
right before it caved in the early 90s.

02:49:55.090 --> 02:49:59.400
And so when I was there it was 440,000
employees which, and again if you

02:49:59.400 --> 02:50:02.830
inflation adjust like today for that
same size of business, inflation

02:50:02.830 --> 02:50:05.750
adjusted, market size adjusted, it would
be its equivalent today of like a two

02:50:05.750 --> 02:50:06.910
or three million person organization.

02:50:06.910 --> 02:50:08.480
It was a nation state.

02:50:09.470 --> 02:50:12.119
There were 6000 people in my
division and we were next door to

02:50:12.120 --> 02:50:14.429
another building that had another
6000 people in another division.

02:50:14.429 --> 02:50:16.930
So you could work there for
years and never meet anybody

02:50:16.930 --> 02:50:17.890
who didn't work for IBM.

02:50:18.349 --> 02:50:20.930
The first half of every meeting
was just IBMers introducing

02:50:20.930 --> 02:50:21.759
themselves to each other.

02:50:22.769 --> 02:50:25.180
It was just mind boggling,
the level of complexity.

02:50:25.210 --> 02:50:31.015
But they were so powerful that they had
four years before I got there in 1985,

02:50:31.340 --> 02:50:34.240
they were 80% of the market capitalization
of the entire tech industry.

02:50:35.300 --> 02:50:38.299
So they were at a level of dominance
that even Google or Apple today

02:50:38.299 --> 02:50:40.629
is not even close to at the time.

02:50:40.629 --> 02:50:41.829
So that's how powerful they were.

02:50:41.829 --> 02:50:45.220
And so they had a system and it
worked really well for like 50 years.

02:50:45.220 --> 02:50:46.400
They had a system which was.

02:50:46.720 --> 02:50:50.420
Most of the employees in the company
were expected to basically follow rules.

02:50:50.800 --> 02:50:52.509
So they dressed the same,
they acted the same, they did

02:50:52.510 --> 02:50:53.550
everything out of the playbook.

02:50:53.880 --> 02:50:57.099
They were trained very specifically
but they had this category of

02:50:57.110 --> 02:50:58.520
people they called Wild Ducks.

02:50:59.240 --> 02:51:01.150
And this was an idea that
the founder Thomas Watson

02:51:01.150 --> 02:51:02.340
had come up with, Wild Ducks.

02:51:02.850 --> 02:51:07.160
And the Wild ducks were, they often had
the formal title of an IBM fellow and

02:51:07.330 --> 02:51:11.609
they were the people who could make new
things and there were eight of them.

02:51:12.139 --> 02:51:16.029
And they got to break all the rules
and they got to invent new products.

02:51:16.029 --> 02:51:17.539
They got to go off and
work on something new.

02:51:17.539 --> 02:51:18.599
They didn't have to report back.

02:51:19.020 --> 02:51:21.439
They got to pull people off of
other projects to work with them.

02:51:22.620 --> 02:51:24.250
They got budget when they needed it.

02:51:24.260 --> 02:51:26.970
They reported directly to the CEO,
they got whatever they needed.

02:51:26.970 --> 02:51:27.840
He supported them in doing it.

02:51:27.960 --> 02:51:29.390
And they were glass breakers.

02:51:29.410 --> 02:51:32.849
And the one in Austin at the
time was this guy Andy Heller.

02:51:32.849 --> 02:51:37.800
And he would show up in jeans and cowboy
boots and amongst an ocean of men in

02:51:38.140 --> 02:51:43.160
blue suits, white shirts, red ties and
put his cowboy boots up on the table and

02:51:43.160 --> 02:51:44.649
it was fine for Andy Heller to do that.

02:51:44.750 --> 02:51:46.270
And it was not fine for
you to do that, right.

02:51:46.270 --> 02:51:51.149
And so they very specifically
identified, we have almost like an

02:51:51.150 --> 02:51:55.000
aristocratic class within our company
that gets to play by different rules.

02:51:55.030 --> 02:51:57.290
Now the expectation is
they deliver, right?

02:51:57.760 --> 02:51:59.620
Their job is to invent the
next breakthrough product.

02:51:59.640 --> 02:52:03.609
But we, IBM management, know that
the 6000 person division is not

02:52:03.610 --> 02:52:04.610
going to invent the next product.

02:52:04.950 --> 02:52:05.879
We know it's going to be crazy.

02:52:05.880 --> 02:52:07.769
Andy Heller in his cowboy boots.

02:52:08.370 --> 02:52:09.820
And so I was always very impressed.

02:52:10.320 --> 02:52:14.540
Again, ultimately, IBM had its issues,
but that model worked for 50 years.

02:52:14.710 --> 02:52:15.900
Right?
Like, worked incredibly well.

02:52:15.900 --> 02:52:18.180
And I think that's basically
the model that works.

02:52:19.760 --> 02:52:20.929
But it's a paradox, right?

02:52:20.929 --> 02:52:24.720
Which is like, how do you have a large,
bureaucratic, regimented organization,

02:52:24.720 --> 02:52:28.520
whether it's academia or government
or business or anything, that has all

02:52:28.520 --> 02:52:31.380
these rule followers in it and all these
people who are jealous of their status

02:52:31.400 --> 02:52:36.560
and don't want things to change, but
then still have that spark of creativity?

02:52:37.600 --> 02:52:38.889
I would say mostly it's impossible.

02:52:39.809 --> 02:52:41.189
Mostly it just doesn't happen.

02:52:41.469 --> 02:52:42.800
Those people get driven out.

02:52:42.800 --> 02:52:46.159
And in tech, what happens is those people
get driven out because we will fund them.

02:52:46.789 --> 02:52:47.511
These are the people we fund.

02:52:47.511 --> 02:52:50.650
Andrew Huberman: I was going to say,
rather, that you are in the business

02:52:50.670 --> 02:52:52.609
of finding and funding the wild ducks.

02:52:52.630 --> 02:52:53.090
Marc Andreessen: The wild ducks.

02:52:53.090 --> 02:52:53.710
That's exactly right.

02:52:53.880 --> 02:52:56.290
And actually, this is
actually, close the loop.

02:52:56.290 --> 02:52:58.850
This is actually, I think, the
simplest explanation for why IBM

02:52:58.860 --> 02:53:02.689
ultimately caved in, and then HP
sort of in the 80s also caved in.

02:53:02.689 --> 02:53:05.959
IBM and HP kind of were these
incredible, monolithic, incredible

02:53:05.959 --> 02:53:09.280
companies for 40 or 50 years, and
then they kind of both caved in.

02:53:09.290 --> 02:53:13.010
In the actually, think it was the
emergence of venture capital, it was

02:53:13.010 --> 02:53:16.190
the emergence of a parallel funding
system where the wild ducks, or in

02:53:16.190 --> 02:53:19.430
HP's case, their superstar technical
people, could actually leave and start

02:53:19.430 --> 02:53:22.449
their own companies is, and again, it
goes back to the university discussion

02:53:22.449 --> 02:53:25.229
we're having is like, this is what
doesn't exist at the university level.

02:53:25.750 --> 02:53:27.560
This certainly does not exist
at the government level.

02:53:27.910 --> 02:53:30.689
Andrew Huberman: And until recently in
media, it didn't exist until there's

02:53:30.690 --> 02:53:33.070
this thing that we call podcasts.

02:53:33.139 --> 02:53:33.870
Marc Andreessen: Exactly right.

02:53:34.250 --> 02:53:37.010
Andrew Huberman: Which clearly
have picked up some momentum, and

02:53:37.010 --> 02:53:42.330
I would hope that these other wild
duck models will move quickly.

02:53:42.340 --> 02:53:44.296
Marc Andreessen: Yeah, but the one
thing you know, and you know this, the

02:53:44.410 --> 02:53:46.710
one thing you know is the people on the
other side are going to be mad as hell.

02:53:47.350 --> 02:53:50.800
Andrew Huberman: Yeah, they're going
to, well, I think they're past denial.

02:53:51.590 --> 02:53:53.300
The counterarguments continue.

02:53:53.790 --> 02:53:55.190
The name calling is prolific.

02:53:55.190 --> 02:53:56.189
Marc Andreessen: Name
calling is fully underway.

02:53:58.619 --> 02:54:04.320
Andrew Huberman: Well, Marc, we've covered
a lot of topics, but as with every time

02:54:04.410 --> 02:54:07.240
I talk to you, I learn oh, so very much.

02:54:07.640 --> 02:54:10.840
I'm so grateful for you taking the
time out of your schedule to talk about

02:54:10.860 --> 02:54:13.479
all of these topics in depth with us.

02:54:14.530 --> 02:54:15.590
I'd be remiss if I didn't say that.

02:54:15.590 --> 02:54:22.140
It is clear to me now that you are hyper
realistic about the landscape, but you

02:54:22.140 --> 02:54:27.160
are also intensely optimistic about
the existence of wild ducks and those

02:54:27.290 --> 02:54:29.939
around them that support them and that
are necessary for the implementation

02:54:29.940 --> 02:54:31.380
of their ideas at some point.

02:54:32.090 --> 02:54:35.180
And that also, you have
a real rebel inside you.

02:54:35.299 --> 02:54:38.490
So that is oh, so welcome on this podcast.

02:54:38.490 --> 02:54:42.440
And it's also needed in
these times and every time.

02:54:42.440 --> 02:54:47.150
So on behalf of myself and the rest of
us here at the podcast, and especially

02:54:47.150 --> 02:54:48.939
the listeners, thank you so much.

